\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Chinese motto walks to the biggest monthly fall in more than 25 years\b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Renminbi accumulates a devaluation of more than 3% in the month of August. It's the biggest drop in over 25 years. The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dropped 3.7% against the dollar in August, according to the data provided by Bloomberg. A day from the end of the month, renminbi has been moving towards the biggest monthly fall since 1994, the year the current Exchange system in China was assembled.It is not surprising this evolution: it was this month that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to the minimum of 11 years against the dollar, becoming necessary more than 7 yuan to "buy" a dollar. Until yesterday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  accumulated ten consecutive daily declines, the largest cycle of devaluations since 2015.\par \par \uc2 This happened because the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 ) allowed it, fixing a higher exchange rate and allowing the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float in values dating back to 2008, the period of the global financial crisis.Unlike the euro, the dollar, the pound or the I\u233\'00\'E9ne, the renminbi does not have a flexible exchange rate, but rather a fixed exchange rate, existing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  or public banks to ensure that it does not value or devalue beyond an interval of 2 % for each side.By letting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down, China makes its exports more competitive in dollars as they become cheaper for consumers and businesses, partly compensating for US-imposed tariffs.However, it also makes it harder to pay debts or buy goods or services from other countries (penalizes imports) and may ultimately lead to the flight of foreign capitals and financial stress, as has already happened in the past.The Chinese authorities argue that the fall of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflects the trade dispute with the United States as customs rates will condition the growth of the Chinese economy, weakening the role of its motto in the Global Panorama.However, this fall of the renminbi was poorly received in Washington who promptly attacked Beijing for being manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Shortly thereafter, the U.S. would even qualify China as a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  handler" for the first time in 25 years, opening the door to potential sanctions.For Deutsche Bank's economist, Yi Xiong, China is adopting a long-term strategy. "China does not think that the trade dispute is something that can be really resolved in the short term," he said, quoted by the Financial Times.Capital Economics Economists have estimated that this fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help boost the economy by 0.2%, helping to compensate for the negative impact rated at 0.8% of U.S. tariffs already on the ground or coming.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190830ef8u001v3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
