\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Markets Lower as U.S.-China Trade Tensions Escalate\b0\par\par\uc2 1,300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2019\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA         25628.90  -623.34  -2.37% \par Nasdaq         7751.77  -239.62  -3.00% \par S&P 500       2847.11   -75.84  -2.59% \par FTSE 100      7094.98   -33.20  -0.47% \par Nikkei Stock 20253.04  -457.87  -2.21% \par Hang Seng    25288.83  -890.50  -3.40% \par Kospi         1916.74   -31.56  -1.62% \par SGX Nifty    10885.00    55.50   0.51% \par August contract \par  \par USD/JPY  105.47-48   +0.09% \par Range    105.66   104.46 \par EUR/USD  1.1134-37   -0.08% \par Range    1.1241   1.1136 \par  \par CBOT  Wheat Sept $4.752 per bushel \par Spot Gold      $1,548.20  +1.4% \par Nymex  Crude (NY)  $53.76  -$1.59 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks slumped after China said it would impose retaliatory tariffs on additional U.S. products and President Trump vowed to respond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average dropped more than 600 points, or 2.4%, putting a halt to a relatively quiet week for markets. Yields on \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds also tumbled, as did commodities markets, such as oil and copper, that are sensitive to the two countries' trade battle.\par \par \uc2 The losses accelerated after President Trump fired off a series of Tweets in response to China's plan to impose tariffs on $75 billion more in U.S. goods.\par \par \uc2 "We don't need China and, frankly, would be far better off without them," he said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , while ordering U.S. companies to start looking for alternatives to producing in China.\par \par \uc2 The developments Friday marked an escalation of trade tensions between two of the biggest global economies, stoking waves of selling in the stock market. The S&P 500 dropped 2.6%, giving up its gains for the week, while the Nasdaq Composite declined 3%.\par \par \uc2 "The timing of it is remarkable," said John Brady, managing director at futures brokerage \uc2 R.J. O'Brien & Associates\uc2  , of the trade news. "It puts tariffs front and center on a very important day for markets."\par \par \uc2 The developments overshadowed a highly anticipated speech from Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  in Jackson Hole, Wyo., about the future of interest-rate policy.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the speech, many investors suggested his comments had the potential to spark big moves across bond, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets. Instead, the reaction was muted. Stocks briefly rose after the speech and took a dive following Mr. Trump's comments on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 "It seems as though it's a freaky Friday," said \uc2 Michael Arone\uc2  , chief investment strategist at \uc2 State Street Global Advisors\uc2  . "It looks as though the markets are bracing for the worst."\par \par \uc2 In one sign of the anxiety in markets, a measure of stock volatility via the \uc2 Cboe\uc2  Volatility Index jumped 26% to 21.04 in recent trading after declining this week.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Powell said uncertainty over trade policy was "playing a role in the global slowdown and in weak manufacturing and capital spending in the U.S," adding the Fed would act accordingly to "sustain the expansion."\par \par \uc2 In Friday's tweets, Mr. Powell again drew the ire of President Trump who said "the Fed did NOTHING."\par \par \uc2 Shares of technology companies were some of the biggest hit in the S&P 500, falling 3.1% and under-performing the broader gauge. Retail stocks also took a hit, with \uc2 L Brands\uc2  and \uc2 Hasbro\uc2  each diving more than 8%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian shares opened lower at the start of the week, after U.S. stocks slumped Friday following China's decision to impose retaliatory tariffs on additional U.S. products and President Trump vowing to respond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese stocks were broadly lower in early trade as a fresh escalation of U.S.-China trade dispute pushed down all 33 Topix sectors. Steel, machinery and financial stocks were falling particularly sharply. The 10-year Japanese government bond yield fell 2.5 basis points to -0.265%, the lowest since July 2016. The Nikkei Stock Average was down 2.3% at 20230.71. A fall below 20110.76 would make it the lowest intraday level since Jan. 10. Japanese financial authorities' comments and response were being closely watched.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's benchmark Kospi index was 1.8% lower at 1914.13, tracking Wall Street's declines Friday as the U.S.-China trade conflict escalated when both sides rolled out new tariffs. Most large-cap stocks, including tech ones sensitive to trade, were down in morning trading. KB Securities said that in addition to the trade tensions, investors remain disappointed by the absence of monetary easing signals in Fed Chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jerome Powell\f28 \uc2 \f2  's speech last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was down 3.2% at 25355.34, after both China and the U.S. announced more tariffs on Friday. The moves intensified trade tensions between the world's two largest economies and may trigger renewed selling pressure in the Hong Kong stock market, KGI Securities said, expecting the index to trade between 25300 to 25800 today. Tech suppliers are among the biggest decliners. Property stocks are trading lower too. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Deterioration in risk appetite as a response to U.S. trade policy could produce sharp divergence in the performance of the U.S. dollar, Sean Callow, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. A flight to safety should see the dollar gain versus the AUD, NZD and Asia ex-Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with the Chinese yuan being watched closely, he says. But EUR and JPY could continue to benefit from the market's move to price in more aggressive Fed easing. The Fed headed into Jackson Hole trying to stress positives for the U.S. economy, but the White House seems likely to get the looser monetary policy it desires, albeit due to self-inflicted economic damage, he said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices continue to strengthen in early Asian trade after global uncertainties helped push the precious metal to a fourth consecutive weekly gain. VM Markets says gold "provides the ultimate haven for any dollar \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  threats," amid the escalating U.S.-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trade war and the weakening yuan which may increase the odds of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war. Spot gold was recently up 1.4% at $1,548.20/oz. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices were lower on renewed concerns that escalation of the U.S.-China trade war could lead to a global economic slowdown and thereby affect oil demand. "Oil markets remain in a trade war funk, and with constant trade war overhang, the markets are prone to turn even sourer if there is no definitive evidence of progress on the U.S.-China trade talks," VM Markets said. Front-month WTI futures were recently down 1.8% at $53.20 a barrel, while Brent futures edged 1.4% lower at $58.52 a barrel. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par  \par Trump Aides Say He Isn't Ordering U.S. Companies Out of China \par Trump Says U.S. and Japan Have Reached Trade Deal in Principle \par Tension Between Trump, World Leaders Takes Spotlight at G-7 Summit \par Central Bankers Worry About Trump's Tactics to Reorder Global Trade \par China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Unveils New Mortgage Loan Policy \par China \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Injects CNY150 Bln in Liquidity via Medium-Term Lending Facility \par U.K.'s Johnson Charms Trump, Questions Trade War \par Water Cannons and a Gun Shot: Hong Kong Protest Violence Intensifies \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2   Explored European Banking Alliance \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortescue\f28 \uc2 \f2  Annual Profit Surges, Final Dividend Doubles \par Disney Stores Headed to 25 Target Locations \par Mexico Nears Deal to Resolve Pipeline Conflict \par Netanyahu Reaches for the Russian Vote \par Iran's Foreign Minister Makes Unexpected Visit on Sidelines of \f28 \uc2 \f2 G-7\f28 \uc2 \f2  Meeting \par Hedge Funds Wager China's HNA Will Look After Foreign Investors \par New Details Emerge Over \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mallinckrodt\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Role in Opioid Crisis \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 25, 2019 23:00 ET (03:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190826ef8q0007f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
