\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan extends losses as dollar firms on risk-off mood\b0\par\par\uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2019\par 07:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SHANGHAI, Aug 13 (Reuters) - China's yuan extended losses on\par Tuesday as the dollar gained traction on global risk-off\par sentiment after protests in Hong Kong and a crash in Argentina's\par peso. \par     Deepening unrest in Hong Kong and surprise primary election\par results in Argentina have significantly affected investors'\par appetite for risk.\par     Prior to market opening on Tuesday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of\par China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 lowered its midpoint rate for the ninth\par straight trading session to 7.0326 per dollar. That was 0.16%\par weaker than the previous fix and the weakest since March 25,\par 2008.\par     But the official midpoint continued to be set firmer than\par market expectations, traders said, a situation that has been\par persistently seen since last week after Beijing let its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par weaken past the key 7 per dollar level.\par     The fixing was 95 pips, or 0.14%, firmer than Reuters'\par estimate of 7.0421 per dollar.\par     In the spot market, onshore yuan opened at 7.0599\par per dollar and was changing hands at 7.0638 at midday, 53 pips\par weaker than the previous late session close.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tommy Xie\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of Greater China research at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OCBC Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in\par Singapore, said recent firmer-than-expected fixings suggested\par that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might have used the counter-cyclical\par factor to slow the pace of yuan depreciation.\par     "RMB is likely to be more volatile in the short term to\par absorb the external shock," he said in a note.\par     "As all the counter-cyclical measures are still in place, we\par think \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will not hesitate to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should RMB deviate\par too much from its fundamental level."\par     External risks, domestic economic slowdown and uncertainty\par around the year-long Sino-U.S. trade tensions have all piled\par pressure on the yuan. \par     Several traders said the spot rate has been steadily trading\par lower this week and they see the next support level at 7.1 per\par dollar.\par     A trader at a Chinese bank said the market has turned\par cautious and is waiting to see if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will take any\par action if the next key level is breached.\par     Another trader said sentiment was bearish as many investors\par had recently signed dollar/yuan call options at 7.3 per dollar\par towards the end of this year.\par     Separately, expectations for further monetary policy easing\par are likely to put additional downside pressure on the yuan after\par downbeat July loan growth data, traders and analysts said.\par \par     "The slowing China credit creation in July has signalled a\par higher chance for \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  targeted RRR cuts the rest of this year,"\par said Ken Cheung, senior Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in\par Hong Kong.\par     Yuan borrowing costs edged up offshore on Tuesday morning as\par seen from rises in the CNH Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate\par benchmark (CNH Hibor), set by the city's Treasury Markets\par Association (TMA).\par     The overnight contract rose to 2.96828%, the\par highest since July 22, and was 43 basis points higher than the\par previous fix of 2.53517%.\par     Higher CNH funding costs could discourage yuan short-sellers\par as their borrowing costs rise dramatically, traders said, noting\par that a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bill auction in Hong Kong on Wednesday will\par also lead to funds being tied up.\par     The global dollar index rose to 97.597 at midday from\par the previous close of 97.38. \par     The offshore yuan was trading at 7.0981 per dollar.\par \par     The yuan market at 0453 GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      7.0326   7.0211    -0.16%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          7.0638   7.0585    -0.08%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    0.44%              \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       -2.70%\par  Spot change since 2005                17.17%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \par  Item            Current     Previous  Change\par                                        \par  Thomson         91.49       91.44     0.1\par  Reuters/HKEX                          \par  CNH index                             \par  Dollar index    97.597      97.38     0.2\par    \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    7.0981    -0.48%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              7.1434    -1.55%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore\par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par \par  (Reporting by Winni Zhou and Andrew Galbraith;\par Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-13T06:40:16.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190813ef8d00jit\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
