\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Increased risk of recession\b0\par\par\uc2 972 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Until recently, financial markets had behaved positively. But this year virtually all asset classes have appreciated strongly. In recent weeks - and especially in the last few days - it became increasingly clear that this strong trend is not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 A large part of the rally in financial assets is explained by the expectation of more rate cuts and other monetary stimulus measures. Especially in the United States, the outlook for monetary policy has completely changed since the end of 2018. The cycle of rate hikes came to an end and markets began to anticipate sharp rate cuts. At its meeting in late July, the Fed finally cut the interest rate by 25 basis points by setting the benchmark rate between 2% and 2.25%, the first reduction since the Great Financial Crisis.\par \par \uc2 This, in theory, should be positive for risk assets. The problem is that the market, after the recent rise, not only expected this cut but at least three more cuts in the next 12 months. Some even expected a 50-point cut in July. In contrast, Fed President \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  stated that rate cuts are only "a mid-cycle adjustment" as the economy "remains favorable" even though the trade war creates uncertainties. It also indicated that this reduction was not the beginning of a fee reduction cycle.\par \par \uc2 The reaction of the equity market was slightly negative and the dollar strengthened - although both movements gave no reason to worry too much. It's just an illustration that monetary authorities are going to have a hard time pleasing investors. The problem is that the Fed faces not only drastically changing market expectations, but also growing political pressure.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has criticized the Fed on many occasions, calling for much lower interest rates, undermining the independence of \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some tweets were quite offensive saying the Fed has no idea what it's doing. So, it was not surprising that as soon as the Fed announcement was known, Trump showed his displeasure and sent a tweet saying "as usual, Powell has let us down" and reinforced "We're winning anyway, but I'm certainly not getting much help from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 !"\par \par \uc2 While Trump's reaction was no surprise, it remains very troubling. War rhetoric clearly shows the intent to manipulate and use the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as a weapon to fight China. If this were not enough, just a day after the Fed's announcement, Trump announced his intention to impose a 10% tariff from the first of September on the rest of Chinese imports worth at $300 billion.\par \par \uc2 This threat represents a sharp escalation in the trade conflict. Trump blamed China on tweeting that the Asian giant had agreed to buy U.S. agricultural products in "large quantities," but that China "didn't." But timing the announcement leaves us with doubt stake whether Trump is actually challenging the Fed to fulfill its desire to cut rates more aggressively.\par \par \uc2 China responded by asking state-owned enterprises to boycott U.S. agricultural products and let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, depreciate beyond 7, a new low in 11 years. This in turn led the US administration to declare China officially a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -manipulating country, something not seen since 1994.\par \par \uc2 This accusation is completely absurd and will not generate formal sanctions by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ), if the United States formally presents the case. Inreality, are the market forces and uncertainties surrounding the trade war, initiated by Trump, that weaken the yuan. In fact, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market until 2014, buying dollars and weakening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Over the past five years, however, reserves have begun to decline because China was trying to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prevent stronger depreciation.The escalation in the US-China conflict caused a sharp correction in equities. The S&P 500 fell 8% in three days, looking at the most intraday endpoints. At the same time, investors took refuge in safe bonds and gold. The yield of the 10-year-old American treasury fell to 1.6%, the lowest level since 2016. The yield on the German bond fell to -0.6% and in Switzerland the entire sovereign curve, up to 50 years, is negative. The total amount of bonds with negative yields went to a record $15 billion.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the escalation of the trade war, there was some disappointing economic data, especially in Europe. UK GDP fell 0.2% in the second quarter, the first contraction in almost seven years. June industrial production in Germany (-1.5%) France (-2.3%) disappointed and represents additional evidence of the manufacturing recession. Finally, Italy is on the brink of a new political crisis.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that additional monetary measures will not stimulate economies immediately, but rather, with a delay of 12 to 18 months. Economic news is therefore likely to continue to disappoint in the coming months. The probability of a recession in the U.S. rose to 32% and economic growth is estimated to drop to 1.8 percent in the third quarter. Given the recent commercial escalation, the risk is clearly downward.\par \par \uc2 Stock markets haven't corrected much because there is still a common belief of a "Trump Put." If Trump wants to win re-election in November 2020, he needs a strong economy. And since the slowdown is mostly self-induced, a trade deal is expected to save the economy. While this is not impossible, the risk of a policy error or miscalculation has certainly increased. Therefore, investors are advised to increase portfolio protections. Also, increase the duration through American Treasury bonds to 10 years and accumulating positions in gold, despite the recent rally in these assets.\par \par \uc2 *With the collaboration of Diego Agudelo.\par \par \uc2 Copyright Group of Diarios Am\u233\'00\'E9rica - GDA/La Nacion/Costa Rica\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190811ef8a0008l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
