e turning point" \u8226\'20\'22 RBA SOMP was dovish, but contained no surprises \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD traded as high as 0.6822 before settling around 0.6805/10 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance @ 38.2 of 0.7082/0.6677 @ 0.6832 & break suggest bottom in place \u8226\'20\'22 Support at Thursday's 0.6746 low and break returns pressure to downside GBP treads water in Asia, BoJo election call ignored \u8226\'20\'22 GBP steady in Asia, cable 1.2135-47, GBP/JPY 128.24-71, EUR/GBP 0.9212-23 \u8226\'20\'22 News overnight of BoJo call for elections post-Brexit falls on deaf ears \u8226\'20\'22 GBP bias looks to remain down ahead of likely no-deal Brexit \u8226\'20\'22 Cable recent low 1.2080 8/1, GBP/JPY 128.07 8/6, EUR/GBP 0.9265 high o/n \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/USD stops sub-1.2080, 1.2000, 1.1980 with 1.1983 low in January '17 \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/JPY stops eyed sub-128.00, 126.44 low next key support, low Nov '16 RBA as dovish as market - AUD may go higher anyway The market heard plenty from the RBA today, but none of the messaging was surprising. The lack of a more dovish tone after the RBNZ shocked the market with a 50bps rate cut on Wednesday might result in the AUD/USD appreciating. RBA Governor Lowe was unambiguously dovish, but his comment that the Australian economy may have reached a "gentle turning point" suggests the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not in a mad rush to cut rates again. The RBA SOMP was also decidedly dovish, basing the bank's economic forecasts on market pricing of another rate cut in 2019, followed by one more in 2020 to 0.50%. It also highlighted the near-term risks posed by the escalating trade war between the U.S. and China. But the RBA SOMP hasn't pushed the market into pricing in the possibility of a September rate cut or that the RBA will join other central banks in Asia by easing more aggressively than expected. The AUD/USD is trading with a steady tone above 0.6800 and a break above resistance at 0.6830/35 would warn bears that a bottom is in place. Natural USD demand over Japan's O-Bon holidays? Japan has O-Bon holidays next week, beginning with the national holiday on Monday. This is usually the hottest time of the year in Japan, and many people flee Tokyo and other major cities for either holidays away or back to ancestral homes to pay their respects. Japanese manufacturers more often than not shut down plants, and headquarters are left half-manned, as are financial institutions. What will be the effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets? Japanese players, including retail have already gone long USD into this holiday period, with financials looking to pick up yield on carries, importers to lock in current levels and retail speculators on a feeling USD/JPY has either bottomed or is finding a base. The recent U.S.-China trade war escalation has afforded these players with ample opportunity. Some buy-side orders will remain to 105.50 and 105.00, but these will be accompanied by stops below both levels and especially the latter. Large option expiries at 105.50 and 105.00 Monday may play a part.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-12T04:32:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190809ef8900f1p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
