\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Cautious calm reigns after Washington softens trade war rhetoric\b0\par\par\uc2 920 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2019\par 13:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Washington says open to trade talks\par \par \uc2 * STOXX 600 up 0.7%; MSCI world equity index up 0.2%\par \par \uc2 * Yuan slips despite support from state banks\par \par \uc2 * Gold at six-year highs\par \par \uc2 * German 10-year bond yield falls to record lows\par \par \uc2 * Graphic: World \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in 2019 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2  (Updates prices; adds Kiwi dollar)\par \par \uc2 By Tom Wilson\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 7 (Reuters) - A cautious calm returned to stock markets on Wednesday as softer rhetoric from Washington on the U.S.-China trade war soothed investors, though demand for safe-haven assets like government debt underscored lingering anxiety over recession risks.\par \par \uc2 Europe's STOXX 600 climbed 0.9% as deal-making in the chemicals sector also helped it claw back ground after a bruising three-day sell-off prompted by rising tensions between Washington and Beijing.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's world equity index, which tracks shares in 47 countries, also rose 0.2%. It had suffered its worst day in 18 months on Monday.\par \par \uc2 But gold soared to a six-year high and benchmark government debt from Germany to the United States was in high demand as money still headed towards safe-haven assets.\par \par \uc2 Even as relative calm returns, bond markets in particular have benefited from fears the trade war could spark a global slowdown and bolster the case for looser monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 U.S. shares had gained overnight after President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  downplayed worries of a lengthy trade war and senior adviser \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  said Trump's administration is planning to host a Chinese delegation for talks in September. Wall Street futures gauges also rose.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. administration's remarks marked a shift in tone from recent days, when Beijing warned that Washington's labelling China as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on Monday would have severe consequences for the global financial order.\par \par \uc2 Still, market players voiced caution. Trump's threat to impose additional tariffs on more Chinese products is set to take effect in less than a month.\par \par \uc2 "There is some cautious buying creeping back in," said Michael Hewson, chief market strategist at CMC Markets. "But if you want that to be sustained you have to look towards September 1, when the new tariffs kick in, and whether or not Trump presses ahead with them."\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was slightly lower.\par \par \uc2 Also easing the mood were signs that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to steady the yuan after its recent sharp fall, soothing investor fears of a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  designated China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on Monday after it allowed the yuan to weaken below 7 per dollar for the first time in over a decade. The U.S. move rattled financial markets and dimmed hopes the trade war was ending.\par \par \uc2 Since then, China's state banks have been active in the onshore yuan forwards market, tightening dollar supply and supporting the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sources told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Despite that support, the yuan still dropped 0.2% to 7.0708 in offshore markets, with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets still on edge after the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 set its official reference rate at an 11-year low..\par \par \uc2 "We had a little bit of recovery yesterday, but this morning we are seeing that stalling due to the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  fixing the dollar-yen higher again," said Thu \uc2 Lan Nguyen\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SAFE HAVENS IN DEMAND\par \par \uc2 The skittish mood was underlined by continuing demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodities considered safe havens.\par \par \uc2 Gold touched a six-year high of $1,489.76 per ounce. The Japanese yen rose 0.2% to 106.26, although that was still some way from levels seen on Monday when the trade war's escalation panicked investors.\par \par \uc2 The rush to the yen was also fuelled by a 2% slump in the New Zealand dollar after its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made an aggressive interest rate cut and said negative rates were possible, promoting bets on further policy easing around the world..\par \par \uc2 Central banks across the world, looking to rev up growth and fight low inflation rates, have turned increasingly dovish in recent months.\par \par \uc2 But the extent of the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2 's move caught markets off-guard, sending the Kiwi \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its lowest level since early 2016 and dragging the Australian dollar down 0.4% to $0.6378.\par \par \uc2 U.S. bonds stood tall, retaining much of their gains made in the past week. Ten-year Treasury notes yielded 1.66% percent , their lowest since 2016, as investors bet on another rate cut by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  in September.\par \par \uc2 Germany's 10-year bond yield fell to record lows deep in negative territory as the bigger-than-expected Kiwi interest rate cut and weak German economic data fuelled further a rally in bond markets.\par \par \uc2 German industrial output fell more than expected in June, adding to signs that Europe's biggest economy contracted in the second quarter as its exporters were caught up in trade disputes.\par \par \uc2 In commodity markets, oil prices slipped to near seven-month lows, with the potential for damage to the global economy and to dampen demand from the Sino-U.S. trade dispute casting a shadow over the market.\par \par \uc2 International benchmark Brent crude futures were at $58.65 a barrel by 1107 GMT, down 19 cents, or 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 For Reuters Live Markets blog on European and UK stock markets, please click on:\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tom Wilson; Editing by Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-7T12:15:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190807ef87021ud\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
