\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China's July  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves fall to $3.104 trln amid rising trade tensions\b0\par\par\uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2019\par 11:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fall $15.54 bln in July, less than expected\par \par \uc2 * Fall in reserves due to changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , assets-regulator\par \par \uc2 * Sharp recent slide in yuan could spur more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * U.S. brands \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Aug 7 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fall by $15.54 billion in July to $3.104 trillion, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Wednesday, as the yuan came under pressure amid rising trade tensions with the United States.\par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters had expected the country's reserves, the world's largest, would fall by $18 billion to $3.101 trillion.\par \par \uc2 The fall in July - which was only the second monthly drop this year - was due to changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and prices of assets which China holds in its reserves, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said in a statement after the data release.\par \par \uc2 China has been able to keep capital outflows under control over the past year despite the bruising trade war with the United States and weakening economic growth at home. Reserves have rebounded from an October 2018 low thanks to capital controls and rising foreign investments in Chinese stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 But downward pressure on the yuan is building after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slide through the key support level of 7 to the dollar on Monday and it tumbled to 11-year lows.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  moved subsequently to label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has now weakened around 1.5% since U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  threatened last Thursday to impose more tariffs on Chinese goods from Sept. 1, though there are signs China is trying to stem the declines.\par \par \uc2 The yuan fell 0.28% against the dollar in July, despite an agreement by U.S. and Chinese leaders in late June to hold off on further punitive trade measures while the two sides attempted to restart negotiations, which had broken down in May.\par \par \uc2 China burned through $1 trillion of reserves supporting the yuan in the last economic downturn in 2015, which also saw it devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a surprise move.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan earlier this year as trade talks continued, "but the fact that they have allowed the yuan to go past 7 shows they have sort of given up," Capital Economics senior China economist Julian Evans-Pritchard said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "We want to see how long the yuan stays past 7 to see if this is just a warning shot. If it stays past 7, it could weaken significantly further."\par \par \uc2 Adding to the pressure, China's economic growth slowed to 6.2% in the second quarter, its weakest pace in at least 27 years, as demand at home and abroad faltered.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect July data releases over the coming week to show a further loss of momentum, highlighting the need for Beijing to announce more growth boosting measures.\par \par \uc2 DIVERSIFICATION\par \par \uc2 China will pursue the diversification of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in a steady, prudent way, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said in late July.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, it released some broad data on its reserve holdings, which have been a closely held secret.\par \par \uc2 Of China's reserves in 2014, 58% were held in U.S. dollar-denominated assets, down from 79% in 1995, the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) said.\par \par \uc2 It did not disclose information for years after 2014.\par \par \uc2 China has been ramping up its gold reserves this year.\par \par \uc2 It held 62.26 million fine troy ounces of gold at end-July, up 4.5% from 59.560 million ounces at end-2018.\par \par \uc2 The value of China's gold reserves rose to $88.876 billion at the end of July from $87.27 billion at the end of June.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Beijing Monitoring Desk and Kevin Yao; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-7T10:41:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190807ef8701m6h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
