\par\par \b \uc2 Heard on the Street: Stymied on Trade, China's New Play Is to Wait Out Trump -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nathaniel Taplin \par \uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2019\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B14\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (August 7, 2019).\par \par \uc2 China's leaders appear to have concluded that they won't secure an acceptable trade deal with President Trump. What they can do is try to weather the next 18 months while inflicting maximum political damage -- and hope a weakening U.S. economy delivers a new president.\par \par \uc2 Letting the yuan weaken over 1%, passing the key level of seven to the dollar, was clearly meant to provoke. Shortly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crossed the line Monday morning, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released a statement pegging the weakness to "protectionist measures and expectations of increased tariffs on China." Beijing also confirmed early Tuesday that Chinese companies had ceased new purchases of U.S. agricultural goods. The U.S. has responded by formally labeling China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 Why the sudden hawkishness from Beijing?\par \par \uc2 One reason may be that China's leaders, like many investors, have concluded that Mr. Trump is more interested in strong-arming U.S. rates lower than making a deal on trade -- and may even see trade tensions as a way to squeeze the Fed.\par \par \uc2 Beijing would love both a trade deal and lower U.S. rates. But lower rates alone would be a huge help: China could keep easing its own monetary policy and allow moderate yuan depreciation -- both needed to shore up growth -- with a smaller risk of big capital outflows. To the extent that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, it has largely been to slow depreciation and prevent outflows.\par \par \uc2 Being able to blame a big yuan drop on Mr. Trump -- rather than, for instance, on poor-quality assets at home -- is actually rather helpful. So far, the market reaction appears to validate this strategy. The yuan sold off sharply Monday but strengthened Tuesday. And forward markets have remained relatively stable. One reason: Unlike in 2015, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been saying for weeks that seven was no hard and fast line.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, to the extent that Mr. Trump is determined to keep pushing tariffs higher regardless of what Beijing does, trade tensions have the advantage of damaging the U.S. economy and the president too. The U.S. manufacturing purchasing managers index for July was the weakest since late 2016, with new export orders leading the way lower. China's economy is still shaky, but June and July data contained hints that the pace of the slowdown may be easing.\par \par \uc2 All of this gives Beijing a strong incentive to wait and see. If it must endure tariffs, then the obvious strategy is to take advantage of falling U.S. rates and allow the yuan to take some -- but not too much -- of the pressure.\par \par \uc2 Write to Nathaniel Taplin at nathaniel.taplin@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications The U.S. designated China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator after Beijing's announcement on agricultural purchases. An earlier version of this article incorrectly stated the designation came before the announcement. (Aug. 6, 2019)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 07, 2019 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190807ef87000ln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
