\par\par \b \uc2 Why the U.S. Labeled China a  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Manipulator and What It Means\b0\par\par\uc2 By Josh Zumbrun \par \uc2 1,053 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2019\par 04:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- After a decade of flirting with the issue, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  on Monday labeled China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator, something the U.S. hadn't done since 1994.\par \par \uc2 Here is a look at what the action means, and why the Treasury Department acted now.\par \par \uc2 What is \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation?\par \par \uc2 Countries can weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and purchasing U.S. dollars. This makes dollars more expensive and the other country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker. Once the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is weaker, goods from that country become cheaper on international markets, potentially providing a substantial boost to exports.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  monitors for this behavior to determine if countries are trying to gain "an unfair competitive advantage in international trade."\par \par \uc2 Weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this way is considered artificial because it is the result of government policy rather than the natural market forces of international trade and capital flows.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, China has been the center of concerns. Policy makers from both parties have long worried that China artificially weakened its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make its exports cheaper on the world market, boosting its manufacturing sector.\par \par \uc2 Why did the U.S. act now?\par \par \uc2 Beijing can tightly control the exchange rate. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  has vast holdings of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it can use for purchases or sales during \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and it can also direct \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's state-owned banks to take steps to guide the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Over the past decade, China has sometimes controlled the rate tightly (for nearly two years from mid-2008 to mid-2010, it kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged at almost exactly 6.8 yuan per dollar). Since then, it has allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drift around, but didn't let the exchange rate surpass 7 yuan per dollar. In late 2016 and again in late 2018, the exchange rate got close to 7 but didn't break through.\par \par \uc2 That changed on Monday, as trade relations between the U.S. and China took another step backward. After a round of negotiations in Shanghai last week, the U.S. failed to secure specific farm purchases, and responded by promising a new round of 10% tariffs on nearly $300 billion in goods. China said it was halting purchases of U.S. agriculture products.\par \par \uc2 China's yuan dropped to a record offshore low of 7.1087 to the dollar in Hong Kong trading.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said the yuan broke through the threshold because of market forces. Indeed, hitting a country with large tariffs typically has the effect of weakening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But because Beijing can tightly control its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it means when such a move happens that they at least passively allowed it, even if they didn't actively engineer it.\par \par \uc2 Why hadn't the U.S. done it in the past?\par \par \uc2 The U.S. labeled China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator from 1992 to 1994, and Japan and Taiwan are the other countries that were hit with the designation in the late 1980s.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department studies the issue in depth in a semiannual report. Under five such reports under President Trump's own Treasury, they concluded that China didn't meet formal criteria for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation.\par \par \uc2 In a textbook example of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, a country would have a giant overall trade surplus because its artificially low \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would make its exports far exceed its imports.\par \par \uc2 About a decade ago, China had a giant surplus, and many believed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator designation was warranted. \uc2 Henry Paulson\uc2 , the Treasury secretary under President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  at the time, was a firm believer in the value of ongoing dialogue with China. After the financial crisis, although its surplus with the U.S. remained large, China's overall surplus continually shrunk.\par \par \uc2 When President Obama and Mr. Trump's Treasury Departments studied the issue, they concluded that China no longer met the long-established definitions of manipulation (some of which are outlined in the two laws that govern \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation: the 1988 Omnibus Trade and Competitiveness Act and the 2015 Trade Facilitation and Trade Enforcement Act.)\par \par \uc2 Under Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2 , the Trump administration published five semiannual reports which concluded that China didn't meet the definition of manipulation because of its now small current-account surplus, and because it wasn't actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market very much.\par \par \uc2 What Happens Now?\par \par \uc2 The move is considered largely symbolic.\par \par \uc2 The ultimate reason for designating a country a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator is to justify some sort of retaliation. But the Trump administration has already shown both its ability and willingness to use other authorities to impose sweeping penalties against China for policies the president says treat the U.S. unfairly. The designation would carry more oomph against a country that's not already in the middle of a trade war with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The office of the U.S. Trade Representative has already established its legal authority, through a report on China's intellectual-property practices, to impose tariffs on any and every thing coming from China. If the USTR moves forward with its newest tariff threat -- tariffs on $300 billion of goods beginning Sept. 1 -- then nearly every item from China will be subject to tariffs.\par \par \uc2 In theory, the U.S. could try to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on its own to offset \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakening exchange rate. But in the U.S., it is the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , rather than the Treasury, that has the authority to do this on a large scale. The Fed has typically been reluctant to do this, and some fear that layering U.S. manipulation atop foreign manipulation is unlikely to lead to a more natural market.\par \par \uc2 As for negotiations, the USTR and Treasury are already engaged in negotiating a deal (which Mr. Mnuchin said was poised to include a strong chapter on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, before the talks broke down).\par \par \uc2 White House economic adviser Lawrence Kudlow said on CNBC Tuesday that, even after the designation, the U.S. would still like to negotiate a deal, and it is still planning on a Chinese trade delegation visiting the U.S. in September.\par \par \uc2 Write to Josh Zumbrun at Josh.Zumbrun@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 07-08-19 0250GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020190807ef870003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
