c2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be the latest weapon in the trade war.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's overnight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Department\f28 \uc2 \f2  labeled\par Beijing as a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator as it let the yuan slide to a more than decade low on\par Monday.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - European shares finished lower on Tuesday weighed down by trade worries as\par support from upbeat German data and China stepping in to stabilise its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  proved\par to be temporary.\par     After trading higher for most of the session and gaining up to 0.7%, the\par pan-European stocks benchmark STOXX 600 index closed 0.5% lower, extending a\par trade driven rout to a third session.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japanese shares plummeted on Tuesday to their lowest since early January,\par spooked by the spectre of a full-blown economic war between the United States and China\par after Washington designated China a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator.\par     The Nikkei share average fell 0.65% to 20,585.31, after diving 2.94% at one\par point and hitting its lowest since Jan. 10. The broader Topix lost 0.44% to\par 1,499.23.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - China's tumbling yuan steadied on Tuesday as authorities took steps to\par contain its slide, while stocks tanked after Washington labelled Beijing a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par manipulator in a sharp escalation of Sino-U.S. trade tensions.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has slumped as much as 2.7% over the past three days, breaking through\par the symbolic 7-per-dollar level, pounding stocks and pushing bonds higher as investors\par feared the yuan's value has become a new front in the trade war.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - Australian shares are set to open higher on Wednesday, tracking a\par recovery on Wall Street after a sharp sell-off in the previous session, as China's\par efforts to stabilize the yuan alleviated fears of a possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war between the\par world's two largest economies. \par     The local share price index futures rose 0.8% to 6438 by 2030 GMT, a\par 40.1-point discount to the underlying S&P/ASX 200 index close. The benchmark\par ended down 2.4% on Tuesday.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - South Korean shares fell more than 1.5% on Tuesday, marking the fifth\par consecutive session of losses, after Washington labelled Beijing as a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par manipulator. The Korean won held steady, while the benchmark bond yield rose.\par     The Seoul stock market's main KOSPI ended down 29.54 points or 1.52% at\par 1,917.44, while the junior KOSDAQ lost more than 3%.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par     NEW YORK - The Chinese yuan strengthened and demand for safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like\par the Japanese yen and Swiss franc eased on Tuesday, as China appeared to take steps to\par stabilize its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which breached a key level on Monday and sparked broad risk\par aversion.\par     The dollar rebounded after falling the previous session, benefiting from relative\par calm in the market.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     CHINA - China's tumbling yuan steadied on Tuesday as authorities took steps to\par contain its slide, while stocks tanked after Washington labelled Beijing a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par manipulator in a sharp escalation of Sino-U.S. trade tensions.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has slumped as much as 2.7% over the past three days, breaking through\par the symbolic 7-per-dollar level, pounding stocks and pushing bonds higher as investors\par feared the yuan's value has become a new front in the trade war.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - The Australian dollar snapped a 12-day losing streak on Tuesday after\par the country posted its biggest trade surplus ever and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  paused policy\par easing after two back-to-back cuts.\par     The Australian dollar was 0.6% higher at $0.6798, after hitting a\par seven-month trough of $0.6748 on Monday.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL -  The won snapped four straight sessions of declines, with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers\par citing likely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by authorities to curb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakness.\par     The won was quoted at 1,215.3 per dollar on the onshore settlement platform\par , steady from its previous close at 1,215.3.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TREASURIES\par     \par     NEW YORK - The spread between U.S. shorter- and longer-dated Treasury yields\par contracted on Tuesday, as shorter-dated yields rose on $38 billion in three-year debt\par supply and nagging worries about U.S.-China trade tensions pushed down longer-dated\par yields.\par     The modest bounce among most U.S. yields followed four straight sessions of torrid\par declines as investors rushed into \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt to shield their money from\par stocks and other risky assets in response to rising trade friction between China and\par the United States and bets on more stimulus from global central banks to combat slowing\par business activity.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - Euro zone government bond yields fell to fresh record lows on Tuesday, due\par to rising concern about a global trade war after the United States branded China a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator.\par     German, Dutch and Austrian 10-year bond yields\par all hit fresh record lows. Other euro zone bond yields, down 2-3 basis points on the\par day, kept recent lows in sight, ES10YT=RR.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japanese government bond yields rose at the long end of the curve on\par Tuesday after China took steps to curb declines in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which reduced demand\par for safe-haven assets.\par     Benchmark 10-year JGB futures were little changed, up 0.03 point to\par 154.24, with a trading volume of 28,059 lots.\par     For a full report, click on\par  \par     \par     COMMODITIES\par     \par     GOLD\par \par     Gold prices strengthened on Tuesday, consolidating near the highest in more than\par six years as an intensifying U.S.-China trade war threatened global economic growth.\par     Spot gold rose 0.5% to $1,470.96 an ounce as of 2:08 p.m. EDT (1808 GMT),\par after hitting $1,474.81, the highest since May 2013. The previous session, gold jumped\par as much as 2%.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     IRON ORE\par     \par      Dalian and Singapore iron ore futures extended their losses on Tuesday, while spot\par prices slipped into bear market territory, on downbeat demand outlook for the\par steelmaking ingredient in China and signs that the global supply crunch is easing.\par     The most-traded iron ore contract on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dalian Commodity Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 , with\par January 2020 expiry, slumped as much as 2.8% to 689 yuan ($97.98) a tonne, its weakest\par intraday level since July 5. It ended down 2.2% at 693.50 yuan.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     BASE METALS\par     \par     Copper prices hovered around a two-year low on Tuesday as further escalation in the\par U.S.-China trade conflict delayed resolution to a dispute that has depressed economic\par growth and increased fears over metals demand.\par     Three-month copper on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Metal Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) ended mostly\par unchanged at $5,685 a tonne, failing to achieve any real distance from the $5,640\par two-year low touched in the previous session.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     OIL\par     \par     Oil prices fell slightly on Tuesday, with Brent crude remaining near seven-month\par lows just below $60 a barrel because of increasing trade tensions between China and the\par United States.\par     International benchmark Brent futures fell 58 cents to $59.23 a barrel by\par 2:25 p.m. EDT [1825 GMT], having dipped earlier in the session to their lowest since\par Jan. 14 at $59.07.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     PALM OIL\par     \par     Malaysian palm oil futures reversed early losses to close higher on Tuesday,\par hitting a fresh two-month high on the back of a weaker ringgit and expectations of\par lower August production.\par     The benchmark palm oil contract for October delivery on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bursa Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par Derivatives Exchange was up 0.4% at 2,102 ringgit ($502) per tonne at the close of\par trade, after briefly hitting 2,108 ringgit, its strongest level since May 29.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     RUBBER\par     \par     Tokyo Commodity Exchange (TOCOM) futures fell for the eight consecutive session on\par Tuesday, even as Shanghai rubber rose on technical rebound\par     The benchmark TOCOM rubber contract for January delivery,\par finished 0.3 yen ($0.0028) lower at 165.9 yen per kg\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par \par  (Bengaluru Bureau; +91 80 6749 1130)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-6T21:31:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190806ef860422d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
