\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Plummett on Trade Row\b0\par\par\uc2 2,898 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2019\par 21:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GLOBAL STOCK INDEXES: \par               LATEST   CHANGE  % CHG \par DJ Indus     25674.95 -810.06 -3.06% \par Nasdaq         7717.64 -286.43 -3.58% \par NYSE  Comp    12411.65 -427.86 -3.33% \par S&P 500       2841.80  -90.25 -3.08% \par Russell 2000  1472.66  -61.00 -3.98% \par TSM          29170.24 -934.93 -3.11% \par S&P/TSX      16271.66 -105.38 -0.64% \par FTSE 100      7223.85 -183.21 -2.47% \par FTSE 250     18871.42 -381.75 -1.98% \par Xetra DAX    11658.51 -213.93 -1.80% \par CAC40         5241.55 -117.45 -2.19% \par Nikkei Stock 20720.29 -366.87 -1.74% \par Hang Seng    26151.32 -767.26 -2.85% \par S&P/ASX 200   6640.30 -128.30 -1.90% \par Global Stock Indexes at 1925 GMT. \par  \par INTERBANK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RATES: \par            Latest  Previous % Chg \par                       Close \par USD/JPY 106.04-05 106.58-59 -0.50 \par EUR/USD 1.1203-06 1.1107-10 +0.86 \par GBP/USD 1.2140-42 1.2161-63 -0.17 \par USD/CHF 0.9735-39 0.9819-23 -0.86 \par USD/CAD 1.3207-12 1.3202-07 +0.04 \par AUD/USD 0.6762-66 0.6800-04 -0.56 \par NZD/USD 0.6535-41 0.6533-39 +0.03 \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates at 1850 GMT. \par  \par TREASURYS: \par               PRICE CHG  YIELD% \par 10-Year Note    31/32    1.747 \par 5-Year Note      5/32    1.557 \par 2-Year Note      2/32    1.605 \par  \par US STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks were pummeled by selling, pushing indexes from New York to Shanghai lower, as the yuan reeled and fresh trade threats between Beijing and Washington raised fears of an economic slowdown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 856 points, or 3.2%, to 25628. The S&P 500 shed 3.3% and the Nasdaq Composite declined 3.8%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks elsewhere retreated, with benchmark indexes in Europe, Japan and Hong Kong falling at least 1% apiece.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest wave of selling started, investors and analysts said, after reports showed Chinese and U.S. officials ratcheting up pressure on each other in their prolonged trade fight. The Chinese yuan sank below 7 per dollar and hit an all-time low in offshore trading Monday, with local officials blaming the depreciation on President Trump's decision last week to extend tariffs to almost all Chinese imports. Mr. Trump responded on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  , accusing China of engaging in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The back-and-forth dealt a blow to some investors' hopes that the two countries would ultimately reach a trade agreement. Worries about the conflict have weighed on global growth this year. The trade fight has also had an impact on the U.S., where growth and consumer spending remain strong: The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  last week cut interest rates for the first time since 2008, in part to cushion the economy against what it sees as rising financial risks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Global stocks had been priced for perfection," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kenny Polcari\f28 \uc2 \f2 , managing principal at Butcher Joseph Asset Management. "Now people are realizing we may not get a deal."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, even with Monday's pullback, stocks remain close to all-time highs. The S&P 500 is up 13%, about 6% off its July 26 record and outperforming the Stoxx Europe 600, Nikkei Stock Average, Shanghai Composite and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index for the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But fears about the trade fight intensifying have nevertheless cast uncertainty on the market's trajectory. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  broke through the psychologically important level of 7 yuan to the dollar, prompting President Trump to accuse Beijing of manipulating its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a way that would backfire.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The depreciation sent the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to a record low for offshore trading, and came days after Mr. Trump threatened to broaden U.S. tariffs to cover essentially all Chinese imports. He and many other U.S. officials have long accused China of weakening the yuan to make its exports cheaper and gain an unfair advantage in trade. Beijing has denied doing so.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even so, \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suggested that the depreciation was in response to Mr. Trump's decision last week to extend punitive tariffs to almost all Chinese goods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's slump was "due to the effects of unilateralist and trade-protectionist measures and the expectations for tariffs against China," the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a tweet about 12 hours after the yuan crossed the 7 threshold, Mr. Trump described the move as "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation." He said: "China dropped the price of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to an almost a historic low. It's called "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation." Are you listening Federal Reserve ? This is a major violation which will greatly weaken China over time!"\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan slid as much as 1.9% to a record offshore low of 7.1087 to the dollar in Hong Kong on Monday, according to data from Refinitiv. That put it on course for the biggest single-day loss since August 2015, when Beijing allowed a sudden depreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . China has let the yuan trade offshore, in locations such as Hong Kong, since 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In mainland China, the yuan also weakened beyond the 7 level-which policy makers in recent years have defended at various points-for the first time since 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "has effectively weaponized the exchange rate, even if it is not proactively weakening the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " through direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Julian Evans-Pritchard, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2 , wrote in a note to clients. His company now forecasts the yuan will end the year at 7.30 to the dollar, and stand at 7.5 a year later.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weiqi Zhu, a fund manager at Gao Zheng Asset Management in Hong Kong, said many investors had expected the yuan to weaken in the coming weeks, but Chinese authorities' allowing it to breach 7 now prompted a selloff in stocks and other riskier assets. Stock indexes in Japan, South Korea and Hong Kong dropped more than 2% each. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US TREASURYS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury prices surged, sending yields lower, as investors sought havens after China allowed its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken below a closely watched level, marking an intensification of the U.S.-China trade war.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note fell dropped 11.3. basis points to 1.742%. The 2-year Treasury yield fell 13.25 basis points to 1.581%, while the 30-year Treasury bond yield dropped 9.3 basis points to 2.297%. Yields move in the opposite direction of debt prices. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European markets end the first session of the week firmly in the red as rising trade tensions between the US and China spook investors. The Stoxx Europe 600 drops 2.3%, with the FTSE 100 down nearly 2.5%, the DAX off 1.8% and the CAC-40 falling 2.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China irritated Washington by allowing the yuan to decline against the dollar and reportedly instructing state-controlled firms not to buy US agricultural goods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The trade war is heating up and dealers fear the dispute will drag on for the foreseeable future," says David Madden at \f28 \uc2 \f2 CMC Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "China is a major buyer of metals and oil, and in turn mining and energy stocks have suffered." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei fell 1.7% and Korea's Kospi dropped 2.6%, while the yuan weakened beyond the psychologically important 7-per-dollar level, falling as much as 1.9% to 7.1087 per dollar in Hong Kong in early trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Monday the yuan's decline was a result of trade protectionism and higher tariffs on Chinese goods. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the yuan remains stable and strong against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and that the bank has the ability to keep it at a "reasonable equilibrium." It also said it would crack down on short-term speculation in the yuan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a tweet Monday morning reacting to the yuan's drop, Mr. Trump said: "China dropped the price of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to an almost a historic low. It's called '\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index fell nearly 3%, as a citywide strike disrupted the airport and subway services. It followed a ninth weekend of protests against a controversial extradition bill and China's growing influence on the city. In a speech Monday, Hong Kong's leader \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carrie Lam\f28 \uc2 \f2  said society has become dangerous and unstable. The city's stock market has fallen 9% in the past few weeks as the protests dent business sentiment and weigh on economic growth. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian shares marked their sharpest fall in almost eight months, dented along with markets across the region by worries about global trade amid heightened tensions between the U.S. and China. The S&P/ASX 200 closed 1.9% weaker at 6640.3, near the day's low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since logging an all-time closing high last Tuesday, the index has declined the last four-straight sessions and has now pulled back 3%. Losses were broad, led by a 5.2% drop in the technology subindex. The heavily-weighted biggest four banks were down by 0.8%-1.7%, and the miners saw heavy selling as \f28 \uc2 \f2 BHP\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  each lost more than 3% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortescue\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 7.2% with a sharp decline in Chinese iron-ore futures. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ZEALAND STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand shares suffered sustained selling by investors as U.S.-China trade tensions simmered, with the benchmark NZX-50 index closing 0.9% lower at 10766.03. The NZX-50 has fallen about 1.4% since hitting an all-time high at the end of last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dairy stocks were hit hardest, with market heavyweight A2 Milk down 3.3% at NZ$17.05 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Synlait Milk\f28 \uc2 \f2  falling 3.7% to NZ$9.65. Both companies are key exporters to China and risk becoming collateral damage in the trade row. Utilities were mostly lower, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Contact Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 1.9% at NZ$7.77 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Meridian Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropping 1.1% to NZ$4.77. A large-cap stock bucking the negative trend was \f28 \uc2 \f2 Auckland International Airport\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which rose 2.1% to NZ$9.55. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN & NZ CORPORATE & ECONOMIC CALENDAR: \par Tuesday, August 6, 2019 \par  \par CORPORATE: \par Local/GMT/ET \par 0900/2300/1900  AUS     Pinnacle Investment            Full year 2019 Earnings conference call \par                         Management Group Limited \par                         (PNI.AU) \par 0900/0100/2100  AUS     \f28 \uc2 \f2 BWP Trust\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BWP.AU)             Full year 2019 Earnings conference call \par 1000/0000/2000  AUS     Shopping Centres Australasia   Full year 2019 Webcast \par                         Property Group (SCP.AU) \par      N/A        AUS     \f28 \uc2 \f2 BWP Trust\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BWP.AU)             Full year 2019 Results \par      N/A        AUS     Pinnacle Investment            Full year 2019 Results \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Aug 2019 15:47 ET \par Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Plummett -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                         Management Group Limited \par                         (PNI.AU) \par      N/A        AUS     Shopping Centres Australasia   Full year 2019 Results \par                         Property Group (SCP.AU) \par  \par ECONOMIC/POLITICAL: \par Local/GMT/ET \par 1045/2245/1845  NZ   Q2       Labour Cost Index (Salary & Wage Rates) \par 1045/2245/1845  NZ   Q2       Quarterly Employment Survey \par 1045/2245/1845  NZ   Q2       Household Labour Force Survey \par 1130/0130/2130  AUS  Jul      ANZ Job Ads \par 1130/0130/2130  AUS  Jun      International Trade in Goods & Services \par 1430/0430/0030  AUS  Aug      Australian cash rate decision \par 1500/0300/2300  NZ            RBNZ Survey of Expectations \par  \par OIL FUTURES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures resumed their drop, tracking losses ripping through broader financial markets as concern for a prolonged trade war and its risk to global crude demand was rekindled.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last week, the U.S. oil benchmark suffered its biggest one-day fall in more than four years and ended lower for the week after President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  moved to impose additional import tariffs on Chinese goods and China pledged retaliation on other goods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday weakened below the important level of 7 yuan to the dollar, sparking a global selloff in equities and other assets perceived as risky as investors flooded into haven assets, including Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This latest headline risk does suggest both sides [on the U.S.-China negotiations] are digging in for the long haul and it's unlikely \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  supply discipline will provide an adequate counterbalance to these escalating trade tensions, which could weigh like an anchor on oil prices this week," said Stephen Innes, managing partner with VM Markets in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 West Texas Intermediate crude for September delivery settled down 97 cents, or 1.7%, to $54.69 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's rebound recovered a portion of the 7.9% drop from Thursday. But the front-month contract still suffered a 1% weekly loss last week, according to Dow Jones Market Data, as WTI's drop on Thursday marked the biggest percentage fall for a front-month contract since Feb. 4, 2015 and the settlement at $53.95 that day was the lowest since June 19, according to Dow Jones Market Data. WTI remains off 17% from its 2019 settle high of $66.30 hit April 23.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global benchmark October Brent crude settled at $59.81 a barrel on ICE Europe, down slightly on the day. With its loss fattened by Thursday's 7% slide, the contract ended 2.3% lower for last week. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US COMMODITIES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold's haven appeal boosted the metal to a more than six-year high, as an escalating U.S.-China trade fight sparked a selloff in assets perceived as risky.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold, now up about 15% from its low of 2019, has surged over recent sessions after President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  intensified a trade fight with China by announcing additional tariffs on Chinese goods and China pledged retaliation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's yuan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday fell to its lowest level in more than a decade, breaching the key 7-to-the-dollar level. Investors took that as a sign Beijing could allow further weakness, with the potential to further intensify trade tensions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold for December delivery on Comex rose $19, or 1.3%, to $1,464.60 an ounce. That was the highest most-active contract finish since May 9, 2013, according to Dow Jones market data. Prices ended about 2.7% higher for last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The yellow metal is on the verge of a making for a run towards the $1,500 an ounce level and after that there is not much resistance until the $1,650 region," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Edward Moya\f28 \uc2 \f2  , senior market analyst at Oanda. "The trade war, a negative global interest-rate environment and weak corporate earnings are all positive catalysts for the remainder of the summer for gold."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September silver -- which because of its industrial use in addition to haven status can be negatively impacted by the trade news -- added 12 cents, or 0.8%, to $16.34 an ounce Monday. It posted a loss of 0.8% for last week, however.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, industrial metals traded mixed. October platinum gained $7.50, or 0.8%, to $860.50 an ounce, after a weekly decline of around 1.7% through Friday. September palladium lost $14.30, or 1%, at $1,418.50 an ounce. Palladium logged at an 8.3% drop for last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September copper fell nearly 2 cents, or 0.6%, to $2.555 a pound, ending 4.2% lower for last week. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES: \par China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Weakening Escalates Trade War \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beijing's move to devalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  escalated the U.S.-China trade fight yet again and raised the potential of another retaliatory round from Washington. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trade Tensions Whipsaw Hog Prices \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global trade tensions weighed on trader expectations that China will continue to rely on U.S. pork exports, leading to a volatile session in hog futures. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Farm-Income Pinch Filters Through U.S. Economy \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As China halts buying U.S. farm goods, American farmers' own financials come under pressure. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Services-Sector Growth is Slowest in Three Years \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. service sector expanded at the slowest pace in nearly three years in July-with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Institute for Supply Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  's nonmanufacturing purchasing managers index falling to 53.7 from 55.1 in June-in a sign that a key segment of the economy continues to cool. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tyson Discloses Justice Department Subpoena in Chicken-Pricing Probe \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tyson Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it received a subpoena from the Justice Department, signaling an expansion of an investigation into chicken pricing. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2   to Slash Thousands of Jobs After Ousting CEO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  plans to cut up to 2% of its 237,685 employees, as the bank flagged a worsening outlook for the global economy in its second-quarter results. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Australia Looks to Siphon U.S. Oil Stockpile to Avoid Running Out of Gas \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. is considering opening its emergency oil reserves to Australia, as concerns grow about shipping security and this Washington ally's ability to withstand a global oil-supply shock. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citgo\f28 \uc2 \f2  Beats Power Grab From Maduro Loyalists \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corporate directors tied to opposition leader \f28 \uc2 \f2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\f28 \uc2 \f2  have a better claim to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citgo\f28 \uc2 \f2  because of his recognition by the Trump administration as Venezuela's legitimate head of state, according to a ruling issued Friday by Vice Chancellor Kathaleen McCormick of Delaware's Court of Chancery. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cars.com\f28 \uc2 \f2   Completes Strategic Review But Finds No Buyer \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cars.com\f28 \uc2 \f2  said that it will remain independent after a strategic review didn't yield a buyer for the online car marketplace.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 05, 2019 15:47 ET (19:47 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190805ef85002m5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
