\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Tumble by China's yuan sends most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  into a tailspin\b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2019\par 07:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * U.S.-China trade war escalates, making investors seek\par safety\par     * Markets worry whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the trade dispute's new\par front \par     * South Korea's won badly affected amid spat with Japan \par \par  (Adds context on yuan falling below key level, updates prices)\par     By Nikhil Nainan\par     Aug 5 (Reuters) - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  across Asia suffered sharp\par losses on Monday as China's yuan crossed the 7 per dollar level\par for the first time in over a decade, following escalation of the\par Sino-U.S. trade war to new heights.\par     The yuan sank nearly 1.5% to 7.042, breaching the\par key level for the first time since the global financial crisis\par in 2008. The closely-managed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move lower sets the\par stage potentially for a new front in the trade dispute.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  "has effectively weaponised the\par exchange rate, even if it is not proactively weakening the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Julian Evans-Pritchard,\par Capital Economics senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  economist said in a note.\par     The build-up of hostilities comes after U.S. President\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  late last week declared he would slap 10% tariffs\par on $300 billion in Chinese imports, ending a month-long trade\par truce. \par     "We had anticipated that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  would eventually devalue\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in response to trade tensions but hadn't expected\par it to come quite this soon," Evans-Pritchard added. \par     Uncertainty engulfed Asian markets in general on Monday,\par with flows moving to safer bets, with the Japanese yen\par strengthening 0.6%.\par     Chris Weston, head of research at Pepperstone brokerage in\par Australia, said in a note the market "really does not need\par worries about a capital flight in China at this point in time\par and a subsequent tightening of financial conditions in the\par Chinese economy".\par     Trade-reliant economies such as South Korea took major hits\par on Monday. The won touched a three-year low of\par 1,218.3 per dollar, while the Taiwan dollar dropped\par 0.7%.\par     Also weighing on the won is its diplomatic spat with Japan,\par which tightened controls in July on exports to South Korea. This\par escalated a row over wartime forced labourers and sparking a\par boycott by South Korean consumers of Japanese products and\par services.\par     "The South Korean won is least favoured because of its\par country's spat with Japan over the latter's export ban," DBS\par said in a note. \par     India's rupee also fell to its weakest level since\par early March, ahead of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meeting on Wednesday, at\par which DBS expects a 25 basis point rate cut. \par     Indonesia reported its weakest economic growth in two years\par in April-June, though the 5.05% figure matched market forecasts\par and did not move the rupiah, which was 0.7% lower at\par 14,270 to the dollar for the day.\par     The Thai and Philippine central banks  have policy meetings\par this week amid a global easing wave as growth falters. \par     Thailand's baht, the strongest Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this\par year, was marginally lower while the Philippine peso\par dropped 0.4% to 51.84 a dollar.\par     \par \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par  Change on the day at 0459 GMT                    \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                Latest bid  Previous day  Pct Move\par  Japan yen              106.020     106.58        +0.53\par  Sing dlr               1.382       1.3765        -0.41\par  Taiwan dlr             31.609      31.376        -0.74\par  Korean won             1214.000    1198          -1.32\par  Baht                   30.840      30.8          -0.13\par  Peso                   51.840      51.64         -0.39\par  Rupiah                 14270.000   14175         -0.67\par  Rupee                  70.425      69.59         -1.19\par  Ringgit                4.173       4.155         -0.43\par  Yuan                   7.029       6.9420        -1.24\par                                                   \par  Change so far in 2019                            \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                Latest bid  End 2018      Pct Move\par  Japan yen              106.020     109.56        +3.34\par  Sing dlr               1.382       1.3627        -1.41\par  Taiwan dlr             31.609      30.733        -2.77\par  Korean won             1214.000    1115.70       -8.10\par  Baht                   30.840      32.55         +5.54\par  Peso                   51.840      52.47         +1.22\par  Rupiah                 14270.000   14375         +0.74\par  Rupee                  70.425      69.77         -0.93\par  Ringgit                4.173       4.1300        -1.03\par  Yuan                   7.029       6.8730        -2.22\par  \par  (Reporting by Nikhil Kurian Nainan in Bengaluru; Editing by\par Richard Borsuk)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-5T06