he sole owner of the land and decides on its allocation and use, so it is common for companies to receive subsidies on it, for example, the producers of wire rod Ansteel Group or \uc2 Shougang Group\uc2  , which have the support of their respective municipal governments, as noted in the Report of the European Commission and the Report of the Coalition of the Steel Industry. The \uc2 U.S. Department of Commerce\uc2  also noted, in the Report of the \uc2 U.S. Department of Commerce\uc2 's Final Determination on Imports of Carbon Steel Wire and Alloy from China, which Chinese government-owned companies wire-producers benefit from land tenure by acquiring it below its fair value; labour is another factor of production used in the manufacture of the product under review and throughout its upstream production chain, which is also distorted by Chinese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by not allowing free association and wage bias, which is possible, as China has not ratified four agreements that the \uc2 International Labour Organization\uc2  considers fundamental and would allow workers to be given rights and market-based wages the existence of residence controls through the hukou system that hinders the mobility of workers; (viii) electricity is used intensively in the production of the product under review and throughout the upstream production chain. Government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  distorts the cost of it and the entire production chain by having control and ownership over 50% of the energy-generating companies and 100% of those that distribute it, which allowed it to benefit the company of 60 million RMB that producer of wire rod and wire products "Anyang Steel", as noted in the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  Report. For its part, the \uc2 U.S. Department of Commerce\uc2  also noted, in the Report of the \uc2 Us Department of Commerce\uc2 's Final Determination on Imports of Carbon Steel Wire rod and China's Alloy, that China's owned by theChinese government wire producers benefit from electricity below their fair value, which de facto makes the implementation of the special pricing and preferential tariff policy for the steel industry set out above; ix. as noted in the comments of the AISI, the Chinese government, to ensure the supply of iron ore, a primary input in the production of liquid steel, has invested in iron ore deposits outside China. In addition, it subsidizes commodity prices or restricts exports of these products to support its steel industry, y x.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0the cost structure of the wire rod is distorted by the costs of land, electricity, labor, iron ore and the main input of the wire, which is the billet, as the producers of the wire rod buy it below its fair market value , as noted by the \uc2 U.S. Department of Commerce\uc2 . Moreover, by government policies that provide financial support, and by owning major steel producers, their cost is also distorted. 68. The Secretariat analyzed the information provided by Deacero and requested additional evidence of the assertions made, an explanation of the similarity of the steel chain production process of weldments between Mexico and China, and an analysis of the composition and contribution of the production factors used intensively in the manufacture of the product under review, as well as its cost structure, in order to observe the distortion caused in the product under review by the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In addition, it was requested to consider in its analysis the criteria provided for in the second paragraph of Article 48 of the RLCE, in order to determine whether the welded-link steel chain producing sector operates under the principles of a non- Market. In this regard, Deacero noted: a. the production process of the steel chain in both Mexico and China is capital intensive. The technology of the machines is mature, based mainly on electronic panels that automates the activity of forming the links by cutting and bending the wire, giving the shape to the link; the welding activity of the links, by applying welding on the link to close it, and the activity of calibration or chain testing by applying a mechanical force; b. land and capital are the most representative factors of production, and to a lesser extent work and energy. Such factors are identified as one of the areas in which significant distortions persist, representing the raw material the largest proportion within the cost structure of the product under review; c. distortions in production factors are legitimized in the public policies of the Chinese government, due to the participation and control it exercises over its costs and prices, which directly and indirectly affects the product under review and its entire chain and confers benefits that enable companies to reduce their operating expenses and set more competitive prices in export markets; d. Because China's steel chain-producing industry is highly exporting, it benefits from the Chinese government's control over the exchange rate by allowing it to sell its exports at competitive prices, as is clear from the Trade Map statistics; (e) as regards labour, government control restricts freedom of negotiation, mobilization and does not guarantee the minimum wage in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's domestic market; f. Chinese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is throughpublic policies and through the ownership and participation of the same in the companies of the steel sector, of which the product under review and its entire production chain are part, which has led to serious distortions in the investment of that sector, as well as such as the increase in its production capacity, which does not correspond to the market behavior and the depression of its prices, fact documented in the research "The China, Inc Challenge to Global Trade Governance" and in the document of \uc2 Wiley Rein LLP\uc2 . Investment restrictions continue to occur, despite the approval of foreign investment in the steel sector only in four of the free trade zones, as documented in the article "China will allow foreign-owned steel plants in four free zones"; G.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0the control and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Chinese government in the steel, energy, railway and financial system industries directly affects the cost and price structure of the product under review, as it allows companies to have access to energy, gas, water, freight and inputs such as billet and wire rod, at prices below their market level, giving advantage to these companies and even more to those integrated vertically, by decreasing their costs and prices being able to compete with low prices. Government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also translates into debt restructuring and subsidies for these industries; h. Qingdao Special Steel, a producer of welded-link steel chain, was restructured and transferred to another Chinese government-owned company, Grupo Citic, a very important industrial conglomerate, manufacturer of supplies used in the production of the product under review. Companies have access to the benefits of belonging to the Chinese government, as Qingdao Special Steel's assets were transferred in their entirety to Grupo Citic, without any of the companies incurring any cost. In addition, Grupo Citic is under the control of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it takes over its financial obligations, as documented in the company profiles and news of the city of Qingdao, and i.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0these companies, because of their status as enterprises, benefit from government policies at the country level, such as labour policies that restrict the bargaining power of wages and labour mobility, monetary policies that set the value of the exchange rate in order to promote exports, financial policies that allow companies to have access to sources of capital at a lower cost, and more importantly, support policies, since, as is the case with \uc2 Citic Group\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes over your financial obligations. 69. Deacero concluded that the distortion in the cost and price structure of the steel sector, including the product industry under review, as well as its upstream production chain, the lack of freedom in the negotiation of wages, the government's interference and control through state-owned enterprises and through the participation of its party's groups, the government's participation in the exchange rate, and problems in the compatibility of accounting methods, provide a basis for consider that in China the production of welded-link steel chain does not operate in accordance with the principles of a market economy. Which is why the domestic market prices of a substitute country should be used. b. Determination 70. The Secretariat carried out a comprehensive analysis of the arguments and information provided in this review in the administrative file. 71. In principle, the Secretariat notes that, in accordance with paragraph 15(d) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO,only subparagraph (a) Roma (ii) expired in December 2016. However, subparagraph (a) and Roma (i) of paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO remain as existing text. This subparagraph (a) provides for the possibility of applying a price or cost-based methodology in China, of Chinese producers, or a methodology that is not based on those prices or costs. Thus, the Secretariat considers that the mere subparagraph (ii) of paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO does not mean that there is no longer the possibility of using a methodology that is not based on a comparison strict with domestic prices or costs in China. 72. Indeed, the methodological basis for determining price comparability in anti-dumping proceedings involving products of Chinese origin is expressly contained in paragraph 15 (a) of the Protocol of China's accession to the WTO, which, like Roma (i), has not expired. Under subparagraph (a), there is a legal possibility to use the prices or costs of the Chinese producers investigated in China, or to use a methodology that is not based on a strict comparison with prices or costs in China. 73. However, the Roma (i) points out that where the investigated producer can clearly demonstrate that, in the industry producing the product similar to that investigated, the conditions of a market economy prevail with regard to manufacturing, the production and sale of such product, domestic prices or costs shall be used in China. 74. In addition, the expiration of Roma (ii) of paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO implies that, in order to be used a calculation methodology that is not based on a strict comparison with domestic prices or costs in China, evidence is needed that the investigated Chinese producers producing the like product operate under conditions that do not correspond to a market economy with respect to the manufacture, production and sale of such a product. 75. In this area of ideas, it is important to note that in this proceeding, No Chinese exporters or any other interested party appeared, and therefore no information was available to call into the case provided by Deacero. 76. Consequently, in order to support the prevailing cost and price structures in China and, in particular, in the welded-link steel chain production industry, it is subject to analysis of the arguments and evidence provided by Deacero to support it, in accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the RLCE, as well as subparagraph (a) romanite (i) of paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO. 77. Accordingly, the Secretariat considers that there is a legal basis for assessing Deacero's proposal to consider China as a non-market economy in the production and sale of steel chains and to analyze the merits of applying the substitute country methodology , in accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 RLCE. Therefore, the Secretariat conducted a comprehensive analysis of factors in the macroeconomic environment and the specific conditions that characterize the production, manufacture and sale of welded-link steel chains. 78. The Secretariat noted, from the evidence provided by Deacero, that there is control of the Chinese government in the steel industry based on regulatory provisions, public policies or direct government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that selectively discriminate companies, which have an impact on price formation, either throughsuppression of capital and energy costs, restrictions on labour mobility or the absence of markets on land ownership, which has a distorting effect on the prices and costs of the steel chain, as a result of the following : a. there are a considerable number of companies in the Chinese steel industry, which are owned by the government and controlled by various bodies and appliances created by it, as is the case of the Chinese Communist Party, which is involved in the chain-producing company Qingdao Special Steel, as its General Manager is a member of that party and has held positions in the Chinese government, and SASAC, an institution responsible for providing capital and appointing the managers of the public companies it manages. You are in control of Baosteel Co., Ltd., Hebei Iron and Steel Group and Anyang Steel Group, major steel companies that manufacture, among other products, wire rod, input from which the raw material is obtained for the production of steel chain of welded links; b. there is government control when setting the prices of certain supplies used in the steel chain's production chain, such as electricity and gas through the NDRC; differentiated electricity and water prices in the steel industry through regional policies, such as in Hebei province, where the restructuring of the steel industry is also sought from the construction of mega-enterprises, including include \uc2 Shougang Group\uc2  and HBIS Group Tangsteel, and companies such as Great Wall Steel and Bohai Steel, the latter gun manufacturer; c. The Chinese government provides support to its steel producers, for example through laws, industrial policies and plans, preferential loans, debt-for-capital loans, and tax refunds. It also provides them with indirect support, such as restrictions on foreign investment. For example, its Thirteenth Five-Year Plan directs financial institutions, at different levels of government, to use existing funding channels to carry out the tasks established therein; d. The financial bailout, by the Chinese government, of different companies in the Chinese steel industry, such as Bohai Steel, \uc2 Chongqing Iron & Steel Co., Ltd\uc2 ., Valin Steel Co. is documented. Ltd. and \uc2 Dongbei Special Steel Group Co., Ltd\uc2 ., all of which are Chinese government-owned steel companies and wire rod producers, among other products; e. government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in companies that are part of the Chinese steel industry has caused an excess in the production capacity of the industry, in contrast to its low profitability, and a growing debt problem. \uc2 Wiley Rein LLP\uc2 's study notes that China's steel industry has lost billions of RMB, and its debt continues to grow. Under the direction of the Chinese government, the debt is continuously refinanced, expanded and eventually removed from accounts to become "asset management companies" or other financial companies designed to absorb corporate debt uncollectable and cover losses; f. in this context, the steel chain and other supplies of the qingdao Special Steel production chain was rescued by the Qingdao Municipal Government under the government-owned corporate restructuring policy. According to the information on the record, Quindao Special Steel signed the Steel Restructuring Cooperation Agreement with Grupo Citic, which is also a state-owned company. The agreement stipulates that, in accordance with national laws and regulations, assets are transferred without compensation and the \uc2 Citic Group\uc2  assumes all rights and debts of Qingdao Special Steel creditors. AlsoCitic Group will invest large sums of money to turn Qingdao Special Steel into a world-class steel base; G.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0this is in accordance with the public policies of the Chinese central government set out in the 13th Five-Year Plan, as it directs local governments to apply preferential credits, tax exemptions, the provision of electricity services, water and land-use rights below their value. This only shows that State \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , both in the value chain of the product under review and in the producers of the product under review, confers advantages and distorts the market. State \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has prevented the closure of unprofitable enterprises, leading to a problem of overproduction, a fact that would be contrary in a system based on market forces; h. there are distortions in the allocation of raw materials and supplies that affect the allocation of resources in welded-link steel chain production and interfere with the cost and price determination of factors in which it is intensive , through the following situations: (i) distortion in electricity prices, through government-determined prices, for example, electricity distribution prices of electricity grids. The distortion in electricity prices affects the entire "upstream" production chain of the welded-link steel chain, considering that the production process of wire rod is also intensive in the use of electric power, which increases the distortion already existing in the production of the product under review itself; ii. the wire rod cost structure is distorted by the costs of land, electricity and billet, as wire producers buy it below its fair market value; iii. the prices of the supplies of the product under review may be depressed because the government grants land and minerals to various enterprises, including those owned by it. In addition, Chinese wire producers have access to purchase supplies below market prices, making it clear that there is an undercutting of raw material production costs, which is transferred at wire prices and is reflects the costs and prices of the product under review; iv. the financing provided by the Chinese government to capital is based on the ownership of four banks and investment funds under SASAC's control to government-owned steel companies, and v.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  distorts the cost structure of land, labor and capital causing a multiplier effect on the wire rod and billet, which are the forerunner supplies of the welded-link steel chain. The effect results in depressed costs and prices, and causes distortion in steel welded link chain production and investment decisions. i. The Secretariat identified distortions in macroeconomic variables, as described below: (i) the Secretariat noted that the Chinese government was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the macroeconomic level in foreign direct investment, as it was subject to approval by multiple government entities. In addition, it requires foreign investors to transfer technology, resources, brands, financial services, among others, to their companies; ii. the exchange rate policy established by the Chinese government that affects all sectors of the economy in general, translates into an exchange rate containment by the Chinese government allowing it to only fluctuate within a band determined by the same Itin order to boost its exports. This policy results in exports with prices below international prices set by the laws of supply and demand, and iii.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0labor, being subject to residency controls by the Chinese government, does not allow wages to be established through free negotiation between employees and employers, resulting in wage containment. As one of the productive factors in the manufacture of the product under review, it can be inferred that this has an impact on the cost and price structure of the subject merchandise. 79. On the basis of the above, the Secretariat considers that the information provided by Deacero raises the presumption that in companies producing link steel chain in China, cost and price structures prevail that are not determined according to principles because market distortions were identified in the market of factors affecting the allocation of resources in the production of the product under review and interfering with the determination of the costs and prices of the productive factors in which it is intensivo. On account of the above, and in accordance with paragraph 15(a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the RLCE, the Secretariat proceeded to consider the proposal to use a market economy country of China to effect calculation of the normal value. c. Substitute country selection 80. Deacero proposed To Brazil as a substitute country for China to determine normal value. To support the existence of market conditions in Brazil's steel chain sector, he indicated that the price is determined by the free participation between bidders and claimants, there are no restrictions on the entry or exit of the product, there is no interference decisions in that sector, since the government does not own any steel chain production company and has no price controls or quotas for the production, marketing, import or export of that product, as set out above in the market study presented by an independent consultant. 81. Based on Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 third paragraph of the RLCE, Deacero submitted the following: a. the report "Brazil Investment Climate Statement 2018", prepared by the \uc2 United States Department of State\uc2  and the "Report on Labor Relations in Bra deloitte," which says that there is freedom of employment in Brazil, since workers have the right to be part of trade unions, collective bargaining and enjoy fundamental labour rights; b. According to the reports "Doing Business and Investing in Brazil 2013", prepared by the consulting firm \uc2 PricewaterhouseCoopers\uc2 , and "Brazil Investment Climate Statement 2018", prepared by the \uc2 United States Department of State\uc2 , do not exist restrictions on foreign investment in the steel industry, with Brazil encouraging foreign direct investment. Similarly, the latter report indicates that there is free convertibility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and that the purchase or sale rates are determined by market forces; c. The Study "Market Analysis of Welded Steel Chains", updated to 2018 by an independent Brazilian consultant, notes that market economy conditions prevail in Brazil's steel chain industry, in addition to no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry decisions on prices and costs, quota controls, or import or export bans, and (d).\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0the study "Industrial Competitiveness in Brazil 10 years after liberalization", prepared by the Economic Commissionfor Latin America and the Caribbean, 2004, indicates that market conditions prevailed in Brazil since the 1990s, as economic reforms led to the demise of price controls and protected markets; reduction of tariff and non-tariff barriers and the privatization of manufacturing industries, including steelmaking. i. Brazil is a producer of welded-link steel chain 82. The study "Market analysis of welded steel chains", updated to 2018 by an independent Brazilian consultant, notes that in Brazil there are more than 10 producers of steel chains, with three of these companies concentrating more than 80% of production product under review. ii. Similarity in the production process 83. To demonstrate the similarity in the manufacturing process of the product under review, Deacero presented the diagram of the production process in Brazil and China, as well as a comparative table of these processes. In addition, it noted that, according to a communication with the company producing machinery used in the production of welded-link steel chain, the manufacturing process in both countries has the same phases, using automatic machinery and mature technology, being capital-intensive. iii. Availability of the main supplies 84. Based on the statistics reported by the CRU for 2018, Deacero stated that both Brazil and China are producers of steel wire rod, the main input for the manufacture of the steel chain, in addition to the production of the wire rod exceeding the consumption of the steel rod. With regard to electricity, another input to the production process mentioned that both countries have access to a vast supply of input. As documentary support, it provided the electricity production and consumption statistics for the "Global Energy Statistical Yearbook 2018". iv. Other Elements 85. Deacero indicated that the prices of the Brazilian product identical or similar to the one examined are not distorted by government policies or unfair trade practices, as indicated in the Study "Market Analysis of Welded Steel Chains", updated to 2018 by an independent Brazilian consultant. In addition, it noted that ownership of steel companies was in the private sector, as noted on the website of the Brazilian Iron and Steel Institute. It also stated that Brazil's steel industry does not report receiving subsidies or other benefits from the Brazilian government, as per seen, according to Brazil's 2018 NOTIFICATION of WTO subsidies. 86. It argued that the information and documentary evidence it provided endorses Brazil as China's reasonable substitute country for the purpose of calculating normal value in this proceeding. v. Determination 87. As noted in the previous points, the Secretariat conducted a comprehensive analysis of the information provided by Deacero to consider Brazil as a substitute country for China, for the purpose of calculating a normal value. The Secretariat noted that the two countries manufactured welded-link steel chain and that there was similarity in production processes. Regarding the availability of inputs necessary for the manufacture of the same, in both Brazil and China there is production of steel wire rod, main input for its manufacture. From the above, it can be reasonably inferred that the intensity in the use of the factors of welded-link steel chain production is similar in both countries. 88. Based on the analysis described in points 80 to 86 of this Resolution, and in accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the RLCE, the Secretariat considered using Brazil as a market economy countryChina's substitute for the calculation of a normal value. d. Domestic prices in Brazil 89. To prove normal value in Brazil, Deacero presented the study "Market Analysis of Welded Steel Chains", updated to 2018 by an independent Brazilian consultant. This study includes the academic and work profile of the consultant, highlighting that he has more than 50 years of experience in the steel industry in Brazil and Mexico. The Secretariat corroborated the consultant's information at the Brazilian consulting firm that carried out the market study in the previous procedure, corresponding to the product under review and its relationship with the industry containing the product under review. 90. The Market Study contains real price references per kilogram of the welded-link steel chain of six Brazilian producers, representing more than 90% of Brazil's production. Quotes refer to various types of steel chain considering diameter and finish (polished or galvanized) and correspond to August 2018, at the ex-factory level. 91. Deacero noted that price benchmarks are above cost and result in a profit margin, which is corroborated by comparing them with the cost structure included in the market study. 92. Because the quotes are outside the review period, Deacero estimated the cumulative variation of each month of the review period using the Brazil Iron and Steel Producer Price Index, which it obtained from the Institute Brazilian Geography and Statistics. To convert prices to dollars per kilogram, he used the average exchange rate published by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil for the review period. 93. Based on Articles 2.1 of the AD Agreement, 31 of the LCE and 39, 48 and 58 of the RLCE, the Secretariat considered the information proposed by Deacero and calculated the normal value for each of the 12 specific types of steel chain observed in the calculation export price. e. Domestic price adjustments in Brazil 94. Since some of the price quotes for Brazil's domestic market include local taxes and discounts, Deacero proposed to adjust those prices for tax charges, in particular for the Goods Circulation Tax and Services (ICMS) and for discount. 95. According to the market study, the prices of two Brazilian production companies include ICMS, and one producer has a discount on the list price by volume. In this regard, Deacero calculated the corresponding amount of taxes and the applicable discount percentage and subtracted them from gross prices. 96. Based on Articles 2.4 of the AD Agreement, 36 of the LCE and 53 and 57 of the RLCE, the Secretariat applied the proposed adjustments and considered the information provided by domestic production. f. Prices in the domestic market of China 97. Deacero noted that, although China continues to behave as a non-market economy, it offers price references for steel chains in China's domestic market as another option for calculating normal value in these proceedings. To this end, it provided quotes from four Chinese producers and a Chinese marketer, provided by consultant UNO. The Secretariat verified the consultant's information on the website and noted that it was a research and analysis company with experience in anti-dumping proceedings. 98. The producers' and marketers' quotes contain the prices in dollars per tonne, at the ex-factory level, of different types of steel chains according to diameter and finish (polished or galvanized). 99. Deacero noted that Chinese steel chain producers are representative,they have a large productive capacity, and the marketer reports significant annual income. 100. Since the quotes are outside the review period, Deacero adjusted them for inflation, for which he used the inflation index information in China, which he obtained from the website Inflation.eu. 101. With regard to marketer's quotes, domestic production adjusted them for margin of marketing, which it obtained from consultant UNO. 102. On the basis of these quotations, the Secretariat identified the types of steel chain by considering diameter and finish (polished or galvanized), referred to in point 57 of this Resolution, and calculated an average normal value for each type of steel chain dollars per kilogram for which it had information of normal value. In addition, it adjusted the marketer's prices for margin of marketing and applied the inflation adjustment to bring the price references to the period under review. The above, in accordance with Articles 2.1 and 2.4 of the AD Agreement, 31 and 36 of the LCE and 39, 53 and 58 of the RLCE. 3. Conclusion 103. Since the purpose of these proceedings is to determine the consequences of the elimination of the countervailing quota, the Secretariat considered for the analysis the information in the administrative file, i.e. it assessed both the information domestic prices in the proposed substitute country, as well as prices in China's domestic market. 104. In accordance with the information and methodologies described above, and on the basis of Articles 6.8, 11.3 and Annex II of the AD Agreement, 54 second paragraph, 64 last paragraph and 89 F of the LCE, the Secretariat analysed export price information and of normal value. It determined that there is sufficient evidence to support the elimination of the countervailing quota would repeat dumping on exports to Mexico of steel chains originating in China. F. Analysis of continuation or recurrence of damage 105. The Secretariat analyzed the information in the administrative dossier, as well as the information that it itself relied on, to determine whether there were elements to support the elimination of the final countervailing quota imposed on chain imports of steel from welded links originating in China, would result in continuation or recurrence of injury to the domestic industry of the like product. 106. In carrying out its analysis, the Secretariat considered the information for the period from 1 April 2013 to 31 March 2018, which includes both the period analyzed and the review period, as well as the estimates for the April 20, 2018 periods March 18-March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020. Unless otherwise indicated, the behaviour of the economic and financial indicators of a given year or period is analysed with respect to the previous immediate equivalent period. 1. Domestic Industry 107. Deacero and Industrial Wires stated that the national welded link steel chain industry is currently formed by Deacero, Industrial De Wires and Truper. To prove the above, they submitted a letter issued by CANACERO recognizing them as domestic producers of welded-link steel chain, while ANTAAC submitted a letter recognizing them as domestic steel chain producers and provided for Deacero and Industriade de Alambres their production volume and percentage of participation during the period analyzed. 108. In addition, in order to corroborate the participation in domestic production of the production companies, the Secretariat requested Truper to submit its volume ofproduction and sales for each of the periods that make up the period analyzed. 109. Based on the information in the administrative file, the Secretariat determined that Deacero and Industrial de Alambres constitute the domestic industry, accounting for a significant proportion of the domestic steel chain production of in accordance with Articles 4.1 and 5.4 of the AD Agreement, 40 and 50 of the LCE and 60 and 61 of the RLCE, since in the review period its joint production accounted for 64% of domestic production. 2. International Market 110. Deacero stated that it did not have specific information relating to the product under review available to it, and therefore submitted available data at the level of steel wire rod, on production and domestic consumption on the world market, obtained from the website CRU. It also submitted statistical information obtained from Trade Map on global steel chain exports and imports through subheading 7315.82 for the period 2013 to 2017. 111. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that the information submitted by Deacero corresponded to carbon steel wire rod, a commodity other than the one under review, however, it considered this information in its international market analysis as the main input for the manufacture of welded link steel chain. 112. The Secretariat analysed the information described in the previous points and noted that global steel wire mill production showed a cumulative decrease of 5% from 220.2 million to 209.6 million tonnes from 2013 to 2017. In 2017 it focused mainly on the following producing regions: Asia 72.1%, Western Europe 9.3%, North America 3% and the Commonwealth of Independent States (CIS) 2.7%. The main producing country was China, with a 65% stake, followed by Germany with 3%, Japan with 2.7% and Italy with 2.2%, while Mexico participated with 1.3%. 113. In addition, the apparent global consumption of steel wire rodwas similar to that of production, from 219.2 to 208.5 million tonnes, from 2013 to 2017, resulting in a cumulative decrease of 5% over the entire period. In 2017 this indicator was distributed in the main regions: Asia 71%, Western Europe 8.9% and North America 3.6%. The largest consumer was China with 62.6%, followed by Germany with 2.6%, Italy with 2.2% and Japan with 2.1%, while Mexico consumed 1.2%. 114. The production balance minus steel wire rod consumption indicates that in the period 2013 to 2017, Asia, The CIS and Western Europe recorded exportable surpluses of 23.8, 8.7 and 7.3 million tonnes, respectively. The countries with the largest exportable surplus were China, Japan, Germany and Spain, with 45.8, 6.7, 4.8 and 4.3 million tonnes, respectively. The United States, together with Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg, stands out as deficit countries in that period (-8.7 and -5.1 million tonnes, respectively). 115. Deacero submitted information from the major exporting countries of the goods classified under subheading 7315.82, noting that the main exporter was China, which accounted for about 88% of total world exports in 2017, percentage that represents that China exported steel chain of welded links 30 times more, by volume, than that exported by the United States, which is the second largest exporting country in the world. 116. For its part, the Secretariat drew on the statistics on world exports and imports, for subheading 7315.82, reported by UN Comtrade for the period 2013 to 2018, since they correspond to the product range more restricted containing thesteel chain of welded links. Data indicate that world exports saw a cumulative increase of 3%, from 311.5 to 319.4 thousand tonnes from 2013 to 2017. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that in 2017, China was the world's largest exporter, as it concentrated 66.9% of the total, followed by Germany with 7.9%, Spain with 5.8%, the Czech Republic with 3.2% and the United States with 2.2%. Mexico ranked 24th with a share of 0.2%.Exports by country of origin to the world through subheading 7315.82PositionCountryVolume in KilogramsPart 2017(%)201320142015201620171china190,491,080201,897,953192,021,061198,158,043213,794,31666.9%2Germany33,252,08129,652,63925,240,51921,057,00225,237,0477.9%3Spain12,621,48421,033,10751,173,36516,564,88818,525,3045.8%4Czech Republic8,415,8468,524,2758,118,8939,968,79510,104,6513.2%5United States10,799,9459,681,9737,376,0076,877,2717,104,1732.2%24Mexico587,803495,418765,155912,055744,9050.2%Rest of the World55,362,16054,350,64657,014,59740,781,62343,845,82713.7%Total General311,530,399325,636,011341,709,597294,319,677319,356,223100%Source: UN Comtrade, Classification: Harmonized System, as reported. 117. For its part, world imports saw a cumulative increase of 93%, from 203.4 to 392.6 thousand tonnes from 2013 to 2017. It was noted that in 2017, the main importers were Slovenia with a 46.9% share of world imports, followed by the United States with 13.3%, Russia with 3.1%, Canada with 2.9%, China with 2.8% and Korea with 2.2%. Mexico ranked 18th with a share of 0.8% of total imports.\par \par \uc2 Imports by country of origin to the world through subheading 7315.82PositionCountryVolume in kilogramsI'm leaving. 2017(%)201320142015201620171Slovenia350,063287,102354,183296,231184,305,05446.9%2United States42,409,01743,035,95547,641,39538,776,32052,076,61413.3%3Russia12,886,35211,650,0119,435,94810,241,33012,029,4203.1%4Canada11,442,26010,731,1778,326,4209,148,92111,504,3312.9%5china8,719,44210,781,7958,303,1395,899,90310,803,6702.8%6Korea7,063,4317,268,6997,243,6968,947,8108,457,0842.2%18Mexico3,188,0803,052,6853,072,5693,264,2032,953,5190.8%Rest of theWorld117,306,993141,303,133145,964,609115,826,101110,438,88528.1%Total General203,365,638228,110,557230,341,959192,400,819392,568,577100.0%Source: UN Comtrade, Classification: Harmonized System, as reported. 3. National Market 118. Deacero and Industrial de Alambres stated that, together with Truper, they are the only national producers of single-link steel chain on the domestic market. 119. They indicated that they make their sales throughout the Mexican territory, mainly through distributors and service centers, the same distribution channels used by foreign suppliers exporting the steel chain of welded links to the country . 120. With regard to the main consumers of the goods, they noted that they are masons, swings manufacturers, fence installers, ranchers, trailers, contractors, mechanical workshops, storage warehouses, among others. They added that the product under review is primarily used as an item to delimit areas, load objects, and move items and vehicles. 121. Deacero and Industrial de Alambres indicated that, on the demand side, its structure remains stable, since the consumer industries are fixed, without new industries claiming the product. In addition, the offer also maintains its structure, noting only that, during the period of compensatory quota, Truper began to produce steel chain of welded links. 122. The Secretariat assessed the behaviour of the domestic market for welded-link steel chain based on the information available in the administrative dossier. To do this, it calculated the ANC from the production and export data calculated by the Secretariat itself based on the information provided by Deacero, Industrial de Alambres and Truper, as well as the import figures obtained from SIC-M for the period analyzed, corroborated by a sample of import pediments and purified by the Secretariat, as outlined in point 135 of this Resolution, and noted that the domestic steel chain market for weldments was trending throughout the period analyzed. 123. Indeed, the domestic welded-link steel chain market, as measured through the ANC, as total domestic production, plus imports, fewer exports, grew 21%, 5%, 13% and 6% in the periods April 2014-March 2015, April 2015 -March 2016, April 2016- March 2017 and in the review period, respectively, so that it accumulated an increase of 52% in the period analyzed. In this sense, the performance of the ANC components was as follows: a. total imports decreased by 41% in the periods April 2014-March 2015 and April 2015-March 2016, and grew 27% and 24% in the periods April 2016-March 2017 and in the period examination period, respectively, so they accumulated a decrease of 46% in the period analyzed. In the same period, total steel chain imports were imported from 20 countries. In the period April 2017-March 2018, the main supplier was the United States, which accounted for 49% of total imports, followed by Taiwan (31%), Germany (5.5%) Spain (5%); b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0domestic production increased by 21%, 4%, 13% and 8% in the periods April 2014-March 2015, April 2015-March 2016, April 2016-March 2017 and in the review period, respectively, so that it recorded a cumulative increase of 54% in the country analyzed riodo, and c.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0total exports decreased by 11% and 26% in the April periods of2014-March 2015 and April 2015-March 2016, respectively, and grew 18% and 48% in the periods April 2016-March 2017 and in the review period, respectively, resulting in a cumulative increase of 16% in the period analyzed. In that period, sales to the external market accounted for on average 5% of domestic production. 124. In addition, domestic market-oriented domestic production (PNOMI) increased during the period analyzed at 57%, resulting from increases of 24%, 6%, 13% and 6% in the periods April 2014-March 2015, April 2015-March 2016, April 2016, 2016-March 2017 and in the review period, respectively. 4. Actual and potential analysis of imports 125. Deacero and Industrial de Alambres stated that, despite a countervailing quota, the product under review entered the Mexican market during the analysis period, suggesting that if the countervailing quota was eliminated, the repayment of unfair practice. They added that the domestic market will remain attractive to exporters because of the expectations of growth in the Mexican economy from the second quarter of 2018 and 2019, as well as trade opening with respect to the product under review. 126. Deacero noted that the domestic steel chain market is characterized by being one of the most open in the world, since during the period of the countervailing quota was imported steel chain of welded links from more than ten countries , including China. 127. For its part, Industrial de Alambres noted that while the countervailing quota was not sufficient to completely eliminate unfair practice, imported volumes were limited by the application of the countervailing measure. 128. In support of their arguments, Deacero and Industrial Dewires through CANACERO provided the value and volume of welded-link steel chain imports, corresponding to tariff fraction 7315.82.02 of THE TIGIE. 129. Deacero and Industrial de Alambres stated that through the tariff fraction through which the product under review enters, other products such as: stainless steel chain, roller chain, plasticized chains, chains with hooks and screws, among others. They also noted that the product under examination is distinguished from other chains by the type of steel, by the inclusion of accessories at its ends and by the types of coatings. 130. In addition, in order to identify import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  relating to the product under review, Deacero submitted a methodology, from which it considered, inter alia, to exclude various import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on account of a range of obtained from the prices of alloy and stainless steel chains, for not corresponding to the steel chain under review. In this regard, the Secretariat required it to provide a methodology that did not consider this criterion, since the price distortion of the product exported to Mexico is one of the variables that is analyzed to determine whether the abolition of the countervailing quota would lead to a continuation or repetition of unfair practice. 131. In response to the request for information from the Secretariat, Deacero provided a methodology by which it selected import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that paid a countervailing fee and which, by their description and the importer's profile, steel chains of welded links. 132. However, it noted that the purification of the import statistics of tariff fraction 7315.82.02 of the TIGIE is unreliable and makes it complex to identify and determine the price of some import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the product under review , because: (i) for thatfraction enter both the chain being examined and other types of strings; (ii) in Mexican customs practice, these products are generally identified with common terms such as "chains", "link chains", among others, and (iii) it is normal for chains that are not the product under review, with a generic description, to pay a fee Compensatory. In this regard, it provided only the quotation of a Chinese producer of steel chains, which contains references to export prices during the review period. 133. For its part, Industrial de Alambres noted that the universe of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  contained in the statistical basis of imports of tariff fraction 7315.82.02 of the TIGIE does not constitute a reasonable basis, as ambiguity is observed in the description of the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  contained in that base. It added that the tariff fraction comprises a variety of products that differ significantly in their physical characteristics and value from the product under review, so the universe of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  identified based on a description general is inconclusive, as it could correspond to any type of string. 134. In order to obtain further detail on import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Secretariat drew aview to import statistics originating in China that entered Mexico for tariff fraction 7315.82.02 of THE TIGIE, which it obtained from SIC-M, as well as from import orders with their accompanying documentation required to various customs agents and information concerning the import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  required of various importers of the product under review. The Secretariat considered the basis of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the SIC-M as the best available information, as set out in point 54 of this Resolution. 135. Because the import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were carried out at atypical implied volumes and prices, or that they paid a countervailing quota chains that are not subject to examination, in order to identify the product examined, the Secretariat adjusted the database of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the SIC-M for the period analysed, with the information it required from customs agents and import ingencies, as referred to in the previous point of this Resolution, taking into account the detailed description and product-specific characteristics, including the chemical and physical composition of the imported product and calculated the value and volume of the subject imports in the period analyzed. 136. Results indicate that total imports decreased 41% in the periods April 2014-March 2015 and April 2015-March 2016, and grew 27% and 24% in the April 2016-March 2017 period syllain , respectively, so that they accumulated a decrease of 46% in the period analyzed. Imports from non-China origins performed similarly, decreasing 35% in the period April 2014-March 2015 and 37% in the period April 2015-March 2016, and grew 27% in the period April 2016-March 2017 and 25% in the period (d) and examination, which resulted in a 35% fall in the period analyzed. 137. For its part, steel chain imports originating in China, recorded the following performance in the period April 2013-March 2018: they decreased 68% in the period April 2014-March 2015 and 92% in the period April 2015-March 2016 , increased 39% in the period April 2016-March 2017 and decreased 78% in the review period, which meant a cumulative decrease of 99% in the period analyzed. It points out that imports of the product under review had decreasing behaviour throughout the period under review, and insignificant volumes were carried out, in particular,during the exam period. 138. In terms of domestic market participation, the Secretariat noted that total imports participated in the ANC with 4.5% in the period April 2013-March 2014, 2.2% in the period April 2014-March 2015, 1.2% in the period April 2015-March 2014, 2.2% in the period April 2015-March 2014 2016, 1.4% in the period April 2016-March 2017 and 1.6% in the period under review. 139. For its part, imports originating in China were little significant in achieving a participation in the ANC of 0.7% in the period April 2013-March 2014, 0.2% in the period April 2014-March 2015, 0.02% in the periods April 2015-March 2016 and 0.2% in the period April 2016-March 2017 and 0.004% in the review period, which is virtually non-existent, which is explained by the presence of the countervailing quota that inhibited the concurrence of subject imports to the domestic market. 140. With regard to imports from other sources, they decreased their share of the ANC by 2.2 percentage points in the period analyzed, from a contribution of 3.8% in the period April 2013-March 2014 to 1.6% in the review period. 141. Deacero and Industrial de Alambres stated that in the immediate future it is foreseeable that, in the face of an intensification of Chinese exports of steel products, mainly Chapter 73, the Mexican link steel chain market soldiers to be receiving significant quantities of the product under review if the compensatory quota is eliminated, displacing domestic production. 142. To support their arguments, Deacero and Industrial de Wire provided projections for the periods April 2018-March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020, of the volume of steel chain imports of welded links from China, in the where the compensatory quota is eliminated, as well as its respective methodology. 143. The Secretariat analysed Deacero and Industrial's proposed methodology for making its projections and noted that in order to project the volume of imports of the product under review for the period April 2018-March 2019, they started from the volume of imports of the product under review that entered Mexico in 2000, reported in the welded-link steel chain anti-dumping investigation, updated with the growth rate of chain imports in Mexico from 2000 to 2017, obtained from UN Comtrade. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that chain imports originating in China reported by UN Comtrade, in considering all \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out through subheading 7315.82, contain uninvestigated product, and therefore required Deacero And Industrial Wires to justify the use of such source. 144. Subsequently, Deacero and Industrial de Alambres proposed to consider the average volume of imports from the import base provided by CANACERO, which, as those companies pointed out, was not feasible because of their poor reliability and difficulty in identifying the product under review, which is why the Secretariat again required these companies to justify the use of such methodology. 145. In view of the foregoing, in its response to the requests for information made by the Secretariat, described in points 30 to 33 of this Resolution, Deacero and Industrial wires acknowledged that they added the growth rate or its average, obtained from the CANACERO import base, the volume of estimated potential imports of the product under review is inapplicable, given its low reliability and difficulty in identifying the product under review, as a result proposed projecting the volume of link steel chain importssoldiers, from the volume of imports of this product that entered Mexico in 2000, reported in the ordinary steel chain investigation of welded links, as these imports were the last to enter under weldment conditions unfair practices of international trade. They updated this volume with Mexico's quarterly GDP growth rate during the period April 2001-March 2018, and it was noted that the volume would be representative of imports originating in China that could enter a scenario where the countervailing quota, as follows: a. for the first projected period, April 2018-March 2019, estimated that China's representative imports would increase to the growth rate recorded in 2000 (42.49%), per considered a transition period, and b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0for the second projected period, April 2019-March 2020, they proposed that it would be 100% of China's representative imports. 146. The Secretariat analysed and assessed the proposed methodology and considered it reasonable to make such projections, as it was based on the level of imports originating in China that was made prior to the application of the countervailing quota. 147. In addition to the above, the Secretariat considered that it was feasible to achieve the volume of steel chain imports originating in China, which Deacero and Industrial de Alambres estimated, taking into account the high export profile, the capacity freely available and China's export potential in relation to the Mexican market, a situation that is supported by the analysis described in points 210 and 211 of this Resolution. 148. In replicating the calculations that Deacero and Industrial de Alambres provided on their estimates to project imports originating in China, in the event of the elimination of the countervailing quota, the Secretariat noted that the imports subject to would reach significant volumes that would allow them to have a 9% stake in the ANC in the Period April 2018-March 2019, while for the period April 2019-March 2020 it would be 22%, after the period under review was little Significant. 149. Based on the information and results of the analysis described above, the Secretariat concluded that there are sufficient evidence to support the elimination of the countervailing quota imposed on steel chain imports originating in China, these would again be brought to the domestic market in considerable volumes and under conditions of price discrimination, as noted in point 104 of this Resolution, which would displace domestic sales and thus achieve a share which would negatively impact the performance of relevant economic and financial indicators in the domestic industry. 5. Real and potential effects on prices 150. Deacero stated that domestic industry prices fell by approximately 15% during the period of the countervailing quota. The performance of these prices arose in an environment in which imports of the product under review entered the national territory at unfair prices and lower than national prices. 151. On the basis of points 128 to 133 of this Resolution, Deacero and Industrial de Alambres stated that the information contained in the base of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered Mexico for tariff fraction 7315.82.02 of the TIGIE, provided by CANACERO, is not a reliable basis for considering the universe of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the product under review, as for that tariff fraction other types of chains are entered that are not the subjectexamination. 152. They provided references to export prices of the product under review for the review period, obtained from the listing of a Chinese producer and exporter. They also submitted price references from other Chinese producers, obtained from the made-in-china.com and penta-transaction.com Internet pages, in order to ascertain the validity and representativeness of those references, as well as prices china's export to third countries during the review period. 153. On the basis of the foregoing, Deacero and Industrial de Alambres proposed that, instead of using China's export price to Mexico, obtained from the base of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that entered Mexico for tariff fraction 7315.82.02 of the TIGIE, provided by CANACERO, shall use the price observed in the price references referred to in the previous point, on the basis that this is information of the product under review. 154. The Secretariat analysed the methodology proposed by Deacero and Industrial wires referred to in the previous point and considered it reasonable, since, in accordance with points 137 and 139 of this Resolution, the imported volume did not transactions involving quantities in which the steel chain is normally marketed on the domestic market, so it is unclear that such import transactions will allow us to observe the price behaviour of the product as described in point 55 of this Resolution. 155. The Secretariat therefore determined to use for the price analysis the prices of quotes submitted by Deacero and Industrial de Alambres, as well as the domestic prices of sales to the domestic market of the domestic industry. 156. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that during the period of validity of the countervailing quota, the domestic sales price of the domestic industry performed downwards, recording a cumulative decrease of 16% over the period analyzed, with negative growth rates of 4%, 11% and 6% in the periods April 2014-March 2015, April 2015-March 2016, April 2016-March 2018, respectively, and 5% growth in the review period. 157. In order to assess the existence of undervaluation, the Secretariat considered the price placed on the sole of sales to the domestic market of the domestic industry and compared it with the price of the quote of a Chinese producer and exporter referred to point 152 of this Resolution, during the review period, adjusted for freight and insurance, as well as increased costs, customs duty, customs agent fees and the corresponding countervailing fee, as a result it was observed that the price of China's quotes were 10% below the domestic price. In this regard, Deacero's argument that imports of the product under examination of Chinese origin entered the domestic territory at prices below domestic prices are confirmed. 158. Deacero and Industrial de Alambres argued that the elimination of the countervailing quota would cause the product under review to become an attractive offer for importers, as such a product would compete with an even lower price on the market which is estimated to continue below the price of domestic goods, to the detriment of the price of the domestic steel chain industry of welded links. 159. To support the above, Deacero and Industrial de Alambres presented estimates for the periods April 2018-March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020 on the prices of steel chain imports fromweldments originating in China, as well as their sales prices to the domestic market, in the scenario where the countervailing quota is eliminated, as a result of the following: a. to estimate the price of the subject imports in the period April 2018-March 2019, they submitted import price information for the product under review to 6 Latin American countries, noted their trend during the period analyzed, and applied it to the price obtained from the price references referred to in point 152 of the Resolution. To obtain prices for the period April 2019-March 2020, they applied the observed growth rate compared to the previous immediate period, and b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0to estimate the domestic sales price of the domestic industry, they applied the undervaluation observed in the review period to the projected price of Chinese origin for each projected period. 160. The Secretariat considered reasonable the methodology that Deacero and Industrial de Wires used to estimate the prices of steel chain imports of welded links originating in China and domestic prices, since these prices are based actual market performance and market trends, underpinned by the margin of undervaluation that prices recorded during the review period. 161. The Secretariat replicated the calculations that Deacero and Industrial de Alambres made for their estimates and noted that the price of steel chain imports of weldments originating in China would decrease by 17% and 19% in the periods April 2018-March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020 with respect to the review period, respectively, ranking 17% below the national price, in both periods. 162. For its part, the domestic sales price of the domestic industry would register a declining trend from April 2018 to March 2020, as it would decrease by 10% and 13% in the periods April 2018 March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020 compared to the examination period, respectively. 163. Based on the available evidence and the analysis described above, the Secretariat concluded that there is a well-founded likelihood that, in the event of the elimination of the countervailing quota, steel chain imports originating in China will enter the market such price levels, which would negatively impact domestic prices on the domestic market, as they could achieve significant levels of undervaluation that would increase demand for new imports, forcing the industry to to lower their prices in order to compete. 6. Actual and potential effects on the domestic industry 164. Deacero asserted that the period of validity of the countervailing quota under consideration has not been sufficient to counter the injury to the domestic industry, as conditions prevailing that place the domestic industry in vulnerable condition. It added that the ana's behaviour, when comparing the period analyzed from start to finish, i.e. the period April 2013-March 2014 against the period April 2017-March 2018, shows an upward trend of 5%, leading to the conclusion that the negative effect indicators of domestic industry, such as domestic sales, installed capacity and production, are not attributable to a decrease in consumption in the market, but can be linked to competition with imports of the product subject to investigation under unfair conditions. 165. For its part, Industrial de Alambres stated that, during the period of the compensatory quota, it operated in a context of shrinking the domestic market, experiencing a deterioration in its economic and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  variables, onall in production and domestic sales, and while it did so with an increase in the price of domestic sales, it lagged for the increase in the cost of sales over the same period, with the consequent financial impact that puts the company in a vulnerable condition in the face of a possible elimination of the compensatory quota. It noted that while the countervailing quota was not sufficient to completely eliminate unfair practice, since during the period of validity, imports of Chinese steel chain at discriminated prices and below their domestic sales prices, imported volumes were limited by the application of the measure. 166. It added that, during the period of the countervailing quota, its volume of production was reduced by 42%, while its sales volume to the domestic market fell by 40%, so that its share of the ANC contracted by 20 percentage points. 167. It also noted that this decrease in production in the period of the countervailing quota involved an increase in the idleness of its production capacity by 20 percentage points at the end of this period, i.e. during the review period, April 2017- March 2018, for the period April 2013-March 2014. 168. In order to assess the conduct of the domestic industry over the period under review, the Secretariat considered the economic and financial indicators provided by Deacero and Industrial de Alambres. For the analysis of financial indicators, they provided dictated financial statements of both companies for the fiscal years 2013 to 2017; the status of costs, sales and profits of similar domestically produced merchandise intended for domestic sales for the periods April 2013-March 2014, April 2014-March 2015, April 2015-March 2016, April 2016-March 2017 and April 2017- March 2018. 169. In order to make financial figures compatible, the Secretariat updated the information considering the method of changes in the overall price level, based on the National Consumer Price Index, published by the National Institute of Statistics and Geography. The analysis of financial statements and income, costs and profits was carried out at the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  level. 170. Based on the information described in the previous points, the Secretariat noted that the volume of welded steel chain production of the domestic industry showed a cumulative decrease of 2% in the period analyzed, while its performance over that period showed 21% growth in the period April 2014-March 2015 and decreased 10%, 5% and 6% in the periods April 2015-March 2016, April 2016-March 2017 and in the review period Respectively. For its part, the volume of PNOMI of the domestic industry grew 0.5% cumulatively in the period analyzed, resulting from growth 24% in the period April 2014-March 2015 and decreasing 9%, 4% and 7% in the periods April 2015-March 2016 , April 2016-March 2017 and in the review period, respectively. 171. In terms of market share, the Secretariat determined to carry out the analysis of PNOMI's participation in the ANC, only in the period From April 2015 to March 2018, that the production company Truper started \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2015. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that in this period the PNOMI of the domestic industry lost 22 percentage points of participation in the ANC (13 and 9 percentage points in the period April 2016-March 2017 and the review period, respectively). Imports originating in China, on the other hand, virtually maintained their market share during the sameimports from other sources grew by one percentage point during the review period. 172. Sales to the domestic market of the domestic industry grew 13% in the period April 2014-March 2015, decreased 6%, 15% and 1% in the periods April 2015-March 2016, April 2016-March 2017 and the review period , respectively, accumulated a decrease of 11% in the period analyzed. 173. Sales to the domestic industry's external market decreased by 11%, 34% and 22% in the periods April 2014-March 2015, April 2015-March 2016 and April 2016-March 2017, respectively, and grew 40% in the review period , which results in a cumulative decrease of 36% for the entire period analyzed. Relatively speaking, the domestic industry's exports accounted for on average 5% of its production in the period analyzed, indicating that it is fundamentally dependent on the domestic market, in which it would compete with imports of the product under conditions of price discrimination. 174. The domestic industry's installed capacity increased by 1%, 3% and 4% in the periods April 2014-March 2015, April 2015-March 2016, and April 2016-March 2017, respectively, and decreased by 1% in the review period. As a result of the performance of installed capacity and production, the use of the first indicator decreased by 5 percentage points in the period analyzed, from 52% in the period April 2013-March 2014 to 47% in the review period. 175. The inventories of the domestic industry showed mixed behaviour, increased 78% in the period April 2014-March 2015, decreased 14% in the period April 2015-March 2016, grew 23% in the period April 2016-March 2017 and decreased by 11% in the review period, so they cumulatively recorded 68% growth in the period analyzed. 176. The employment of the domestic industry increased by 3% in the periods April 2014-March 2015, April 2015-March 2016 and April 2016-March 2017, respectively, and 9% in the review period, which meant a cumulative growth in the 20% in the period analyzed. 177. The productivity of the domestic industry increased by 17% in the period April 2014-March 2015 and decreased by 13%, 8% and 14% in the periods April 2015-March 2016, April 2016-March 2017 and the review period , respectively, which meant a cumulative decrease of 20% in the period analyzed. 178. The behaviour of the domestic industry's wage mass showed the following behaviour: it grew 18% and 4% in the periods April 2014-March 2015 and April 2015-March 2016, respectively, decreased 0.5% in the period April 2016-March of March 2015 2017 and grew 13% in the review period, so cumulatively increased 38% in the period analyzed. 179. The described performance of the domestic sales volumes of the domestic industry and its prices was reflected in the performance of its revenues. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that sales revenue to the domestic market of the domestic industry increased by 11% in the period under review. The behavior of this variable in each of the periods was as follows: they increased 37% in the period April 2014-March 2015 and decreased 3% in the period April 2015-March 2016, 13% in the period April 2016-March 2017 and 4% in the exam period. 180. Meanwhile, operating costs (costs plus operating expenses) increased by 1% in the period analyzed: they increased 2% in the period April 2014-March 2015 and decreased 5% in the period April 2015-March 2016 and 2% in the periodApril 2016-March 2017, while in the review period they increased 6%. 181. Considering the performance of the revenue and operating costs noted above, the Secretariat noted that the operating profits of the domestic industry were positive and the operating margin resulting from market sales domestic recovery during the period analyzed, in this regard: a. the operating profits of the like product of the domestic industry, derived from domestic market-oriented sales, accumulated growth of 177% during the period analyzed: increased 618% in the period April 2014-March 2015 and 6% in the period April 2015-March 2016; they subsequently decreased by 49% in the period April 2016-March 2017 and 72% in the review period, and b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0operating margin accumulated an improvement of 9.6 percentage points, going from -5.7% at the start of the period analyzed and ending at 3.9% at the end of the same period: in the period April 2014-March 2015 it increased from -5.7% to 21.5%, in the period April 2015-March 2016 cr 23.3%, in the period April 2016-March 2017 decreased 9.7 percentage points and was left at 13.6% and in the review period again decreased, to end by 3.9%. 182. The results described in the previous points confirmed that sales revenues to the domestic market grew by 11% during the period analysed, while operating costs increased by 1% during the same period; which was reflected in a 177% improvement in operating results and an increase in operating margin of 9.6 percentage points during the period April 2013-March 2018. 183. With respect to The Return of the Investment in Assets (ROA) variables, cash flow and capital raising capacity, the effects of the subject imports were assessed on the basis of the Deacero and Industrial Wire Sores, which consider the production of the most restricted group or range of products that include the like product, in accordance with Articles 3.6 of the AD Agreement and 66 of the RLCE. 184. With respect to the domestic industry's ROA, calculated at the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  level, from 2013 to 2017 increased by 13 percentage points and changed from 109% to 122%: in 2013 it was 109%, in 2014 from 110%, in 2015 it increased to 113%, in 2016 it again increased to 125% , and in 2017 it declined and ended by 122%. 185. The Secretariat analysed the state of change in the financial situation of the domestic industry and noted that cash flow, as a result of operating activities, increased 46% from 2013 to 2017: it decreased 94% in 2014, increased 496% in 2015, grew 423% in 2016 and diss 18% in 2017. 186. The Secretariat measures a producer's ability to obtain the financial resources necessary to carry out productive activity through solvency, leverage and debt indices. In general, a relationship between current assets and short-term assets is considered appropriate if it is a ratio 1 to 1 or higher. In this case, the domestic industry had adequate solvency and liquidity levels between 2013 and 2017, as the ratio of working assets to short-term liabilities was greater than 1 during the period analyzed; the same situation was observed as far as the acid test was concerned. The observed data are reported below: a. the current ratio (ratio between current assets and short-term liabilities) was 2.11 in 2013, 2.33 in 2014, 2.07 in 2015, 1.52 in 2016 and 2.79 times in 2017, and b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0acid test (circulating asset minus the value of inventories, relative to short-term liabilities) or reason for fast-performing assets recorded1.43 in 2013, 1.85 in 2014, 1.39 in 2015, 1.03 in 2016 and 1.96 in 2017. 187. As regards the level of leverage, a share of total liability to book capital less than 100% is considered to be a manageable indebtedness level. The liability-to-book capital ratio recorded levels above this percentage throughout the 2013-2017 period, so the domestic industry's leverage was considered to be high during that period; whereas, as regards the total liability ratio for total assets or debt, it recorded levels below 100% during the same period, so this ratio was deemed to have maintained adequate levels. The results of the leverage ratios were as follows: a. the total liability to book capital was 201% in 2013, 230% in 2014, 180% in 2015, 176% in 2016 and 185% in 2017, and b. the total liability relative to total assets recorded levels of 67% in 2013, 70% in 2014, 64% in 2015, 64% in 2016 and 65% in 2017. 188. Based on the analysis and results described in the previous points, the Secretariat noted that, in the period under review, some of the domestic industry's economic indicators performed negatively, such as production, sales to the productivity and utilization of installed capacity, as well as increased inventories. As for financial indicators, although they showed positive performance over the period analyzed, the following variables performed negatively in the review period, domestic sales revenue, operating profit and margin Operating. In addition, during the period 2013 to 2017, the Secretariat noted that while the domestic industry recorded adequate levels in its solvency to meet its short-term obligations, there was a high level of indebtedness that compromised the branch investment. Therefore, the Secretariat considered that the state submitted by the domestic industry is vulnerable, because, in the face of a declining domestic sales price context and the presence of undercutting margins in the product under review during the review period, the domestic industry faces a deteriorating situation, so the Secretariat determined that in the event of a knockout of the countervailing quota imposed on steel chain imports originating in China, the the deterioration that it presents. 189. Deacero noted that, if the countervailing quota under consideration is eliminated, the product under review will enter the domestic market at unfair and lower prices than it maintained during the review period, having a damaging effect on the volume and price of sales from the domestic industry to the Mexican market, as well as in its volume of production, which would affect its other economic indicators. 190. It estimated that the volume of sales of the domestic industry to the Mexican market will decline by more than 9% and 19% in the periods April 2018-March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020, respectively, with respect to the volume it had during the review period , as a result of the entry and increase in imports of the product under review. 191. He added that, due to the fall in the volume of sales of the domestic industry to the Mexican market, its production volume would also be affected, as it would have a decrease of more than 26% and 35% in the periods April 2018-March 2019 and April 2019- March 2020, compared to the volume it had during the review period and the first projected period, respectively. 192. For its part, Industria de Alambres noted that it has directed its production significantly to the domestic market, which increases its degree ofvulnerability to the eventual massive return of Chinese chain imports at unfair prices, given the minimum leeway to mitigate any injury caused by such imports. 193. It noted that the abolition of the countervailing quota will lead to an accelerated deterioration in its fragile \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  circumstance, as it was exposed to intense unfair competition against Chinese goods, which is estimated to enter at reduced prices and with margins of undervaluation with respect to the domestic prices it offers, which will displace domestic production in a precarious market environment and a low-price sensitive industry of the product under review. 194. It stated that the sudden presence of Chinese imports on the domestic market would have a negative impact on indicators such as sales, production, participation in the ANC, utilization of installed capacity, employment and sales prices to the domestic market. It warned that reductions in their sales prices would involve offering sales prices to the domestic market below the level of unit production costs, bound by the prices of Chinese imports in the absence of the countervailing quota in both projected periods, with the consequent financial impacts. 195. To support their arguments, Deacero and Industrial de Alambres provided projections of the economic indicators of the national steel chain industry of weldments, as well as their main economic and financial indicators, in a scenario considering the elimination of the countervailing quota for the periods April 2018-March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020. They also explained that, in order to make industry estimates of production indicators, sales to the domestic and external market and installed capacity, they used the historical information of the above-mentioned indicators and applied their respective participation that they recorded during the period under review, for each of the projected periods. 196. In addition, Deacero and Industria de Alambres presented a methodology for estimating their relevant economic indicators in the periods April 2018-March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020, in the scenario that considers the elimination of the quota compensatory and described below: a. domestic sales: for the period April 2018-March 2019, the average annual growth rate was calculated from April 2013-March 2014 to April 2017-March 2018 and applied to domestic sales of the review period, they were subtracted from the volume of imports affected by projected Chinese imports from the same period. For the period April 2019-March 2020, the same methodology was repeated, but calculating the average growth rate from April 2014-March 2015 to April 2018-March 2019 and applying the rate to the data obtained in the first projection; (b) sales to the external market: (a) sales to the external market of the review period, the average annual growth rate was applied for each of the projected periods, as described in the previous point, without eliminating the affected imports; c. Inventories: An inventory rotation (total domestic industry sales volume among final inventories) was first calculated for each period of the period analyzed and the average of these was calculated for the entire period analyzed, in order to project final inventories for the periods to be estimated; d. production: Average inventory turnover was multiplied by the total sales projected in each period to obtain the projected final inventories for each period. These final inventories were subtracted from the initial inventory and the total projected sales were added to eachperiod to calculate estimated production. This, taking into account that the final inventories for the second period to be projected, would be the initials of the previous projected period; (e) employment: estimated to be projected production among projected productivity for both projected periods; (f) wage mass: the average annual growth rate observed by direct labour in the period analyzed was calculated and applied to the wage mass per employee of the review period; finally, it was multiplied by the number of employees for the projected periods, and g.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0installed capacity: remained constant. 197. The Secretariat analysed the methodologies referred to in the previous point to project the main economic indicators of the domestic industry. In this regard, it determined that these methodologies are economically reasonable, on the basis that the economic indicators are based primarily on the historical performance they recorded during the period analyzed. 198. In replicating Deacero and Industrial De Alambres' projections, the Secretariat noted that by comparing the projections of the domestic industry for the periods April 2018-March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020, with relative to the levels they recorded during the review period, they would reflect the following impacts on the relevant economic indicators of the branch: a. in the period April 2018-March 2019, the main decreases would be recorded in production (25.6 %), PNOMI (26.3%), market share (11 percentage points), domestic sales (12%), exports (11%), employment (38%), wages (32%) utilization of installed capacity (12 percentage points), and b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0in the period April 2019-March 2020, decreases in production (39%), PNOMI (40%), domestic market share (19 percentage points), domestic sales (30%), exports (20%), employment (50%), wages (34%) utilization of installed capacity (18 percentage points). 199. In addition, Deacero and Industrial de Alambres stated that, in the face of the elimination of the countervailing quota, they would expect a decrease in their revenue from sales to the Mexican market and a reduction in their operating profits, up to due to the effect on its sales volume on the domestic market and the decrease in the selling price to maintain its market share. 200. In order to demonstrate the financial impacts of the domestic industry in eliminating the current countervailing quota, Deacero and Industrial de Alambres presented projections of their cost statement, sales and profits of domestic merchandise oriented to the domestic market for the periods April 2018-March 2019 and April 2019-March 2020 in which they considered two scenarios: one in which the application of the current countervailing quota continues and the other in which the elimination is assumed of it. 201. The Secretariat replicated the calculations made by Deacero and Industrial de Alambres and noted that the projections of the indicators included in the state of costs, sales and profits of the domestic industry were based on the data recorded during the review period, as described below: a. Manufacturing costs were obtained from applying the average annual growth rate (TMCA) to the unit value of the manufacturing cost for the period under review, the value of that indication was estimated period April 2018March 2019 and, then for the period April 2019-March 2020. The result was multiplied by the projected production for each of the periods, respectively; b. sales to the domestic market(value) resulted from multiplying the volume of sales on the domestic market, taking into view the impact on the return of the imports at issue in this examination, by the price of the adjusted domestic industry, in order to follow the price of the subject imports and not lose market share, and c.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0costs of sale and administration costs: from applying the TMCA to the unit value of operating expenses, the value of this indicator was estimated for the period April 2018-March 2019 and then for the period April 2019-March 2020. 202. On the basis of the above, the Secretariat considered the methodology and projections provided by the domestic industry reasonable, since it was able to ascertain that these were obtained from the figures observed during the period analyzed. 203. The Secretariat noted that the prospective results of the financial variables presented by Deacero and Industrial de Alambres referred that, if the countervailing quota were eliminated, the good performance experienced during the period analyzed would be in because the trend of these indicators would change their direction and the decline they projected in both the domestic price and the volume of sales, as a result of the imminent entry into the domestic market of the subject imports, would have as resulting in a decrease in domestic sales revenue of 23% in the period April 2018-March 2019, a period they considered transitional, and 44% in the period April 2019-March 2020, a period in which they argued that the definitive effect of the imports examined. 204. At the same time, operating costs would decrease by 20% in the period April 2018-March 2019 and 32% in the period April 2019-March 2020; this would result in a decrease in operating profit in the first period of 98% and 323% in the period April 2019-March 2020. In the same vein, the operating margin would be a 3.8 percentage point decline in the period April 2018-March 2019 and would end with a margin of -15.5%; this would mean a deterioration in operating margin of 19.4 percentage points, which would fall from 3.9% in the period under review to -15.5% in the period April 2019-March 2020. 205. According to projections provided by Deacero and Industriade de Alambres, the Secretariat determined that these companies provided sufficient evidence to demonstrate that the elimination of the current countervailing quota could mean the deterioration of financial indicators of the domestic industry, such as domestic sales revenue, which by the period April 2019-March 2020 would decrease by 44%; operating results, which would decrease by 323%, which would mean trading at losses, and operating margin, which would decrease by 19.4 percentage points and end by -15.5%, which could pose a risk to its financial viability. 206. Based on the information and evidence presented, as well as the analysis in the previous points, the Secretariat concluded that the potential volume of imports originating in China, as well as the level of prices at which they would attend, constitute objective elements that make it possible to establish the well-founded likelihood that, in the face of the elimination of the countervailing quota, the domestic industry of the like product would have negative effects on the relevant economic and financial indicators, which would lead to a repeat of the damage to the domestic welded-link steel chain industry. 7. China's export potential 207. Considering point 110 of this Resolution, Deacero and Industrial De Alambres noted that there are factsrevealing that the steel chain production industry of welded links in China will continue with a large productive and export capacity, given the new investments in its production and marketing structure. This, as a result of: (a) China ranks first in terms of globally welded-ended steel chain export volumes, its exports accounted for 88% of world exports, from 2013 to 201 7, with a growth rate of 12% over the same period, according to Trade Map statistics, taking into account only subheading 7315.82, for which the product under review enters; b. the great export potential of Chinese industry is fostered by its immense productive capacity, which is seen by considering only 5 companies that were identified as producers of the product under review, of which their ability to production is significantly higher than the productive capacity of the domestic industry; c. China's high production capacity and exporter quality have forced its industry to place its products in external markets at exceptionally low prices. Chinese welded-sided steel chains supplied 84% on average of global demand during the period of the countervailing quota, and d.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0there are new exporting companies of the product under review that were not considered in the anti-dumping investigation. 208. They also noted that anti-dumping duties were imposed in Colombia and Turkey on steel chain imports of welded links of Chinese origin, which is strong evidence that China continues unfair conduct in the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of export of the product under review. 209. For its part, Deacero argued that the Mexican market will continue to be important for steel chain exports of welded links from China because: a. global chain consumption is declining according to statistics export, so it is clear that China is redirecting its production volume to other markets, particularly those that are open and have higher growth expectations, including Mexico; b. Mexico has ranked among the top 12 steel chain importing countries of welded links. Statistics of Chinese exports to the world show that those oriented to Mexico under subheading 7315.82 doubled from start to end of the review period, and (c).\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0China's welded-link steel chain industry is interested in placing its products in the NAFTA region markets, showing 40% growth in Chinese exports to the U.S. market and 13% to Canadian comparing the exam period with the previous immediate. In contrast, Chinese exports of this product worldwide decreased by 3% over the same period. 210. According to the information described in the previous points, which constitutes the best available information, the Secretariat noted the following: a. in the period of review, the production of steel wire rod from China was 136.9 million tonnes, while its installed capacity to manufacture it stood at 160.5 million tonnes, and China was noted to have recorded a freely available level of capacity of 23.6 million tonnes; b. in the period 2013 to 2017, according to cru statistics, China ranked as the world's leading producer and consumer of steel wire rod, its cumulative production and consumption, over the entire period, was 729.5 and 683.7 million tons, respectively, thataccounted for 67% and 64% of the world's production and consumption, respectively; c. When balancing production and consumption in China from 2013 to 2017, a surplus of 45.8 million tonnes of steel wire rod results in, showing that China has no limitations on the availability of the main input to manufacture the and, if applicable, increase its exports to Mexico, by eliminating the countervailing quota, and (d).\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0the production figures of only 5 Chinese steel wire producers show that they have the capacity to produce more than 20 times the Mexican ANC that was reported during the review period and this volume is more than 26 times higher than those that were exports China's steel wire rod made to the Mexican market from 2013 to 2017. 211. With regard to China's export potential and as described in point 116 of this Resolution, the Secretariat drew on statistics obtained from UN Comtrade on world exports, conducted through subheading 7315.82, and noted that China it was the largest exporter, with a 63% share of total exports worldwide during the period 2013 to 2017 and its exports increased 12% from 190.5 to 213.8 thousand tonnes. The latter volume is equivalent to more than 30 and 29 times the size of the ANC and domestic production during the review period, respectively. 212. The results described in the previous points support China's significantly higher export potential relative to the domestic market. These asymmetries provide elements that support that their export potential could be significant for Mexican production and market. 213. Furthermore, Deacero and Industria de Alambres argued that the Mexican market is a real destination for steel chain exports if the countervailing quota is eliminated, taking into account that Colombia and Turkey imposed anti-dumping duties on Steel chain imports of welded links of Chinese origin, which underpins china's continued unfair conduct in the export \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the product under review. 214. The Secretariat considered that the continuation of the trade measures referred to in the previous point made it possible to presume that Chinese exporters would have an incentive to place their exports in other countries, such as the Mexican market. 215. Based on the information and analysis described above, the Secretariat concluded that China's welded steel chain manufacturing industry has considerable export potential relative to domestic production and size Mexican's like product market. This, in addition to the low prices due to the conditions of price discrimination in which they would enter the domestic market, constitute elements to consider that, in the event of the elimination of the countervailing quota, it would encourage the income of significant volumes of China's exports to the Mexican market that would result in a recurrence of injury to the domestic industry. G.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Conclusion 216. Based on the analysis and results described in this Resolution, the Secretariat concluded that there are sufficient elements to determine that the elimination of the countervailing quota imposed on steel chain imports of welded links originating in China, would lead to a repeat of unfair practice. Among the elements that led to this conclusion, without being limited to aspects identified throughout this Resolution, are the following: a. There are sufficient elements to support the elimination of the compensatory quota Repeatdumping in Mexico's exports of steel chain of welded links originating in China. b. However, during the review period, the application of the countervailing quota desincentivised the presence of significant volumes of steel chain imports of welded links from China, the projection of the subject imports before the elimination of the countervailing quota confirms the well-founded likelihood that the countervailing quota would be re-accounted for on the domestic market in considerable volumes. c. The prices of potential steel chain exports of welded links originating in China, placed on the domestic market, could achieve significant undercutting margins relative to domestic prices of 17%, which would have a significant impact on negatively in domestic prices, since it would force the domestic industry to lower them, in order to compete to remain in the market in the face of increased demand for new imports. d. Given the prices at which steel chain imports of welded links originating in China would be met, it is expected that they would distort domestic prices and significantly displace the domestic product from the market, which would adversely affect the performance of relevant economic and financial indicators in the domestic industry. e. In the period under review, some of the domestic industry's economic indicators showed negative signs, such as declines in prices, production, domestic sales, productivity and utilization of installed capacity, as well as growth in inventories. Therefore, the Secretariat considered that the state of the domestic industry in the period under review was vulnerable to the elimination of the countervailing quota. f. Among the most significant impacts on the domestic industry that would cause the elimination of the countervailing quota in the projected period April 2018-March 2019, with respect to the levels recorded during the review period, are decreases in production (25.6%), PNOMI (26.3%), market share (11 percentage points), domestic sales (12%), domestic sales prices (10%), employment (38%), wages (32%), utilization of installed capacity (12 percentage points), income sales to the domestic market (23%), operating profit (98%) operating margin (3.8 percentage points). The negative impact on the indicatorsdescribed would continue and would be aggravated in the period April 2019-March 2020 with respect to the performance of the domestic industry during the review period. G.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0China has significant export potential and considerable freely available capacity relative to the size of the Mexican welded-link steel chain market. h. Derived from China's welded link steel chain exports facing restrictions in some markets due to anti-dumping averages (Colombia and Turkey) Chinese exporters would have an incentive to place their exports in other markets countries, as would the Mexican market. 217. For the foregoing, on the basis of Articles 11.1 and 11.3 of the AD Agreement, and 67, 70 fraction II and 89 F fraction IV literal (a) of the LCE, the following RESOLUTION 218 is issued. The procedure for reviewing the countervailing quota imposed on imports, final and temporary, including those entering under the eighth of the supplementary rule for the application of the steel chain TIGIE, is declared concluded. welded links originating in China, regardless of the country of origin, entering by tariff fraction 7315.82.02 of the TIGIE or by anyAnother. 219. The compensatory quota referred to in points 2 and 3 of this Resolution is extended for a further five years from 18 July 2018. 220. It is for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Public Credit to apply the final countervailing quota referred to in points 2 and 3 of this Resolution throughout the national territory. 221. Pursuant to Article 66 of the LCE, importers who, under that resolution, are required to pay the compensatory fee, shall not be obliged to pay the compensation of the countervailing fee if they find that the country of origin of the goods is different from China. The verification of the origin of the good shall be carried out in accordance with the provisions of the Agreement laying down the rules for the determination of the country of origin of the imported goods and the provisions for their certification, for non-preferential purposes (previously Agreement laying down the rules for the determination of the country of origin of imported goods and the provisions for their certification, relating to countervailing quotas) published in the DOF on 30 August 1994, and their amendments published in the DOF on 30 August 1994, and their amendments published in 11 November 1996, 12 October 1998, 30 July 1999, 30 June 2000, 1 and 23 March 2001, 29 June 2001, 6 September 2002, 30 May 2003 , 14 July 2004, 19 May 2005, 17 July 2008 and 16 October 2008. 222. Notify the interested parties of the interested parties of their knowledge. 223. Please communicate this Resolution to the SAT for legal purposes. 224. This Resolution shall enter into force on the day following its publication in the DOF. 225. Archive as a total and definitively completed case. Mexico City, July 8, 2019.- The Secretary of Economy, Graciela M\u225\'00\'E1rquez Col\u237\'00\'EDn.- Rubric.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190718ef7h0008h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
