\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR: China Likely To Tolerate Exchange Rate Flexibility\b0\par\par\uc2 1,424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2019\par 09:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 25 Jun 2019 03:14 ET  Press Release: S&PGR: China Likely To Tolerate Exchange Rate Flexibility\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par SINGAPORE (S&P Global Ratings) June 25, 2019--China is likely to tolerate \par greater renminbi flexibility if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  faces depreciation pressures due \par to ongoing U.S.-China trade tensions, said S&P Global Ratings in a report \par published today, titled "APAC Economic Snapshots--June 2019". \par  \par The renminbi may face depreciation pressures for two reasons. First, China is \par losing competitiveness as a result of higher U.S. tariffs on its exports. \par Second, over the long run, slower technology transfer resulting from \par U.S.-China friction could erode China's productivity advantage over its \par trading partners. This would also weigh on the renminbi's valuation. \par  \par S&P Global Ratings does not believe that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . If anything, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been "leaning against the wind" to \par slow the pace of renminbi depreciation and has managed to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par fairly stable on a trade-weighted basis. \par  \par Preventing the renminbi from moving with fundamentals may be a short-term \par palliative. But this can impose heavy costs including an overvalued exchange \par rate that weighs on exports, and capital controls that can cause distortions \par in the financial system. \par  \par "The economic costs of tightly managing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are substantial \par especially when the fundamentals are changing," said Shaun Roache, S&P Global \par Ratings' Asia-Pacific chief economist. "We think that the economics will trump \par the politics of the exchange rate and China will tolerate renminbi flexibility \par versus the U.S. dollar." While the politics remains highly unpredictable, if \par U.S.-China tensions persist or worsen this year, it would mean the exchange \par rate weakening beyond 7. \par  \par This could mean weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  across Asia-Pacific due to an emerging "RMB \par bloc". The renminbi's influence has risen, particularly since 2015, across \par many economies, emerging and developed. \par  \par Recent economic data confirmed a weak start to the year in Asia-Pacific. \par Australia and India both had first quarter GDP growth below expectations and \par central banks in both countries cut policy interest rates by 25 basis points. \par  \par Policy stimulus in China is still feeding through and financial conditions \par remain supportive. Manufacturing activity remains a weak point, with \par investment growth below 3% year over year while the employment component of \par the purchasing managers' index declined steadily to 47. \par  \par "The policy easing signaled by our financial conditions index suggests China's \par economy is on track to meet our 6.2% growth forecast for 2019," Mr. Roache \par said. "While we do not expect any further substantial stimulus unless momentum \par turns negative, the cumulative easing underway is piling up." \par  \par This report does not constitute a rating action. \par  \par The report is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.capitaliq.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.capitaliq.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par If you are not a RatingsDirect subscriber, you may purchase a copy of the \par report by calling (1) 212-438-7280 or sending an e-mail to \par research_request@spglobal.com. Ratings information can also be found on S&P \par Global Ratings' public website by using the Ratings search box located in the \par left column at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Members of the media may request a \par copy of this report by contacting the media representative provided. \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , New York \par Asia-Pacific Chief Economist: Shaun Roache, Singapore (65) 6597-6137; \par                               shaun.roache@spglobal.com \par Asia-Pacific Economist : Vincent R Conti, Singapore +  65 6216 1188; \par                         vincent.conti@spglobal.com \par                         Vishrut Rana, Singapore (65) 6216-1008; \par                         vishrut.rana@spglobal.com \par Media Contacts: Richard J Noonan, Melbourne (61) 3-9631-2152; \par                 richard.noonan@spglobal.com \par                 Chris Davis, Hong Kong (852) 2533-3511; \par                 chris.g.davis@spglobal.com \par  \par  \par No content (including ratings, credit-related analyses and data, valuations, \par model, software, or other application or output therefrom) or any part thereof \par (Content) may be modified, reverse engineered, reproduced, or distributed in \par any form by any means, or stored in a database or retrieval system, without \par the prior written permission of Standard & Poor's Financial Services LLC  or \par its affiliates (collectively, S&P). The Content shall not be used for any \par unlawful or unauthorized purposes. S&P and any third-party providers, as well \par as their directors, officers, shareholders, employees, or