\par\par \b \uc2 Don't Pick a Fight With the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  -- Heard on the Street\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Bird \par \uc2 477 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2019\par 08:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Folk wisdom suggests you shouldn't start an argument with someone who buys ink by the barrel. Investors should think twice before making bets against someone with several trillion dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 That's the lesson for speculators shorting the Chinese yuan this year. Despite escalating trade tensions leading to a steep tariff hike on $200 billion in Chinese goods in May, and a weak Chinese economy, the yuan is roughly flat for the year at 6.87 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Luckily there are other good options for taking a negative view on the yuan--for example, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China's neighbor directly east.\par \par \uc2 Why haven't direct shorts worked so far? For one, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  has supported the yuan by issuing bills in Hong Kong, hoovering up some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  circulating offshore. That in turn raises short-term interest rates, making it more expensive for foreigners to bet against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  issued 30 billion yuan ($4.37 billion) in yuan-denominated bills last week.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, overnight yuan borrowing rates in Hong Kong were 1.5 percentage points more than the onshore, Shanghai equivalent, the steepest gap since October last year.\par \par \uc2 Even if the natural place for the Chinese yuan is past seven to the dollar, a line the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has previously defended, timing the move requires interpreting China's murky politics. How much of its $3.101 trillion reserves will the government burn through to defend the yuan? How tight are capital controls really? Investors without a good answer should avoid taking on the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The good news is there are other options: most obviously the South Korean won. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is closely correlated with the yuan, particularly in times of stress. China accounts for about a fifth of Korea's exports and a third of its imports.\par \par \uc2 The won will probably depreciate with the Chinese yuan if and when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breaks through the symbolic seven line. But is also likely to decline in periods during which the yuan would have fallen sharply if it were freely traded, but doesn't because of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's capital controls or \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The won's sharp fall in April and May left it down by about 7% against the dollar for the year, while the yuan was down just around 1%. Like the yuan, it has since rallied, but is still down by nearly 4% for the year.\par \par \uc2 So if you're going to pick a fight with a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, try to start small: battling \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  looks like a safer bet than taking on Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Write to Mike Bird at Mike.Bird@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 25-06-19 0639GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020190625ef6p00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
