\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Gains as Central Banks Shift Policy -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 991 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2019\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Offshore, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases 1% from its 2019 lows after Fed signals rate cut \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Daniel Kruger \f28 \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (June 24, 2019).\par \par \uc2 Shifts toward easier monetary policy by central banks in the U.S. and Europe have reduced some of the downward pressure building on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan has gained 1% from its 2019 lows earlier this month after signs the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  could lower interest rates later this year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also climbed Tuesday after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  signaled that policy makers could cut interest rates and resume bond purchases.\par \par \uc2 China's yuan is watched closely by investors because previous weakness has coincided with periods of stress in the global economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell 3% in the weeks after President Trump surprised Chinese officials last month by threatening on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to drastically increase tariffs, leading them to consider canceling further talks. A weaker yuan makes Chinese exports more competitive abroad, increasing the country's trade imbalance with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said the yuan's retreat earlier in the year has left it near levels that could force officials at the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  to act, especially as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  nears 7 yuan a dollar. That level is psychologically important not just for markets, but for Chinese citizens, and some fear that breaching it could prompt capital flight that would be bad for the economy.\par \par \uc2 One factor that could ease pressure on policy makers: easy-money policies from the Fed could drag the dollar from its recent two-year high. China has more than $3 trillion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, including $1.1 trillion of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds -- the largest stake among foreign investors. And the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been lifted by interest rates that are higher than those in other developed markets and the dollar's status as a haven in times of market stress.\par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped Wednesday after the Fed held interest rates steady and signaled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could lower rates if the economic outlook worsens.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of what happens to the [yuan] is up to the dollar," said Mark Haefele, chairman of the UBS Global Investment Committee and chief investment officer at UBS Wealth Management.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is trading at about 6.86 a dollar. Some analysts have said that a fall below 7 a dollar could prompt Chinese officials to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollar-denominated assets. But the level of the yuan versus the dollar may be less important than the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  watched by Chinese officials, where it currently trades near the bottom of their preferred range.\par \par \uc2 Pegging the yuan to a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of the dollar allows Chinese officials to adjust policy based on the strength of the global economy, rather than just U.S. growth, some analysts said.\par \par \uc2 This year's weakness in the yuan has exacerbated trade tensions, drawing the ire of Mr. Trump, who has complained that it gives China an unfair advantage against the U.S. Many analysts say that Chinese policy makers have an incentive to stabilize the yuan's value to avoid an additional escalation of the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Tuesday that he will have "an extended meeting" with China's President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  next week on the sidelines of the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  summit of leading global economies meeting in Osaka, Japan.\par \par \uc2 While China's economy is decelerating, it is still growing at an annual pace above 6%. That means the fundamentals don't justify an excessive devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said \uc2 Yu Yongding\uc2 , a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser and member of Chinese Economists 50 Forum, a think tank that advises policy makers.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the value of the yuan," Mr. Yu said. "As long as economic fundamentals are good, we don't need to worry," he said.\par \par \uc2 Several investors and analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely because it could entail a significant sale of China's \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bondholdings. Because China owns about 7% of outstanding Treasurys, a large sale could undermine the value of its remaining holdings.\par \par \uc2 "Chinese reserve managers are under enormous political pressure to manage the people's money well" and not incur losses, said David Loevinger, who oversees emerging market bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 TCW Group\uc2 . Before joining TCW in 2012, he was senior coordinator for China policy at the Treasury Department.\par \par \uc2 A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be an effective tool for Chinese officials to support its manufacturing industries during a time of trade frictions, other analysts said. A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could undercut some of the added costs of tariffs to U.S. consumers while making China's exports more competitive against other countries.\par \par \uc2 Should talks between Mr. Trump and Mr. Xi prove counterproductive, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could resume its decline as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reduces interest rates and expectations for economic growth in China and around the world fade, said \uc2 Alan Ruskin\uc2 , chief international strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "They're going to need a lower \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is still likely to face pressure if the U.S. and China resolve their differences, some analysts said. One risk stems from a sluggish economy in Europe, one of China's major export markets, which could further contribute to the gradual deceleration in growth. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also decline if an aging, more affluent cohort of Chinese citizens starts to save less and consume more imported goods, leading to increased demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of nations exporting to China.\par \par \uc2 Chao Deng contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Daniel Kruger at Daniel.Kruger@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 24, 2019 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190624ef6o000bw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
