\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms China Huarong at 'A'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,937 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2019\par 14:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 13 Jun 2019 08:11 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms China Huarong at 'A'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -Hong Kong/Taipei-13 June 2019: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 China Huarong Asset Management Co., Ltd\uc2  .'s Long-Term Foreign- and Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Ratings (IDR) at 'A'. The Outlook is Stable. A full list of rating actions is at the end of this commentary.\par \par \uc2 China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  is one of four asset-management companies (AMC) established by the State Council to resolve the non-performing assets (NPA) of major banks. The company's core business is distressed asset management (DAM), which involves acquiring NPAs and employing strategies, such as disposals and restructuring, to recover the assets.\par \par \uc2 KEY RATING DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 - 'Very Strong' Status, Ownership and Control: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes the strength of the state's ownership and control of China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  indicates high proximity of China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  with its sponsor, despite the company being a listed entity. China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  is 63.36% owned by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The government demonstrated its high control by appointing the company's new chairperson and president and has played a key role in the company's strategy to refocus on its core business and de-risking initiatives.\par \par \uc2 The company's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are regulated by the China Banking and Insurance Regulatory Commission, including the maintenance of core ratios. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes the high regulatory barrier to entry is supportive of the four AMCs remaining dominant in the acquisition of bulk and large NPAs.\par \par \uc2 'Moderate' Support Record and Expectations: China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  is financially self-sufficient and has not required \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  support from the state. The lack of a record of recurring support, such as injections and subsidies, constrains our assessment of this attribute. There are no legal, regulatory or policy restrictions on the government providing support to government-related entities (GRE) that are financially distressed. Historically, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has observed the state government's extraordinary support for the four AMCs during financial crises, including a combination of low interest-rate loans and state guarantees.\par \par \uc2 'Strong' Socio-Political Implications of Default: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  regards China \uc2 Huarong\uc2 , along with the three other AMCs, as playing an important policy role in maintaining China's financial stability through the orderly resolution of NPAs and in deleveraging of the economy through the reform of state-owned enterprises. A default of the AMCs may disrupt the government's policy initiatives and thus have some political implications.\par \par \uc2 China \uc2 Huarong\uc2 's role cannot be easily substituted by the private sector considering the regulatory barriers, the company's expertise and the large size of NPAs, which may not be entirely absorbed by provincial-level AMCs. However, the replaceability by one of the three other AMCs refrain us from assessing a 'Very Strong' attribute.\par \par \uc2 'Very Strong' Financial Implications of Default: China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  is one of 27 state-owned financial institutions under the administration of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The company is also among the biggest AMCs by total assets, with more than CNY1 trillion in total borrowing and bonds outstanding at end-2018. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes a financial default of China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  could have serious implications for the credibility of the state and the availability of funding for other state-level GREs. This, in turn, could pose systemic risk to the financial system.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  does not differentiate the support score and hence our expectation of extraordinary state support between China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  and the three other state-owned AMCs, which play the same policy role. In the event of a default, potential contagion risk to the remaining three AMCs also underpins the 'Very Strong' assessment.\par \par \uc2 Refocus on Policy Mission: China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  is into the second year of its strategy to refocus on its core business of distressed asset management and to gradually exit non-core segments. The company's total assets declined in 2018 for the first time since its IPO, falling by 8.6% yoy to CNY1.7 trillion on weakness in the asset management and investment segment.\par \par \uc2 China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  faces inherent risks due to its distressed-asset management, as its asset quality may be susceptible to impairment in the event of a financial downturn. This could stress its capital adequacy and liquidity. However, the company's diverse funding sources and our expectations of support from major banks should mitigate these risks.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes a failure of AMCs would most likely be triggered by a significant deterioration in asset quality, which may have wider implications for the health of the financial system. An AMCs' ability to absorb the banking system's distressed assets in the event of a financial crisis would be limited without government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DERIVATION SUMMARY\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Fitch\uc2  classifies \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 Huarong\uc2  as a GRE, having factored in the state-level majority ownership, a high level of government control and the company's important policy role in supporting the stability of China's financial system. Hence, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes there is a strong incentive for the government to provide extraordinary support, if needed. The lack of explicit government support was the major constraint against a higher support score.\par \par \uc2 The medium-term note programme under Huarong Finance 2019 Co., Ltd. and senior unsecured notes under the programme are rated at the same level as China \uc2 Huarong\uc2 's IDR. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  regards China \uc2 Huarong\uc2  as having the ultimate responsibility of payment and expects the company to provide support to Huarong International Holdings Limited, which is the group's key offshore financial platform, under the keepwell deed and deed of equity interest purchase, investment and liquidity support undertakings.\par \par \uc2 The ratings of the perpetual securities is notched down once from China \uc2 Huarong\uc2 's IDR of 'A' to reflect that, in a stress scenario, the company may decide to prioritise senior unsecured debt even though the securities are unsubordinated.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 - A change in the sovereign ratings could result in a similar change in China \uc2 Huarong\uc2 's rating.\par \par \uc2 - Stronger explicit support could lead to the company's ratings being aligned with those of the sovereign. Further dilution of China \uc2 Huarong\uc2 's policy mission of distressed asset management could lead to wider notching.\par \par \uc2 A dilution of the state's shareholding in the entity, leading to a loss of its controlling stake, could result in the AMC no longer being classified as a GRE and therefore being delinked from the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 See \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's commentary, <a href=" {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10038146"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fitch">https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10038146">Fitch }}}\uc2 : China Directive Re-Emphasises Support is Not Uniform for LGREs</a>, published 29 July 2018.\par \par \uc2 The full list of rating actions is as follows:\par \par \uc2 China Huarong Asset Management Co., Ltd\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR affirmed at 'A'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR affirmed at 'A'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 Huarong Finance Co., Ltd.\par \par \uc2 - USD1.2 billion of 4.000% senior unsecured notes due 2019 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 Huarong Finance II Co. Ltd\par \par \uc2 - USD11.00 billion medium-term note programme affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD1.00 billion 2.875% senior unsecured notes due 2019 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD0.50 billion 3.750% senior unsecured notes due 2020 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD1.20 billion 4.500% senior unsecured notes due 2020 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD0.90 billion 3.250% senior unsecured notes due 2021 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD1.35 billion 3.625% senior unsecured notes due 2021 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD0.80 billion 5.000% senior unsecured notes due 2025 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD1.40 billion 5.500% senior unsecured notes due 2025 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD0.90 billion 4.625% senior unsecured notes due 2026 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD0.65 billion 4.875% senior unsecured notes due 2026 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD0.50 billion 2.875% perpetual securities affirmed at 'A-'\par \par \uc2 Huarong Finance 2017 Co., Ltd.\par \par \uc2 - USD11.00 billion medium-term note programme affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD0.50 billion floating-rate notes due 2020 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD1.10 billion 3.375% senior unsecured notes due 2020 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - SGD0.60 billion 3.200% senior unsecured notes due 2021 affirmed at 'A'\par \par \uc2 - USD0.40 b