\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-China's yuan to resist sliding past 7 per dollar despite trade-war pressure\b0\par\par\uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2019\par 05:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/\b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2 -polls?RIC=CNY= poll data\par \par \uc2 By Vivek Mishra and Sumanto Mondal\par \par \uc2 BENGALURU, June 6 (Reuters) - China's yuan is forecast to drift up over the coming year as the monetary authorities keep a tight leash on the partly-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prevent it from falling through the psychological 7 per dollar level any time soon, a Reuters poll showed.\par \par \uc2 However, results from the monthly Reuters poll of 60 institutions taken May 30-June 5 showed a slight increase in the number of forecasters who thought that the 7 mark might yet be crossed as the U.S.-China trade war burns on.\par \par \uc2 The yuan tumbled 2.5% in May, its biggest drop in 10 months, after U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  slapped 25% tariffs on $200 billion of Chinese imports, up from 10% previously.\par \par \uc2 Trump and his Chinese counterpart \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  are expected to make another attempt to find common ground and a potential solution to the trade war when they meet at the G20 summit in Japan later this month.\par \par \uc2 While the yuan's losses last month marked the third time it has approached the 7 per dollar rate, it has not breached that point since the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 To stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling further, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 PBoC\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late last month and kept the daily reference rate stronger than the current 6.9 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is now forecast to gain about 1.5% to 6.80 per dollar in a year.\par \par \uc2 "China fixed the USD/CNY central parity just below 6.90, and its officials warned of huge losses for those who sought to speculate against the Chinese yuan. There is less fear that the Chinese yuan will depreciate past 7 against the USD, for now," said \uc2 Philip Wee\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at DBS.\par \par \uc2 A stable yuan is imperative for China as it seeks to balance its investment-driven economy, the world's second largest, by drawing in more international capital.\par \par \uc2 There is also a risk the onshore equity market could be subject to extreme volatility if the yuan is allowed to slide past the 7 level, similar to what happened in 2015 after the Chinese authorities allowed the exchange rate to trade more freely.\par \par \uc2 That led to heavy capital outflows, triggering a 25% plunge in China's stock market in a year and sparking the highest volatility since the financial crisis of 2008.\par \par \uc2 "We believe that the yuan will not be allowed to touch this level (7 per dollar) as this would create considerable turmoil in onshore asset markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will prevent this from happening," said \uc2 Iris Pang\uc2 , ING economist for Greater \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  always increases when the yuan draws closer to 7 per dollar, as the authorities quietly sell dollars to support it. But in recent times the authorities have become more vocal in their support.\par \par \uc2 The total number of respondents in the June Reuters poll who had a 7 per dollar yuan forecast anywhere on the horizon - one, three, six and 12-month end periods - rose to 11 from just five in the previous survey.\par \par \uc2 Still, that is a significantly lower count than a high of 27 who said so in a poll in October 2018.\par \par \uc2 Of 31 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists in the June poll who answered an additional question, about half said the yuan will not breach the 7 per dollar rate any time soon. But 12 said it would happen in the next 12 months, including one who said within a month.\par \par \uc2 The rest said it would be more than a year from now.\par \par \uc2 When asked about the probability attached to the likelihood of China devaluing the yuan by end-2019, strategists gave a median 22.5% chance.\par \par \uc2 "With U.S.-China relations moving towards divorce it is perhaps no surprise that public trial balloons are being floated by the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  that the magical '7' red line for USD/CNY is not so magic, and that there is little practical difference between 6.99 and 7.10 or 7.20," said Michael Every, senior strategist for Asia Pacific at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "That's completely true provided panic is avoided when we do cross that line - soon - but it's also a signal that the CNY is going to get much weaker ahead."\par \par \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  has one of the most aggressive forecasts for yuan weakness, looking for 7.50 per dollar in 12 months, and 8.50 as a long-run peak for the pair.\par \par \uc2 (Other stories from the global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll: ) (Polling by Khushboo Mittal and Anisha Sheth; Editing by Ross Finley & Shri Navaratnam)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-6-9T03:05:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190606ef66009hq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
