0.4%, Nikkei -1.1%, AsiaxJP -0.5%, 10yr UST yields -2bp 2.246% \u8226\'20\'22 UK service companies "extremely negative" about 2019 outlook \u8226\'20\'22 Queue builds to become the next PM - a poisoned chalice? \u8226\'20\'22 Charts - neutral momentum studies, 5, 10 & 21 DMAs fall - bearish setup \u8226\'20\'22 1.2638, 76.4% 2019 rise proving resilient, break to target 1.2409 2019 low \u8226\'20\'22 1.2605 trend low and this week's 1.2754 high initial support/resistance AUD/USD-Remains resilient despite 'risk-off' mood in Asia \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD holding on to Tuesday's small gains despite easing risk assets \u8226\'20\'22 Dalian iron ore -3.5%; S&P futures -0.35% and AXJ equity index down 0.7% \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD resilience shown in NY trading likely to frustrate bears \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance at the 21-day MA at 0.6945 with sellers tipped ahead of 0.6950 \u8226\'20\'22 Support at 10-day MA at 0.6902 and break would resume downward pressure EUR may replace AUD as proxy for China-US trade war When trade frictions between the U.S. and China started to emerge, resulting in the White House imposing tariffs on Chinese imports in March 2018, the AUD was hit the hardest due to its longstanding status as a proxy for China sentiment. It now appears the EUR might be replacing the AUD in that role, as the negative impact from the U.S.-China trade war on global trade has damaged the German and euro zone economy. Australia has been insulated to a great degree as China's effort to offset the negative impact of the trade war by ramping up infrastructure spending has resulted in increased demand for iron ore, Australia's largest export commodity. Iron ore in AUD terms is approaching all-time highs due mostly to severe supply disruptions in Brazil, but it is also being driven by increased demand out of China. The iron ore price rise has been a boom to the Australian mining sector and this is helping to underpin the AUD. Next UK PM stuck between a rock and a hard place Junior Brexit minister James Cleverly has just joined the queue to become the next UK PM. Boris Johnson remains the favourite, at 7/4 on oddschecker, followed by Michael Gove and Dominic Raab both at 5/1. The strong performance of the Brexit Party and slump in Conservative support in the EU elections, suggest that any new PM will need to appeal to the far right, bringing a no-deal Brexit back into play. Parliament has repeatedly rejected a hard Brexit - it is in fact the only thing lawmakers agree on. The EU stated Tuesday that they will not renegotiate the Brexit deal, so the next UK PM will be in a very difficult position. At the same time, the UK's European election results showed that more people voted to remain in the EU than leave, and opposition leader Corbyn is likely to fully support a second referendum. It is difficult to be a sterling bull given all this uncertainty. GBP/USD charts show neutral daily momentum studies, 5, 10 and 21 DMAs fall, which is a bearish setup; 1.2638, 76.4% of the 2019 rise, is proving resilient, but a sustained break would open the door to a test of the 1.2409 2019 low. Asia c. banks ignore yellow card from US Treasury The US Treasury reached the same conclusion as it did in its October semi-annual report to Congress: that no major trading partners are manipulating their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it highlighted six Asian countries for more scrutiny - China, Japan, South Korea, Malaysia, Singapore, and Vietnam. The report said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been limited over the past year. The crux of the issue is that the US doesn't want trading partners to intentionally devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for export advantages, but in reality, no one's doing that. Instead, Asian central banks have been busy over the past month preventing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling too fast. China's persistent management of the daily USD/CNY benchmark is one example, but also South Korean authorities' suspected selling of USD in the spot market to bolster the KRW. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore has also likely been active recently, keeping USD/SGD rallies in check. Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is well-known to restrain USD/MYR, sometimes through draconian measures involving deployment of monitoring personnel at banks' trading desks. Indeed, the US may be right about \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, but it's clear why they're not about to hand out red cards to Asian central banks - they're all playing on the same side. USD/JPY not poised to bounce yet, trade weighs USD/JPY unlikely to bounce just yet as persistent trade concerns weigh. Despite the goodwill seen during U.S. Pres Trump's recent visit to Japan, bilateral trade issues remain a thorn and might not get resolved till after Japan's Upper House elections. The U.S. Treasury also still sees Japan as a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator alongside a host of other countries. Granted some of these recent U.S. trade-related moves may be part of Trump's attempt to show a strong hand ahead of next year's re-election bid, but they have unnerved most players. Taken in conjunction with more risk-off sentiment as reflected in stock market weakness and sagging US yields, USD/JPY may not see much bounce despite strong bids on dips, and could even see some more downside, perhaps to the 108.50 area, 108.49 the low on January 31.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-6-1T04:42:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190529ef5t00iad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
