\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China's top banking regulator says yuan bears will suffer 'heavy losses'\b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2019\par 11:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes on trade war impact on the economy, background)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, May 25 (Reuters) - China's banking and insurance regulator said on Saturday it did not expect a persistent decline in the yuan and warned speculative short sellers they would suffer "heavy losses" if they bet against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yuan has lost more than 2.5% against the dollar since the festering China-U.S. trade dispute intensified earlier this month. It is now less than a tenth of a yuan away from the 7-per-dollar level authorities have in the past indicated as a floor.\par \par \uc2 "Short-term fluctuation of the yuan exchange rate is normal, but in the long-run, China's economic fundamentals determine that the yuan will not depreciate persistently," Xiao Yuanqi, the spokesman for the China Banking and Insurance Regulatory Commission (CBIRC), told a finance forum in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 "Those who speculate and short the yuan will for sure suffer heavy loss."\par \par \uc2 Xiao was reading from a script prepared for \uc2 Guo Shuqing\uc2 , CBIRC's chairman and the Communist Party chief of the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC)\uc2 . Guo was scheduled to give a speech at the same forum but couldn't make it due to last-minute arrangements.\par \par \uc2 Sources have told Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy tools to stop the yuan weakening past the key 7-per-dollar level in the near-term.\par \par \uc2 A defence of the 7 level could help boost confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and soothe investor fears about the yuan, even as souring trade relations with Washington make competitive devaluation a compelling option for Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Xiao also said Beijing must look out for hot money moving in and out of the country, as well as large amounts of capital flowing into the frothy real estate market.\par \par \uc2 "We must be especially vigilant about money from overseas moving in and out in large quantities, and hot speculative money, and we must resolutely fight bubbles in real estate and financial assets," he said.\par \par \uc2 Chinese policymakers have struggled to manage bubble risks in the property market, the world's largest, without hurting growth in the sector, which is crucial for the wider economy.\par \par \uc2 'LIMITED IMPACT'\par \par \uc2 With talks stalled between Washington and Beijing, and U.S President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  threatening to slap tariffs of up to 25% on all Chinese imports, investors are nervously reassessing risks amid growing fears about their damaging impact on the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Washington slapped higher tariffs on $200 billion in Chinese goods earlier in May, prompting Beijing to retaliate.\par \par \uc2 But Xiao said the impact of additional U.S. tariffs on China's economy, the world's second-largest, would be "very limited" even if the tariffs were levied "to their extreme level".\par \par \uc2 "First of all, most of the (Chinese) products exported to the United States are very suitable for domestic sales. China is in the middle of a 'consumption upgrade', so a huge market with rapid expansion will absorb a large amount of them without 'crowding out' existing consumers," he said.\par \par \uc2 Besides China's vast consumer market, he also cited U.S. importers' willingness to share additional costs, and Beijing's Belt and Road initiative that helps promote trade and investment with the rest of Asia, Europe and beyond as factors that would help cushion any negative shocks.\par \par \uc2 Xiao added he expected further pressure on China's financial market to be "not too big" as it has become more resilient after suffering an "over correction" last year.\par \par \uc2 The United States has recently also ratcheted up tensions with China on the tech front by effectively banning U.S. firms from doing business with \uc2 Huawei Technologies Co Ltd\uc2 , the world's largest telecom network gear maker.\par \par \uc2 Xiao said China was not surprised at such moves, but stressed they would not prevent China from pursuing its own development while some U.S. tech firms are set to suffer lower revenues from such restrictive state policies.\par \par \uc2 "History has long proved and will continue to prove that sanctions and blockades will not prevent a country\u8217\'20\'19s technology and economic development," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Instead, they will inspire its independent research and development and accelerate its own technological progress." (Reporting by Cheng Leng, Yawen Chen and Ryan Woo; Writing by Yawen Chen; Editing by Sam Holmes and Nick Macfie) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-5-25T10:48:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190525ef5p00439\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
