\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stock markets slide as worries about \uc2 Huawei\uc2  fallout mount\b0\par\par\uc2 890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2019\par 13:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Huawei\uc2 , U.S.-China trade dispute rattle markets\par \par \uc2 * European chipmakers fall sharply on supply chain concerns\par \par \uc2 * Australian, Indian shares rally on apparent election results\par \par \uc2 * Oil bounce proves brief\par \par \uc2 * Graphic: World \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in 2019 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2  (Adds details, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Tommy Wilkes\par \par \uc2 LONDON, May 20 (Reuters) - Stock markets weakened on Monday as concerns mounted about an escalating fallout from a U.S. crackdown on China's \uc2 Huawei Technologies\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors already on edge about an escalating U.S.-China trade dispute were further rattled after Beijing accused Washington of harbouring "extravagant expectations" for a trade deal, underlining the gulf between the two sides.\par \par \uc2 Asian shares had managed to reverse some of last week's losses on Monday after Washington said it would lift tariffs in North America, and as investors cheered apparent wins by Conservative incumbent parties in elections in Australia and India.\par \par \uc2 But the mood did not carry over to Europe, where weak corporate earnings added to the gloom.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European Euro STOXX 600 extended earlier losses and was down 1.06% by 1100 GMT - the index, down 3.5% in May, is on track for its first monthly loss in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The German DAX slid 1.38%, while France's CAC 40 weakened 1.39%.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's government added \uc2 Huawei\uc2  to a trade blacklist last week, imposing restrictions that will make it difficult to do business with U.S. companies.\par \par \uc2 The repercussions quickly became evident as \uc2 Google\uc2  suspended some business with \uc2 Huawei\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Europe, chipmakers \uc2 Infineon Technologies\uc2 , \uc2 AMS\uc2  and \uc2 STMicroelectronics\uc2  dropped sharply, falling between 6% and 12% on growing fears of a disruption to the industry's global supply chain.\par \par \uc2 "Market volatility continues to stem from announcements and interpretations of what is going on in trade disputes between the U.S. and its trading partners, but principally China," said Jasper Lawler, head of research at \uc2 London Capital Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "China are unlikely to take \uc2 Google\uc2 's suspension of business with \uc2 Huawei\uc2  lying down."\par \par \uc2 On the positive side, a U.S. decision on Friday to remove tariffs on Canadian steel and aluminium prompted Canada's foreign minister to vow the quick ratification of a new North American trade agreement.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI index of world shares, which tracks shares in 47 countries, slipped 0.14%, leaving it 3.9% below its 2019 highs. The sudden return of trade war jitters has sent the stock market's year-to-date rally into reverse.\par \par \uc2 U.S. S&P 500 e-mini futures dropped 0.51%.\par \par \uc2 Prominent investor \uc2 Jim Rogers\uc2 , who co-founded the \uc2 Quantum Fund\uc2  with \uc2 George Soros\uc2 , told the Reuters Global Markets Forum that he believed Washington and Beijing would soon announce a trade deal, although the current spat would not be the last time Trump tried to exploit the prospect of a trade war.\par \par \uc2 "These are negotiating tactics from Mr. Trump at the moment. What will happen is we will have some good news, the market will have a rally. It will probably be the last rally," he said.\par \par \uc2 AUSSIE JUMPS\par \par \uc2 Oil prices briefly rallied after Saudi Energy Minister Khalid al-Falih said that there was consensus among \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and allied oil producers to reduce inventories "gently".\par \par \uc2 Rising tensions in the Middle East have also supported oil prices in recent days. Trump on Sunday tweeted that a conflict with Tehran would be the "official end" of Iran.\par \par \uc2 Both U.S. crude and Brent crude jumped more than 1%, before giving up most of those gains as broader risk sentiment soured.\par \par \uc2 U.S. West Texas Intermediate crude traded at $62.75 a barrel by 1030 GMT after earlier trading above $63. Brent crude was at $72.30 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the Australian dollar jumped nearly 1% to $0.6890 after the centre-right Liberal National Coalition pulled off a shock win in a federal election, beating the centre-left Labor party.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee also rallied, gaining more than 1% to 69.36 rupees per dollar after exit polls pointed to a majority for Prime Minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2 's Bharatiya Janata Party and allies.\par \par \uc2 China's offshore yuan rebounded after touching its weakest against the dollar since November on Friday. It last traded up 0.1% at 6.944 per dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy tools to stop it weakening past the psychologically important 7 yuan-per-dollar level in the near term, sources told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was little changed against the euro at $1.1155 .\par \par \uc2 Sterling recovered 0.2% to $1.2741 after suffering its biggest weekly loss since 2017 after an apparent collapse in Brexit talks in London.\par \par \uc2 German government\uc2  bond yields edged higher. That followed a fall towards new 2-1/2 year lows last week after investors nervous about trade and a global economic slowdown flocked to safe-haven debt.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield was little changed at 2.396%.\par \par \uc2 Austrian yields held firm after a scandal prompted Chancellor \uc2 Sebastian Kurz\uc2  to pull the plug on his coalition with the far right at the weekend, raising the chances of a snap election. (Additional reporting by Sujata Rao in London and Divya Chowdhury in Mumbai; Editing by Alison Williams) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-5-20T12:13:27.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190520ef5k01mc1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
