\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-European shares hobbled as worries about \uc2 Huawei\uc2  fallout mount\b0\par\par\uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2019\par 10:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * European chipmakers fall on reports of halted shipments\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Huawei\uc2  concerns offset ease in trade tensions\par \par \uc2 * Australian, Indian shares rally on apparent election wins\par \par \uc2 * Oil bounces after Saudi energy minister comments\par \par \uc2 * Graphic: World \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in 2019 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\par \par \uc2 By Tommy Wilkes\par \par \uc2 LONDON, May 20 (Reuters) - European stocks slipped on Monday as concerns about an escalating fallout from a U.S. crackdown on China's \uc2 Huawei Technologies\uc2  offset a slightly more positive tone on trade.\par \par \uc2 Asian shares managed to reverse some of last week's heavy losses on Monday, after the United States said it would lift tariffs in North America, as investors cheered apparent wins by Conservative incumbent parties in elections in Australia and India.\par \par \uc2 But the mood did not carry over to European markets. The pan-European Euro STOXX 600 fell 0.1%, the German DAX slipped 0.17%, while France's CAC 40 weakened 0.21%.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's government added \uc2 Huawei\uc2  to a trade blacklist last week, imposing restrictions that will make it difficult to do business with U.S. companies.\par \par \uc2 The repercussions of that move became evident as \uc2 Google\uc2  suspended some business with \uc2 Huawei\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Europe, chipmakers \uc2 Infineon Technologies\uc2 , \uc2 AMS\uc2  and \uc2 STMicroelectronics\uc2  dropped sharply after Nikkei Asian Review reported that German chipmaker \uc2 Infineon\uc2  had halted shipments to \uc2 Huawei\uc2 . The company had no immediate comment.\par \par \uc2 "Market volatility continues to stem from announcements and interpretations of what is going on in trade disputes between the U.S. and its trading partners, but principally China," said Jasper Lawler, head of research at \uc2 London Capital Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Whilst the latest headlines over Canada are supportive of sentiment, a news bite which increases risk aversion could be just around the corner. Investors know this and are on edge right now. That won\u8217\'20\'19t change overnight. China are unlikely to take \uc2 Google\uc2 's suspension of business with \uc2 Huawei\uc2  lying down."\par \par \uc2 On the positive side, a U.S. decision on Friday to remove tariffs on Canadian steel and aluminium prompted Canada's foreign minister to vow the quick ratification of a new North American trade agreement.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI index of world shares, which tracks shares in 47 countries, rose 0.08%. It remains some 3.6% below its 2019 highs as the sudden return of trade war jitters sent the stock market's strong rally into reverse.\par \par \uc2 U.S. S&P 500 e-mini futures turned higher, rising 0.23% following losses on Wall Street on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Prominent investor \uc2 Jim Rogers\uc2 , who co-founded the \uc2 Quantum Fund\uc2  with \uc2 George Soros\uc2 , told the Reuters Global Markets Forum that he believed Washington and Beijing would soon announce a trade deal, although the current spat would not be the last time Trump tried to exploit the prospect of a trade war.\par \par \uc2 "These are negotiating tactics from Mr. Trump at the moment. What will happen is we will have some good news, the market will have a rally. It will probably be the last rally," he said.\par \par \uc2 OIL JUMPS\par \par \uc2 Oil prices jumped after Saudi Energy Minister Khalid al-Falih said that there was consensus among the members of the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  to maintain production cuts to "gently" reduce inventories.\par \par \uc2 Both U.S. crude and Brent crude jumped more than 1.1% on Monday, with West Texas Intermediate fetching $63.5 a barrel and Brent crude at $73.04 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 Rising tensions in the Middle East, which have supported oil prices, ratcheted up another notch on the weekend as Trump issued new threats, tweeting that a conflict with Iran would be the "official end" of that country.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the Australian dollar jumped nearly 1% after the centre-right Liberal National Coalition pulled off a shock win in a federal election, beating the centre-left Labor party.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee also rallied after exit polls pointed to a majority for Prime Minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2 's Bharatiya Janata Party and allies. The rupee was last up 1.1%.\par \par \uc2 China's offshore yuan rebounded after touching its weakest against the dollar since November on Friday. It last traded up 0.1% at 6.9441 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Sources told Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy tools to stop it weakening past the 7-per-dollar level in the near term.\par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped slightly against the euro to $1.1161 .\par \par \uc2 Sterling recovered 0.3% to $1.2751 after suffering its biggest weekly loss since 2017 after an apparent collapse in Brexit talks in London.\par \par \uc2 German government\uc2  bond yields moved slightly higher. That followed a fall towards new 2-1/2 year lows last week after investors flocked to safe-haven debt.\par \par \uc2 Austrian yields held firm after a scandal prompted Chancellor \uc2 Sebastian Kurz\uc2  to pull the plug on his coalition with the far right at the weekend, raising the chances of a snap election. (Additional reporting by Andrew Galbraith in Shanghai and Divya Chowdhury in Mumbai; Editing by Alison Williams) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-5-20T09:55:02.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190520ef5k0141q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
