\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Shares swoon as Beijing ramps up war of words\b0\par\par\uc2 825 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2019\par 11:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Asian stock markets : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2zpUAr4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2zpUAr4}}}\par \par \uc2 * Shanghai shares skid as Trump targets \uc2 Huawei\uc2 , Europe lower\par \par \uc2 * Asia index ex-Japan hits 15-week trough\par \par \uc2 * Yen edges higher as mood turns risk averse\par \par \uc2 * Bitcoin suddenly tumbles, then claws back losses\par \par \uc2 * Oil waits for OPEC+ output signals\par \par \uc2 * World \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in 2019 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, May 17 (Reuters) - World share markets suffered a fresh bout of risk aversion on Friday after tough words on trade from China, while bets on a new pro-Brexit leader in Britain whipped the pound towards its worst week since October.\par \par \uc2 Europe's bourses slipped 0.6% early on that seemed a minor blip after what had happened in Asia.\par \par \uc2 Shanghai stocks finished 2.5% in the red and the yuan hit its weakest in nearly five months amid growing fallout from President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's move to block China's \uc2 Huawei Technologies\uc2  from buying vital American technology.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Communist Party's People's Daily used a front page commentary to evoke the patriotic spirit of past wars, saying the trade war would never bring China down.\par \par \uc2 In terms of how the trade conflict plays out, "the next fortnight will be very, very important," \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  strategist Kiran Kowshik said.\par \par \uc2 "Chinese counter-tariffs are due on June 1 and if those get effective, I think markets will price in the risk of the U.S. imposing its additional $300 billion of tariffs ahead of the G20 meeting (near the end of June)."\par \par \uc2 The drop in the yuan saw it ease past 6.9400 per dollar in the offshore market for the first time since November 2018.\par \par \uc2 Its slide has been steepening in recent days. Sources in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Reuters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure it did not weaken past 7 to the dollar in the near term.\par \par \uc2 While breaking 7 could reduce some of the effects of U.S. tariff increases, it could hit confidence and trigger fund outflows, one of the sources said.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was at 15-week lows and down 2.6% for the week at the end of trading.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei did manage to bounce 0.9%, while the main Australian index climbed to an 11-year peak as higher commodity prices boosted miners.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, Germany's exporter-heavy DAX fell the most, auto stocks lost as much as 1.6% and E-Mini futures for the S&P 500 shed 0.35% ahead of Wall Street trading.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment had been briefly soothed on Thursday by better U.S. economic news, with housing starts surprisingly strong and a welcome pickup in the Philadelphia Federal Reserve's manufacturing survey.\par \par \uc2 Upbeat results from \uc2 Walmart\uc2  burnished the outlook for retail spending, though the chain also warned that tariffs would raise prices for U.S. consumers.\par \par \uc2 As the earnings season winds down, of the 457 S&P 500 companies reporting about 75% have beaten profit expectations, according to Refinitiv data.\par \par \uc2 MAY COUNTS DOWN TO JUNE\par \par \uc2 The chillier trade winds helped Treasuries, with the 10-year yield down at 2.38% after a second strong week running for bond markets.\par \par \uc2 The dollar lost a little of its shine against the safe-haven yen to stand at 109.64 from a top of 110.03. Against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it was a shade softer at 96.824.\par \par \uc2 Yet the euro could make no ground and held at $1.1173 , down 0.5% for the week so far.\par \par \uc2 Sterling was one of the worst performers as Britain's Prime Minister \uc2 Theresa May\uc2  battled to keep her Brexit deal, and her premiership, intact amid growing fears of a disorderly departure from the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The pound touched a three-month low of $1.2783 and was down 1.6% for the week so far.\par \par \uc2 Also under pressure was the Australian dollar, losing 1.5% for the week to $0.6880 as investors piled into bets that interest rates would be cut in June.\par \par \uc2 Cyber \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bitcoin tumbled over 20% at one stage for no clear reason. It was last down 7%, albeit back on course for its third week of gains and having doubled in value this year.\par \par \uc2 In commodity markets, spot gold steadied at $1,287 per ounce as risk sentiment soured.\par \par \uc2 Oil futures firmed into a fourth session as rising tensions in the Middle East stoked fears of potential supply disruptions.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude was last up 33 cents at $63.20 a barrel, while Brent crude futures rose 19 cents to $72.81.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  and other producers will meet in Saudi Arabia this weekend over whether to continue with supply cuts that have boosted prices more than 30% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Shri Navaratnam and John Stonestreet) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-5-17T10:00:27.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190517ef5h01ard\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
