\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan slides past key mark but signs of \uc2 PBOC\uc2  vigilance cap losses\b0\par\par\uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2019\par 09:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, updates prices)               \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, May 17 (Reuters) - China's yuan fell past the psychologically-important 6.9 per dollar level to its weakest in nearly five months on Friday, but losses were capped on signs the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will defend the  \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening past the critical 7 per dollar mark.               \par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set for a fifth straight weekly loss and has already shed around 2.5 percent to the dollar since U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said on May 5 he was going to raise tariffs on $200 billion of Chinese imports.               \par \par \uc2 But in both onshore and offshore trade it recouped some of the losses in afternoon deals, after sources told Reuters that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy tools to ensure the yuan does not weaken past the 7-per-dollar key level in the immediate term.               \par \par \uc2 "Breaking 7 is beneficial to China because it can reduce some of the effects of tariff increases, but the impact on our renminbi confidence is negative and funds will flow out," one of the sources said.               \par \par \uc2 Market sentiment has been hurt by \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  moves to block China's \uc2 Huawei Technologies\uc2  from buying vital American technology, throwing into question prospects for sales at some of the largest tech companies and drawing a sharp rebuke from Beijing.               \par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts said many corporate clients had set the strike price for their option contracts at around 6.9 per dollar, meaning a breach of that level could quickly send the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 7.               \par \par \uc2 The yuan has lost all its gains made this year. Markets are now debating whether the authorities will let the renminbi breach the 7 per dollar level, which was last seen during the global financial crisis.               \par \par \uc2 Prior to market opening, the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 lowered the midpoint rate to 6.8859 per dollar, 171 pips or 0.25 percent weaker than the previous fix of 6.8688. Friday's fixing was the weakest since Dec. 27, 2018.               \par \par \uc2 In the spot market, the onshore yuan opened at 6.9000 per dollar and fell to a low of 6.9099 at one point, the softest level since Dec. 24, 2018.               \par \par \uc2 At of 0656 GMT, spot yuan was changing hands at 6.9055, 215 pips weaker than the previous late session close.               \par \par \uc2 If the yuan finishes late night trade at the midday level, it would have lost 1.2 percent to the dollar for the week, the biggest weekly loss since July 2018.               \par \par \uc2 But losses in the onshore yuan were also capped as several traders said investors were unwilling to test fresh lows below 6.9 per dollar - a level the market widely believes the authorities will guard to prevent it from sinking further.               \par \par \uc2 "The market does not dare to buy a lot of dollars, as investors are worried that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could step in any time soon," said a trader at a Chinese bank.               \par \par \uc2 Philip Wee\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at DBS, said the yuan was likely to be capped at 7 per dollar ahead of a possible meeting between President Trump and his Chinese counterpart \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  at the G20 summit in late June.               \par \par \uc2 "Assuming the Xi-Trump meeting takes place next month, no one expects a trade deal, if any, to roll back existing tariffs but to hold off Trump's threat to hit a 25 percent tariff on the remaining $325 billion of Chinese goods," he said in a note on Friday.               \par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan weakened to 6.9420 in morning trade, erasing all the gains made since the previous Trump-Xi meeting in November. It traded at 6.9340 per dollar as of 0656 GMT.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-5-17T08:16:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190517ef5h010q9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
