\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-COMMENT- \highlight22\uc2 Central bank interventions \highlight\uc2  will boost the dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 177 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2019\par 12:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Several major central banks are selling or are likely to sell dollars to prop up their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . All of those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to see reserves rebalanced after underpinning the dollar versus euro and yen. Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the HKD's trading band. Bank of Korea has expressed concern over the won's drop, and it's a big hitter when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the biggest hitter of all, and if China wants to strike a trade deal with the United States, it cannot allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep dropping. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely, and with 3 trillion dollars in reserves \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  can easily control USD/CNH. Risk aversion is driving most \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, but EUR/CHF is relatively well supported, less than 1 percent below 2019 highs. Without the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the bid, the euro loses a key support that has typified other periods of risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-5-12T10:09:36.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190509ef5901u1t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
