\par\par \b \uc2 Downgrade Risk of EMEA BBB-Rated Non-Financial Bonds Manageable: S&P s -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2019\par 14:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0813 ET - About $250 billion a year of BBB-rated debt from non-financial companies in Europe, the Middle East, and Africa could fall into junk territory in a severe downturn, says S&P Global Ratings in a report. However, this represents 10% of the outstanding total debt in that category, which the credit rating agency considers "manageable" for the market to absorb. The largest single driver was about $1.5 trillion of debt downgraded from higher ratings due to companies' increasing risk tolerance, it says. Leverage levels in the region for paper rated in the weakest rung of investment grade has surged by about $1 trillion from 2007, reaching $2.2 trillion at year-end 2018, it adds. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0759 ET - Global economic growth may pick up in the second half of the year despite the current weaker economic data, particularly in Europe, says \uc2 Oddo BHF Asset Management\uc2 . Credit growth, a key driver of the Chinese economic growth, is no longer shrinking, and should provide impetus to the world trade in the second half of the year, Oddo BHF AM says. Europe should benefit from this, but also from the significantly looser financing conditions. In the U.S., growth remains robust. Given the lack of inflation pressure globally, the risk of recession is low. Political risks, like Brexit, could put a brake on the recovery, even though progress has been reached there while the U.S. and China may come to an agreement on tariffs, Oddo BHF AM says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0736 ET - One of the drivers behind the recent EUR/USD rise could be the rebound in the Chinese economy, says \uc2 Sebastien Galy\uc2 , strategist at \uc2 Nordea\uc2  . "Chinese economic data is likely to continue to show a mild rebound in March after a weak January and February. A consequence of this rosier outlook has been increased diversification of portfolio inflows...pushing EUR/USD higher," he says. With China included in broad aggregates such as MSCI, portfolio inflows rise, pushing the yuan higher. But the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  reacts by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to calm prices, buying euros and selling yuan. "When the carry trade into China is strong, the upward pressure on EUR/USD is considerable and accelerated as some layers of strikes are reached," Galy says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 ET - The 10-year German Bund yield is confined in a narrow trading range, says \uc2 Oddo BHF Asset Management\uc2 . There is little room to fall given the first signs of economic recovery, Oddo BHF AM says. The room to rise is also limited due to strong investor demand for triple-A rated government bonds, a cautious \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and inflation that looks set to decline, the asset manager says.(emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0718 ET - In the first quarter, USD/CNY fell by only 0.18% and volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair "was still quite small," says ING. "This tells us that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has carefully managed exchange rate movements," ING says. The stability of the Chinese yuan is key for investors as profits become easier to forecast. On Monday, USD/CNY is flat at 6.7073 and ING expects it to finish the year at 6.75. ING's forecast "seems to match what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doing. At least for now," the Dutch bank says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 ET - The upbeat sentiment surrounding European credit has reached the financial sector, with banks raising the equivalent of EUR8 billion through 11 bond issues in March alone, says Gildas Surry, portfolio manager at Axiom Alternative Investments. "Due to the sharp tightening of spreads since the beginning of the year, the primary market was very active during the month", he says. \uc2 KBC Bank\uc2  , \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  , \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  , \uc2 Nordea Bank\uc2  and \uc2 Barclays\uc2  all issued bonds last month.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0643 ET - Auchan's plan to issue 6-year euro-denominated senior unsecured bond comes as a shock to CreditSights analysts, who use the word "astonishingly" to describe the move. The French supermarket chain is holding a global investor call on Monday from 0930 GMT to gauge investor appetite for the transaction, according to a lead manager. The potential issue comes after "dismal" 2018 fiscal year results, which saw the group's largest free cash flows deficit since \uc2 CreditSights\uc2  started tracking this name in 2006, it says. "We worry that this new bond might be implying larger than expected cash flow strain since the start of this year despite the use of the proceeds being described as 'to refinance the upcoming EUR500M in April 15, 2019'," it says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0633 ET - EUR/USD has recovered back above 1.13 but its "support at current levels could give way very quickly with trade tensions back in focus," says \uc2 MUFG\uc2 . EU countries are expected Monday to formally endorse the renewal of trade negotiations with the U.S., "probably in part as a means to halt the threat of auto tariffs being implemented," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  says. Whilst U.S.-China trade negotiations are ongoing, the focus in Washington turns towards Europe and Japan. Later this week, Japanese trade minister \uc2 Toshimitsu Motegi\uc2  will meet his counterpart \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  , this being "viewed as the starting phase of more intense negotiations." EUR/USD is last up 0.1% at 1.1309. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 ET - The Dutch July 2029-dated government bond, or DSL, offers a good buy opportunity against a number of peers in the eurozone, \uc2 Nordea\uc2  's Uffe Kalmar Hansen says ahead of the EUR1 billion-EUR2 billion tap of the Dutch bond Tuesday. \uc2 Nordea\uc2  recommends buying the July 2029 DSL versus the Danish November 2029 peer to take advantage of the very tight level of Danish bonds relative to Germany, and the likelihood of Danish bonds outperforming German ones this year. \uc2 Nordea\uc2  also recommends the July 2029 Dutch bond versus Germany's February 2029 Bund to take advantage of the continued high yield pickup, which more than compensates for differences in liquidity. Alternatively, investors can be long in the Dutch bond versus France after solid performance in the latter of late. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0537 ET - The confidence in the U.S. dollar may be undermined by U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , says \uc2 MUFG\uc2 . As central bankers and economists around the world gathered in Washington, Trump once again criticized the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  for raising interest rates last year. Trump tweeted adding that quantitative tightening "was a killer" and that GDP would have grown at 4% rather than 3%. "If growth in the U.S. slows further he [Trump] is likely to escalate his interference in Fed policy, and undermine confidence in the dollar," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  says. "The likelihood is that the political interference is going to intensify," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  adds. The dollar trades slightly lower with EUR/USD up 0.1% at 1.1317. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 ET - Finland will most likely be governed by a grand coalition, an outcome that is unlikely to have any negative impact on Finnish government bonds, says \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  , after Sunday's election delivered a fragmented landscape. "The inclusion of the conservatives in a 'grand coalition' should secure that Finland will still be focused on keeping the budget deficit in check," says Valtteri Ahti, head of Finland research at Danske . The Social Democrats got 17.7% of the votes, less than expected, securing only a narrow win over the populist True Finns party which got 17.5%. The mainstream right-wing National Coalition Party got 17.0%, and is likely to stay in power in order for the coalition government to muster sufficient seats, the analyst says. The 10-year Finnish bond trades at 0.382%. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0527 ET - The European corporate bond issuance market is "an issuer's market and there doesn't seem to be much on the horizon that can change this in the short run," says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  's senior corporate credit strategist Hyung-Ja de Zeeuw. Investor demand for new bonds seems "almost insatiable", as they look prepared to buy new paper with small new issue concessions or even negative concessions in some cases. This has been reflected in junk-rated \uc2 Telecom Italia\uc2  's recent bond issue, which priced flat on its secondary curve. "Unless issuers push it too far in terms of pricing, investors are likely to continue to add risk to their portfolios," she says. Investor demand has been boosted by central banks' decision to keep interest rates low for the foreseeable future to prop up ailing economic growth. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 15, 2019 08:13 ET (12:13 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190415ef4f001ju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
