\par\par \b \uc2 Euro Rises as Investors Buy More Chinese Assets: \uc2 Nordea\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2019\par 13:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1136 GMT - One of the drivers behind the recent EUR/USD rise could be the rebound in the Chinese economy, says \uc2 Sebastien Galy\uc2 , strategist at \uc2 Nordea\uc2  . "Chinese economic data is likely to continue to show a mild rebound in March after a weak January and February. A consequence of this rosier outlook has been increased diversification of portfolio inflows...pushing EUR/USD higher," he says. With China included in broad aggregates such as MSCI, portfolio inflows rise, pushing the yuan higher. But the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  reacts by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to calm prices, buying euros and selling yuan. "When the carry trade into China is strong, the upward pressure on EUR/USD is considerable and accelerated as some layers of strikes are reached," Galy says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1132 GMT - The 10-year German Bund yield is confined in a narrow trading range, says \uc2 Oddo BHF Asset Management\uc2 . There is little room to fall given the first signs of economic recovery, Oddo BHF AM says. The room to rise is also limited due to strong investor demand for triple-A rated government bonds, a cautious \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and inflation that looks set to decline, the asset manager says.(emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1118 GMT - In the first quarter, USD/CNY fell by only 0.18% and volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair "was still quite small," says ING. "This tells us that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has carefully managed exchange rate movements," ING says. The stability of the Chinese yuan is key for investors as profits become easier to forecast. On Monday, USD/CNY is flat at 6.7073 and ING expects it to finish the year at 6.75. ING's forecast "seems to match what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doing. At least for now," the Dutch bank says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1059 GMT - The upbeat sentiment surrounding European credit has reached the financial sector, with banks raising the equivalent of EUR8 billion through 11 bond issues in March alone, says Gildas Surry, portfolio manager at Axiom Alternative Investments. "Due to the sharp tightening of spreads since the beginning of the year, the primary market was very active during the month", he says. \uc2 KBC Bank\uc2  , \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  , \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  , \uc2 Nordea Bank\uc2  and \uc2 Barclays\uc2  all issued bonds last month.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1043 GMT - Auchan's plan to issue 6-year euro-denominated senior unsecured bond comes as a shock to CreditSights analysts, who use the word "astonishingly" to describe the move. The French supermarket chain is holding a global investor call on Monday from 0930 GMT to gauge investor appetite for the transaction, according to a lead manager. The potential issue comes after "dismal" 2018 fiscal year results, which saw the group's largest free cash flows deficit since \uc2 CreditSights\uc2  started tracking this name in 2006, it says. "We worry that this new bond might be implying larger than expected cash flow strain since the start of this year despite the use of the proceeds being described as 'to refinance the upcoming EUR500M in April 15, 2019'," it says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1033 GMT - EUR/USD has recovered back above 1.13 but its "support at current levels could give way very quickly with trade tensions back in focus," says \uc2 MUFG\uc2 . EU countries are expected Monday to formally endorse the renewal of trade negotiations with the U.S., "probably in part as a means to halt the threat of auto tariffs being implemented," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  says. Whilst U.S.-China trade negotiations are ongoing, the focus in Washington turns towards Europe and Japan. Later this week, Japanese trade minister \uc2 Toshimitsu Motegi\uc2  will meet his counterpart \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  , this being "viewed as the starting phase of more intense negotiations." EUR/USD is last up 0.1% at 1.1309. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT - The Dutch July 2029-dated government bond, or DSL, offers a good buy opportunity against a number of peers in the eurozone, \uc2 Nordea\uc2  's Uffe Kalmar Hansen says ahead of the EUR1 billion-EUR2 billion tap of the Dutch bond Tuesday. \uc2 Nordea\uc2  recommends buying the July 2029 DSL versus the Danish November 2029 peer to take advantage of the very tight level of Danish bonds relative to Germany, and the likelihood of Danish bonds outperforming German ones this year. \uc2 Nordea\uc2  also recommends the July 2029 Dutch bond versus Germany's February 2029 Bund to take advantage of the continued high yield pickup, which more than compensates for differences in liquidity. Alternatively, investors can be long in the Dutch bond versus France after solid performance in the latter of late. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0937 GMT - The confidence in the U.S. dollar may be undermined by U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , says \uc2 MUFG\uc2 . As central bankers and economists around the world gathered in Washington, Trump once again criticized the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  for raising interest rates last year. Trump tweeted adding that quantitative tightening "was a killer" and that GDP would have grown at 4% rather than 3%. "If growth in the U.S. slows further he [Trump] is likely to escalate his interference in Fed policy, and undermine confidence in the dollar," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  says. "The likelihood is that the political interference is going to intensify," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  adds. The dollar trades slightly lower with EUR/USD up 0.1% at 1.1317. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT - Finland will most likely be governed by a grand coalition, an outcome that is unlikely to have any negative impact on Finnish government bonds, says \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  , after Sunday's election delivered a fragmented landscape. "The inclusion of the conservatives in a 'grand coalition' should secure that Finland will still be focused on keeping the budget deficit in check," says Valtteri Ahti, head of Finland research at Danske . The Social Democrats got 17.7% of the votes, less than expected, securing only a narrow win over the populist True Finns party which got 17.5%. The mainstream right-wing National Coalition Party got 17.0%, and is likely to stay in power in order for the coalition government to muster sufficient seats, the analyst says. The 10-year Finnish bond trades at 0.382%. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0927 GMT - The European corporate bond issuance market is "an issuer's market and there doesn't seem to be much on the horizon that can change this in the short run," says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  's senior corporate credit strategist Hyung-Ja de Zeeuw. Investor demand for new bonds seems "almost insatiable", as they look prepared to buy new paper with small new issue concessions or even negative concessions in some cases. This has been reflected in junk-rated \uc2 Telecom Italia\uc2  's recent bond issue, which priced flat on its secondary curve. "Unless issuers push it too far in terms of pricing, investors are likely to continue to add risk to their portfolios," she says. Investor demand has been boosted by central banks' decision to keep interest rates low for the foreseeable future to prop up ailing economic growth. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0908 GMT - Risk-on sentiment, driven by better-than-expected economic indicators in Europe and China, helped boost returns on European credit last week, says Tomas Hirst and Fernando Barajas at \uc2 CreditSights\uc2 . The ICE/\uc2 BofA\uc2  ML euro investment-grade index delivered excess returns of 0.25% and total returns of 0.03% during the period, they say. Investor risk appetite was partially buoyed by above-expectations eurozone industrial-production data for February, released on Friday. This added to upbeat customs data from China last week showing March exports beat expectations, which eased fears over slowing economic growth in the Asian country. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT - The Finnish Social Democratic Party's narrow win in Finland's elections on Sunday bucks the European trend of social democrats losing, but it still leaves the country's political landscape very fragmented and will make forming a coalition difficult, says ING's senior eurozone economist \uc2 Bert Colijn\uc2  . The Social Democratic Party won 17.7% of the votes, only slightly more than the populist Finns Party's 17.5%. This is the first occasion in over a century where no party has won more than 20% of the vote, Colijn says. "The recent surge could be taken as a bellwether for the performance of other eurosceptic parties in the European parliamentary elections, but with current polls still showing a strong lead for centrist parties, momentum would have to change radically across Europe for a large nationalist bloc to change EU policy making," he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 15, 2019 07:36 ET (11:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190415ef4f001c0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
