\par\par \b \uc2 Good US Data Could Be Good for EM: ING -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2019\par 14:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0852 ET - This week, US economic data such as retail sales and trade data could "paint a picture of a solid U.S. economy" which should be a positive for emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to ING. Usually, better data in the U.S. would point to higher interest rates, which would in turn hurt EM assets. But as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has signaled that it is willing to see inflation overshoot, a "solid economy but still cautious" Fed "further help EM \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ING says. The Mexican peso remains the bank's "top EM pick." USD/MXN already broke the 18.80 support level Friday "and the peso is set to benefit further." USD/MXN is last flat at 18.7660. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0840 ET - \uc2 Oddo BHF Asset Management\uc2  expects commodities prices to rise in the second half of the year, due to a weaker US dollar and an economic growth that is set to recover. In particular copper and oil may benefit from this mix, the asset manager says. The price of a barrel of oil should continue to rise as the distorted supply-demand--due to problems in Venezuela and Iran--can't be made up for quickly, despite a rise in U.S. shale production, \uc2 Oddo BHF\uc2  says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0830 ET - \uc2 Bank of America Merrill\uc2  expects carry-driven exposure to emerging markets to continue to be underpinned by a favorable global backdrop. "The overall environment is...still favorable for EM, not so much due to cheap valuations but to still-attractive-enough risk-adjusted carry," says the bank's EM strategist Claudio Irigoyen. He likes Ukraine's GDP-linked securities, Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 -unhedged 10-year government bonds, "as the latest sanctions headlines actually reduce the tail risk of harsher sanctions on sovereign debt", and Bahrain's rates-hedged bonds maturing in 2029. Faltering EM growth rather than higher U.S. rates pose the main risk to EM exposure, he adds. A carry trade involves investors borrowing at low interest rates in order to invest in an asset that is likely to provide a higher return.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0813 ET - About $250 billion a year of BBB-rated debt from non-financial companies in Europe, the Middle East, and Africa could fall into junk territory in a severe downturn, says S&P Global Ratings in a report. However, this represents 10% of the outstanding total debt in that category, which the credit rating agency considers "manageable" for the market to absorb. The largest single driver was about $1.5 trillion of debt downgraded from higher ratings due to companies' increasing risk tolerance, it says. Leverage levels in the region for paper rated in the weakest rung of investment grade has surged by about $1 trillion from 2007, reaching $2.2 trillion at year-end 2018, it adds. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0759 ET - Global economic growth may pick up in the second half of the year despite the current weaker economic data, particularly in Europe, says \uc2 Oddo BHF Asset Management\uc2 . Credit growth, a key driver of the Chinese economic growth, is no longer shrinking, and should provide impetus to the world trade in the second half of the year, Oddo BHF AM says. Europe should benefit from this, but also from the significantly looser financing conditions. In the U.S., growth remains robust. Given the lack of inflation pressure globally, the risk of recession is low. Political risks, like Brexit, could put a brake on the recovery, even though progress has been reached there while the U.S. and China may come to an agreement on tariffs, Oddo BHF AM says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0736 ET - One of the drivers behind the recent EUR/USD rise could be the rebound in the Chinese economy, says \uc2 Sebastien Galy\uc2 , strategist at \uc2 Nordea\uc2  . "Chinese economic data is likely to continue to show a mild rebound in March after a weak January and February. A consequence of this rosier outlook has been increased diversification of portfolio inflows...pushing EUR/USD higher," he says. With China included in broad aggregates such as MSCI, portfolio inflows rise, pushing the yuan higher. But the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  reacts by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to calm prices, buying euros and selling yuan. "When the carry trade into China is strong, the upward pressure on EUR/USD is considerable and accelerated as some layers of strikes are reached," Galy says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 ET - The 10-year German Bund yield is confined in a narrow trading range, says \uc2 Oddo BHF Asset Management\uc2 . There is little room to fall given the first signs of economic recovery, Oddo BHF AM says. The room to rise is also limited due to strong investor demand for triple-A rated government bonds, a cautious \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and inflation that looks set to decline, the asset manager says.(emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0718 ET - In the first quarter, USD/CNY fell by only 0.18% and volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair "was still quite small," says ING. "This tells us that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has carefully managed exchange rate movements," ING says. The stability of the Chinese yuan is key for investors as profits become easier to forecast. On Monday, USD/CNY is flat at 6.7073 and ING expects it to finish the year at 6.75. ING's forecast "seems to match what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doing. At least for now," the Dutch bank says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 ET - The upbeat sentiment surrounding European credit has reached the financial sector, with banks raising the equivalent of EUR8 billion through 11 bond issues in March alone, says Gildas Surry, portfolio manager at Axiom Alternative Investments. "Due to the sharp tightening of spreads since the beginning of the year, the primary market was very active during the month", he says. \uc2 KBC Bank\uc2  , \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  , \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  , \uc2 Nordea Bank\uc2  and \uc2 Barclays\uc2  all issued bonds last month.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0643 ET - Auchan's plan to issue 6-year euro-denominated senior unsecured bond comes as a shock to CreditSights analysts, who use the word "astonishingly" to describe the move. The French supermarket chain is holding a global investor call on Monday from 0930 GMT to gauge investor appetite for the transaction, according to a lead manager. The potential issue comes after "dismal" 2018 fiscal year results, which saw the group's largest free cash flows deficit since \uc2 CreditSights\uc2  started tracking this name in 2006, it says. "We worry that this new bond might be implying larger than expected cash flow strain since the start of this year despite the use of the proceeds being described as 'to refinance the upcoming EUR500M in April 15, 2019'," it says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0633 ET - EUR/USD has recovered back above 1.13 but its "support at current levels could give way very quickly with trade tensions back in focus," says \uc2 MUFG\uc2 . EU countries are expected Monday to formally endorse the renewal of trade negotiations with the U.S., "probably in part as a means to halt the threat of auto tariffs being implemented," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  says. Whilst U.S.-China trade negotiations are ongoing, the focus in Washington turns towards Europe and Japan. Later this week, Japanese trade minister \uc2 Toshimitsu Motegi\uc2  will meet his counterpart \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  , this being "viewed as the starting phase of more intense negotiations." EUR/USD is last up 0.1% at 1.1309. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 ET - The Dutch July 2029-dated government bond, or DSL, offers a good buy opportunity against a number of peers in the eurozone, \uc2 Nordea\uc2  's Uffe Kalmar Hansen says ahead of the EUR1 billion-EUR2 billion tap of the Dutch bond Tuesday. \uc2 Nordea\uc2  recommends buying the July 2029 DSL versus the Danish November 2029 peer to take advantage of the very tight level of Danish bonds relative to Germany, and the likelihood of Danish bonds outperforming German ones this year. \uc2 Nordea\uc2  also recommends the July 2029 Dutch bond versus Germany's February 2029 Bund to take advantage of the continued high yield pickup, which more than compensates for differences in liquidity. Alternatively, investors can be long in the Dutch bond versus France after solid performance in the latter of late. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 15, 2019 08:52 ET (12:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190415ef4f001iu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
