\par\par \b \uc2 Pact Hems Beijing  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Moves -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 902 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2019\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A8\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Trade deal between U.S. and China is to call for disclosure of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By William Mauldin and Josh Zumbrun \f28 \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (April 13, 2019).\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- As part of a prospective deal on trade, the U.S. and China have agreed to measures that American officials say will deter Beijing from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation by requiring greater disclosure of economic actions, according to current and former officials familiar with the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The deal could also include penalties for China if it manipulates its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase exports, which is in violation of international guidelines, these people say.\par \par \uc2 "The fundamental issue on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the board is we want to make sure people meet their obligations, that they don't devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for competitive purposes," Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2  said in an interview. "That's the objective."\par \par \uc2 The trade deal is still under negotiation and American officials caution that nothing will be final until both sides agree on all points.\par \par \uc2 Even so, an enforceable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure "would be significant and would represent a further step forward into bringing discipline to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -manipulation issue," said \uc2 Fred Bergsten\uc2 , a former top Treasury Department official and co-founder of the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The measures for greater economic disclosure are aimed at spotlighting monetary policies that fit the pattern of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation. Beijing doesn't disclose the composition of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves -- which at $3 trillion as of last month are the world's largest -- or its purchases in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. That makes it difficult to assess whether Beijing is manipulating the yuan.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2 , indicated at a news conference last month that better disclosure of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be part of the deal.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  provisions and the overall deal's enforcement system have already been hammered out as a part of the sprawling U.S.-China trade agreement, a work-in-progress that is meant to end the dispute, U.S. officials say.\par \par \uc2 Treasury officials declined to provide more information on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  enforcement rules and a spokeswoman for U.S. Trade Representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  declined to comment. A spokesman for the Chinese embassy in Washington didn't immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 A senior Treasury official said the U.S.-China agreement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has similarities to the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  revamp that the Trump administration signed last year with Canada and Mexico. The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  deal also has "certain aspects that go beyond" the new, unratified Nafta deal, known as the U.S.-Mexico-Canada Agreement, or USMCA.\par \par \uc2 Economists and politicians in recent years have grown more worried about countries manipulating their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to favor exports over imports, effectively reducing or eliminating the gains that companies operating in one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  get from lower tariffs negotiated in trade agreements.\par \par \uc2 Such \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation isn't allowed under the guidelines of international organizations -- including the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  leading global economies -- but these organizations don't have enforcement mechanisms to hold countries to account.\par \par \uc2 Major economies and their central banks have been reluctant to tie their own hands when it comes to monetary tools, seeing it as a key part of economic sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, President Trump said he would formally declare China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator when he took office, but his administration backed down on that promise. Administration officials say \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently to cheapen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but could do so in the future.\par \par \uc2 Many U.S. lawmakers and some exporting industries have long pressed for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules in trade agreements. The Obama administration in 2015 spearheaded a nonbinding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  understanding among the U.S. and 11 Pacific countries included in the Trans-Pacific Partnership. But Mr. Trump withdrew from that agreement.\par \par \uc2 Then last year, the Trump administration included an \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  portion in the USMCA, which awaits Congressional approval. The USMCA language was seen as a deterrent to manipulation. Still, only a section on transparency will be enforceable if the new North American deal enters into force, economists say.\par \par \uc2 Some expect the China pact to go further on enforcement than just requiring transparency, with penalties for violating international economic principles in ways that cheapen a national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bergsten said there is a "possibility that the enforcement mechanism may have broader coverage, which would be significant and would represent a further step forward into bringing discipline to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -manipulation issue."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bergsten said the U.S. and China would likely solve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  disputes in similar fashion to strictly trade-related issues, perhaps with sets of consultations among economic or Treasury officials from the two nations, with tariffs and perhaps other sanctions allowed as penalties.\par \par \uc2 As a so-called executive agreement that wouldn't be submitted to Congress for ratification, the U.S.-China deal wouldn't have the same legal structure as a free-trade agreement. Some members of Congress worry the pact could improperly change U.S. trade policies without their approval.\par \par \uc2 --Kate Davidson contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to William Mauldin at william.mauldin@wsj.com and Josh Zumbrun at Josh.Zumbrun@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 13, 2019 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190413ef4d0004v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
