\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China will fend off cross-border capital flow risks -  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  regulator\b0\par\par\uc2 353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par 11:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, March 29 (Reuters) - China will fend off cross-border capital flow risks and keep the yuan exchange rate basically stable in 2019, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Cross-border capital flows will be adjusted via "market-based counter cyclical measures", the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) said in a report, without elaborating.\par \par \uc2 Since 2016, China has imposed stringent curbs to prevent capital flight, a move made in the aftermath of a spectacular stock market collapse during the last economic downturn. Regulators will continue to crack down on illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  activities, SAFE added.\par \par \uc2 While China's economy has continued to cool, analysts believe the risk of strong capital outflows has greatly diminished in recent months, as the yuan regained its footing and foreign investors piled back into battered Chinese stock markets.\par \par \uc2 On March 24, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2  said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has basically exited from regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and that Beijing will push for reforms which enhance the yuan's exchange rate flexibility.\par \par \uc2 China will push forward capital account convertibility in an orderly way this year, with further opening-up in its equity and stock markets, SAFE said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "(We will) increase the depth of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, expand trading entities, increase trading tools, expand trading scope, promote market opening, and meet the risk-hedging needs of different entities," the regulator said.\par \par \uc2 China's current account is expected to be "basically balanced" in 2019 as physical goods imports and exports are both likely to rebound and stablise, maintaining a surplus, it said.\par \par \uc2 For 2018, China's final current account surplus came in at $49.1 billion, equivalent to 0.4 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) last year.\par \par \uc2 China had a final current account surplus of $54.6 billion for the fourth quarter, compared to the $23.3 billion surplus in the July-September, according to SAFE. (Reporting by Beijing Monitoring Desk and Yawen Chen; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-3-29T11:48:58.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190329ef3t01yal\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
