par 1.   SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES \par  \par  \par      Statement of compliance \par  \par  \par      The consolidated financial statements have been prepared in accordance with HKFRSs issued by the \par      HKICPA. In addition, the consolidated financial statements include applicable disclosures required by \par      the Rules Governing the Listing of Securities on the Stock Exchange and by the Hong Kong Companies \par      Ordinance. \par  \par  \par      Basis of preparation \par  \par  \par      The consolidated financial statements have been prepared on the historical cost basis except for certain \par      financial instruments that are measured at fair values at the end of each reporting period, as explained in \par      the accounting policies set out below. \par  \par  \par      Historical cost is generally based on the fair value of the consideration given in exchange for goods and \par      services. \par  \par  \par      Fair value is the price that would be received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly \par      transaction between market participants at the measurement date, regardless of whether that price is \par      directly observable or estimated using another valuation technique. In estimating the fair value of an asset \par      or a liability, the Group takes into account the characteristics of the asset or liability if market participants \par      would take those characteristics into account when pricing the asset or liability at the measurement \par      date. Fair value for measurement and/or disclosure purposes in these consolidated financial statements \par      is determined on such a basis, except for share-based payment transactions that are within the scope of \par      HKFRS 2 Share-based Payment, leasing transactions that are within the scope of HKAS 17 Leases, and \par      measurements that have some similarities to fair value but are not fair value, such as net realisable value in \par      HKAS 2 Inventories or value in use in HKAS 36 Impairment of Assets. \par  \par  \par      In addition, for financial reporting purposes, fair value measurements are categorised into Level 1, 2 or 3 \par      based on the degree to which the inputs to the fair value measurements are observable and the significance \par      of the inputs to the fair value measurement in its entirety, which are described as follows: \par  \par  \par            Level 1 inputs are quoted prices (unadjusted) in active markets for identical assets or liabilities that \par             the entity can access at the measurement date; \par  \par  \par            Level 2 inputs are inputs, other than quoted prices included within Level 1, that are observable for \par             the asset or liability, either directly or indirectly; and \par  \par  \par            Level 3 inputs are unobservable inputs for the asset or liability. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                           6 \par The principal accounting policies are set out below. \par  \par  \par Basis of consolidation \par  \par  \par The consolidated financial statements incorporate the financial statements of the Company and entities \par (including structured entities) controlled by the Company and its subsidiaries. Control is achieved when \par the Company: \par  \par  \par      has power over the investee; \par  \par  \par      is exposed, or has rights, to variable returns from its involvement with the investee; and \par  \par  \par      has the ability to use its power to affect its returns. \par  \par  \par The Group reassesses whether or not it controls an investee if facts and circumstances indicate that there \par are changes to one or more of the three elements of control listed above. \par  \par  \par Consolidation of a subsidiary begins when the Group obtains control over the subsidiary and ceases when \par the Group loses control of the subsidiary. Specifically, income and expenses of a subsidiary acquired \par or disposed of during the year are included in the consolidated statement of profit or loss and other \par comprehensive income from the date the Group gains control until the date when the Group ceases to \par control the subsidiary. \par  \par  \par Profit or loss and each item of other comprehensive income are attributed to the owners of the Company \par and to the non-controlling interests. Total comprehensive income of subsidiaries is attributed to the owners \par of the Company and to the non-controlling interests even if this results in the non-controlling interests \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Mar 2019 04:49 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shandong Weigao Group Medical Polymer Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par having a deficit balance. \par  \par  \par When necessary, adjustments are made to the financial statements of subsidiaries to bring their accounting \par policies into line with the Group's accounting policies. \par  \par  \par All intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows relating to transactions \par between members of the Group are eliminated in full on consolidation. \par  \par  \par Non-controlling interests in subsidiaries are presented separately from the Group's equity therein, which \par represent present ownership interests entitling their holders to a proportionate share of net assets of the \par relevant subsidiaries upon liquidation. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                    7 \par      Changes in the Group 's interests in existing subsidiaries \par  \par  \par      Changes in the Group's interests in subsidiaries that do not result in the Group losing control over the \par      subsidiaries are accounted for as equity transactions. The carrying amounts of the Group's relevant \par      components of equity and the non-controlling interests are adjusted to reflect the changes in their relative \par      interests in the subsidiaries, including re-attribution of relevant reserves between the Group and the non- \par      controlling interests according to the Group's and the non-controlling interests' proportionate interests. \par  \par  \par      Any difference between the amount by which the non-controlling interests are adjusted, and the fair \par      value of the consideration paid or received is recognised directly in equity and attributed to owners of the \par      Company. \par  \par  \par      When the Group loses control of a subsidiary, the assets and liabilities of that subsidiary and non- \par      controlling interest (if any) are derecognised. A gain or loss is recognised in profit or loss and is calculated \par      as the difference between (i) the aggregate of the fair value of the consideration received and the fair \par      value of any retained interest and (ii) the previous carrying amount of the assets (including goodwill), \par      and liabilities attributable to the owners of the Company. All amounts previously recognised in other \par      comprehensive income in relation to that subsidiary are accounted for as if the Group had directly \par      disposed of the related assets or liabilities of the subsidiary (i.e. reclassified that to profit or loss or \par      transferred to another category of equity as specified/permitted by applicable HKFRSs). The fair value \par      of any investment retained in the former subsidiary at the date when control is lost is regarded as the fair \par      value on initial recognition for subsequent accounting under HKFRS 9/HKAS 39, when applicable, the \par      cost on initial recognition of an investment in an associate or a joint venture. \par  \par  \par 2.   REVENUE AND SEGMENT INFORMATION \par  \par  \par      The Group is principally engaged in the research and development, production and sale of medical device \par      products, orthopaedic products, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventional\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  products, pharma packaging products, blood management \par      products, and operates financing business. \par  \par  \par      For management purposes, the Group is currently organised into six operating divisions - medical device \par      products, orthopaedic products, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventional\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  products, pharma packaging products, blood management \par      products and others, among which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventional\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  products were produced by Argon. These divisions are \par      the basis of the internal reports about components of the Group that are regularly reviewed by the chief \par      operating decision maker (Managing Director) in order to allocate resources to segments and to assess \par      their performance. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                         8 \par Principal activities of the Group's operating and reportable segments are as follows: \par  \par  \par Medical device products               -    production and sale of clinical care, wound management, \par                                               medical testing, anesthesia and surgical related products and \par                                               other consumables \par Orthopaedic products                  -    production and sale of orthopaedic products \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Interventional\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  products               -    production and sale of tumour and vascular \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventional\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par                                               products \par Pharma packaging products             -    production and sale of pre-filled syringes and flushing syringes \par Blood management products             -    production and sale of blood collection, storage, separation and \par                                               sterilization products \par Others                                -    finance lease and factoring business \par  \par  \par In 2017, the Group was organised into three operating divisions - single use medical device products, \par orthopaedic products, and others. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regarding the manufacture and sale of blood purification \par  \par products was discontinued. The reported segment information does not include any amounts for this \par discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which are described in more details in note 7. \par  \par  \par The comparative amounts of segment information have been reclassified to conform to the current year's \par classification of operating segments and presentation. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                  9 \par Segment revenues and results \par  \par  \par The following is an analysis of the Group's revenue and results by operating and reportable segments: \par  \par  \par 2018 \par  \par  \par Continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par                                          Medical                                    Pharma          Blood \par                                            device   Orthopaedic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Interventional\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     packaging   management \par                                          products      products      products      products      products     Others   Eliminations       Total \par                                         RMB '000      RMB '000      RMB '000      RMB '000      RMB '000    RMB '000      RMB '000     RMB '000 \par  \par Revenue \par                                         5,010,655     1,180,563      1,260,026      955,609       402,008          -              -    8,808,861 \par    External sales \par                              