found around 0.7090 where 10 & 21-day MAs converge \u8226\'20\'22 Support at March 20 low at 0.7057 with break targeting trend low at 0.7003 GBP/USD-Soft into a big week for Brexit in parliament \u8226\'20\'22 -0.1% in a 1.3185/1.3220 range - a flurry as Asia fully opened then quiet \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/GBP was GBP led, modest interest after the Tokyo open, 0.8540/63 range \u8226\'20\'22 Should be an interesting day in parliament - vote on amendments \u8226\'20\'22 Breakthrough needed to avoid a hard Brexit, though a long shot \u8226\'20\'22 Trades at familiar levels with conflicting momentum studies, 5, 10 & 21 DMAs \u8226\'20\'22 Chart shows little bias, Ldn 1.3081 low & NY 1.3224 high support/resistance Unwanted no-deal Brexit appears under-priced UK PM May's strategy of keeping no Brexit on the table and forcing parliament to decide between her deal with the \uc2 EU\uc2 , no deal or an as yet unknown consensus position - which has repeatedly proved impossible - remains in play. Her deal does not have the numbers and entrenched views remain, making a hard Brexit appear under-priced. GBP bears have the best value in EUR/GBP. Longs here around 0.8550, with a stop below 0.8460/70, the March trend low and 38.2 percent of the 2015-16 rise, looking for a bounce towards 0.8800 provide good risk-reward if May's deal goes to the wire. Last week saw intraday GBP volatility, but no major moves on a closing basis, while IMM specs slashed GBP shorts. The weekend news was again dominated by Conservative Party manoeuvring. Reports of a leadership challenge were denied but Eurosceptics repeated their demand for May's resignation date in order to support her deal. Parliament will search for consensus through indicative votes according to The Telegraph, or amendments. Remain supporters staged huge demonstrations on Saturday and their petition broke through the 5 million barrier, keeping a second referendum in the mix. Few want a hard Brexit, but a real breakthrough is needed to avoid it. Week Ahead- Brexit in focus as global growth worries swirl Brexit and the trials and tribulations of UK PM May continue to hog the global spotlight. The \uc2 EU\uc2  gave the UK just a two-week extension to force another parliamentary vote on May's Brexit deal. British lawmakers are due to debate the government's next steps Monday to try to find a majority for an alternative way forward. If her deal is voted on a third time this week and passes parliament, the \uc2 EU\uc2  will extend the departure date to May 22. Given that it has been overwhelmingly rejected the first two times, passing the deal remains a tall order. There will be a great deal of uncertainty if the next vote ends with a failure and the market may have to start pricing back in the chance of a no-deal Brexit. Markets ended last week on a sour note as global growth fears returned to dominate sentiment. Investors are questioning whether the strong gains in risk assets and growth-related commodities since the start of 2019 can continue as major central banks and bond markets scream out warnings of a global slowdown. The data calendar is relatively limited this week, so it will be interesting to see if investors start looking for excuses to sell risk. Week Ahead- US GDP & PCE, German Ifo, Japan IP, China PMIs With the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  clearly in data-dependent mode and worried about global growth, upcoming U.S. data will determine if the concerns are justified. The key U.S. data this week includes final Q4 GDP, the core PCE Price Index, consumer confidence, Chicago PMI and \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  consumer sentiment. There will also be a bevy of housing data including building permits, CaseShiller house prices, pending home sales and new home sales. Euro zone data includes the German Ifo, EZ sentiment data, German CPI and retail sales. The key data in the UK this week will be Q4 GDP and current account. Japan's calendar includes Tokyo CPI, employment data, industrial output and retail sales. China is due to release industrial profits on Wednesday and the NBS manufacturing and services PMIs on Sunday. The only Australian data in the week ahead is housing and private sector credit. New Zealand has trade data and an RBNZ decision. Canada is due to release trade and GDP data.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-3-28T04:48:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190325ef3p00gwd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
