\par\par \b \uc2 U.S., China Near  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Deal; Beijing Vows Not to Devalue Yuan to Help Exports\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei and Chao Deng \par \uc2 890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par 01:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BEIJING -- China's top central banker signaled that Beijing and Washington are getting close to reaching a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deal as part of their continuing negotiations to settle a yearlong trade battle.\par \par \uc2 "Both sides have reached consensus on many crucial and important issues, " \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2  said at a news conference Sunday, held on the sidelines of the nation's annual legislative session.\par \par \uc2 As part of the accord, Mr. Yi said, China won't engage in competitive devaluation to give Chinese exporters a leg up in foreign markets -- a commitment Chinese officials have also made in multilateral agreements such as those among the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  industrial nations. "This is what we promised," he emphasized. "Absolutely we won't do this."\par \par \uc2 How China manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a point of contention between Washington and Beijing for years. President Trump was vocal throughout his election campaign in saying China manipulates the yuan to gain an advantage in trade by making its products relatively cheaper. Mr. Trump repeated the charge last year, when the yuan's plunge aroused suspicion that Beijing was pushing down its value to offset U.S. tariffs on Chinese products.\par \par \uc2 In recent trade talks, U.S. negotiators pushed their Chinese counterparts to keep the yuan stable and to improve transparency in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . During a congressional hearing late last month, U.S. Trade Representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  said "we have spent a lot of time on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the agreement will be enforceable."\par \par \uc2 Still, how the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deal, if reached, will be enforced remains unclear. Both sides are haggling over an enforcement mechanism for the broader trade agreement. A senior Chinese trade negotiator, Wang Shouwen, told reporters Saturday that any such system must be "two-way, fair and equal."\par \par \uc2 At Sunday's news conference, Mr. Yi said both sides have discussed how they should disclose information when they buy or sell in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets according to rules set by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 . Another point of discussion, he said, involves how both nations should respect the autonomy of each other's monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 That latter issue is of deepening concern to the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  because it doesn't want a pledge to keep the yuan steady to tie its hands in supporting economic growth by loosening credit policy. Letting more yuan funds flow into the economy could pressure the value of the yuan.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  mainly looks at domestic factors when setting monetary policy, Mr. Yi said. "The exchange rate isn't a major factor in domestic monetary policy."\par \par \uc2 White House economic adviser \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  on Sunday cited the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments as evidence that the two sides are making progress in concluding a trade deal. He said he was "bullish" that the two sides would finish an agreement this month or next. The two sides are "working out some final difficult points," he said on Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Talks recently hit a snag when Beijing balked at setting up a trade summit between President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and President Trump at Mr. Trump's Mar-A-Lago, Florida resort. Chinese officials, watching the failed summit with North Korea, fear Mr. Trump might embarrass Mr. Xi by making take-it-or-leave-it demands.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts and investors note that China's promise not to engage in competitive devaluation isn't much of a concession to the U.S. For a decade, China allowed a largely undervalued yuan to appreciate. After that appreciation trajectory ended in 2015, the yuan started to come under greater pressure, and the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling too much.\par \par \uc2 Too sharp of a depreciation, Chinese officials fear, could prod businesses and investors to move money offshore, potentially destabilizing a financial system already struggling with rising debt woes.\par \par \uc2 At the media briefing, Mr. Yi pointed to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's effort to keep the yuan stable in recent years, noting that it had burned through $1 trillion of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Chinese central banker also sought to temper investors' expectations for more aggressive monetary easing to prop up the slowing economy. A "neutral" monetary stance, Mr. Yi said, means that China will keep its overall leverage ratio stable.\par \par \uc2 In the past year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the amount of cash banks must keep in reserve five times, in a bid to unleash more funds for lenders to make loans. There is still room for such reserve-requirement reductions this year, Mr. Yi said, though the room has narrowed.\par \par \uc2 He also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to foster competition among banks as a way to lower lending rates, but he gave no indication that any reduction in benchmark interest rates are in the cards. At the end of last year, credit amounted to 249.4% of China's gross domestic product, he said, which was 1.5 percentage points lower than the level as of the end of 2017.\par \par \uc2 --Bob Davis contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com and Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 11-03-19 0058GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020190311ef3b0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
