\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  c.bank has exited from  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 142 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par 05:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, March 10 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has basically exited from regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the yuan exchange rate is not a major concern when deciding monetary policy, the head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 China will absolutely not use the exchange rate to boost its exports, nor will the country use exchange rates as a tool in trade frictions, \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 Governor \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2  said at a press conference on the sidelines of the country's annual meeting of parliament.\par \par \uc2 Yi also said China will keep the yuan basically stable, and will unwaveringly push forward financial opening up according to its own timetable and needs of reform. (Reporting by Beijing Monitoring Desk; editing by Christian Schmollinger) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-3-10T05:04:33.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190310ef3a005h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
