\par\par \b \uc2 China state planner's yuan flexibility pledge sparks speculation\b0\par\par\uc2 511 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par 10:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, March 5 (Reuters) - A pledge by China's state planner to increase the flexibility of the yuan's exchange rate set off market speculation Tuesday that a tweak to official wording could mean changes to the country's tightly managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.\par \par \uc2 The pledge came in a report by the \uc2 National Development and Reform Commission (NDRC) \uc2 on the first day of the annual meeting of China's parliament, alongside a reiteration of China's intention to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability.\par \par \uc2 China will "continue to improve the exchange rate formation mechanism, increase the yuan's exchange rate flexibility, strengthen regulations on cross-border capital flows, and keep the yuan's exchange rate basically stable at reasonable levels," the NDRC report said.\par \par \uc2 The annual session of the National People's Congress (\uc2 NPC\uc2 ) is one of the most important economic and political events in China, setting out the government agenda for the year. Delegates pass major bills, approve the budget and endorse personnel nominations.\par \par \uc2 Raymond Yeung, chief economist for Greater China at ANZ in Hong Kong, said markets could interpret the mention of increased flexibility to indicate an openness to "competitive devaluation" of the yuan.\par \par \uc2 In late February, the \uc2 United States government\uc2  was reported to be pressing China to pledge not to devalue the yuan as part of a deal to end their trade war.\par \par \uc2 But Yeung said in a note Tuesday that a significant change in China's policy was unlikely.\par \par \uc2 He said such a change would require China to let the yuan float as cross-border flows are liberalised, in line with a principle known as the "Impossible Trinity" that a country cannot simultaneously achieve free capital movement, independent monetary policy and a stable exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 DEVALUATION IN 2015\par \par \uc2 Yeung said China's policy of exchange-rate flexibility in fact began with a sharp one-off devaluation of the yuan in August 2015, a move that roiled global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the time attributed the move to allowing market forces to play a bigger role in deciding the yuan's value.\par \par \uc2 State Council Research Office Director Huang Shouhong on Tuesday said China was not changing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and that it has been making the yuan more flexible.\par \par \uc2 According to Ken Cheung, senior Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  in Hong Kong, the NDRC's pledge of flexibility could even lead to a stronger, rather than a weaker, yuan this year as the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 reduces \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If our interpretation is correct, higher \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility will be biased toward one-way in appreciation rather than two-way," Cheung said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the speculation, China's yuan remained relatively unchanged on Tuesday. The onshore spot yuan finished the domestic session at 6.7038 per dollar, slightly firmer than the previous late night close of 6.7101 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has strengthened 2.5 percent against the dollar this year. (Reporting by Winni Zhou and Andrew Galbraith; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-3-5T10:16:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190305ef3501blx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
