\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar May Remain Defensive -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par 10:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0915 GMT - The greenback's post-GDP gains may build near-term versus the Singapore dollar, says \uc2 UOB\uc2 . "While the rapid rise appears to be running ahead of itself, there is scope" to test S$1.3540, the investment bank notes. That as it doubts the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inherent strength, pointing to recent comments by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief on no need for policy stimulus ahead of April biannual policy meeting. The greenback has risen slightly in Asian trading to S$1.3527. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0832 GMT - Though China's economic growth in 2018 was the slowest since 1990 and it may decelerate further this year, CEOs there remain upbeat. PwC's 22nd annual global CEO survey shows 73% of mainland China chieftains are optimistic about global growth in the coming year. That figure is 42% globally, and 41% in Hong Kong. China is also "the only economy among the major territories surveyed who were more optimistic this year than the last," the consultancy notes. But PwC thinks the executives "are likely downplaying fears to maintain stability and confidence as a way to cushion the direct impact of the trade dispute." (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0829 GMT - Swiss consumers started 2019 on weak footing, with retail sales falling 0.3% in January in both monthly and annual terms. Despite very low unemployment that should support confidence and spending, Switzerland is still heavily dependent on exports for growth. EUR/CHF was last flat at 1.1357. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT - Spain's economic challenges shouldn't be underestimated in the run-up to elections, \uc2 Natixis\uc2  analyst Jesus Castillo says. Debt levels are still high in the public and private sectors, as is unemployment, while the labor market is still beset by structural problems. Spain will hold snap elections on April 28, followed by European, regional and municipal elections on May 26. "Spain's main political parties will focus rather more on issues to do with internal politics to stand out from their rivals," Castillo says. Internal political issues include Catalonia's independence efforts and immigration. "As the significant fragmentation characterizing the Congress of Deputies is likely to remain a feature, this raises questions about the next government's capacity to push through the many reforms that are necessary," Castillo says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT - The 10-year German Bund yields are moving away from the 0% level, having risen this week, but the question remains how much further the correction can go, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  . The 10-year Bund yield is trading at 0.19%, having risen from 0.093% last Friday, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . Bond yields move inversely to prices. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  's strategists say that data on Friday are unlikely to provide many impulses. While the eurozone inflation is expected to have edged higher in February, this move will mostly be driven by energy and is unlikely to add much new information after Thursday's releases of national inflation data, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. "That said, a breach of 0.20% level should bring yields pave the way for a test of the 0.30% area," it adds. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0755 GMT - Eurozone government bond yields remain subjected to upward pressure as global yields keep rising after stronger-than-expected US gross domestic product numbers, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  analyst Daniel Brodsgaard says. On the data front, flash estimate February eurozone inflation is due at 1000 GMT, with economists in The Wall Street Journal's survey expecting a rise to 1.5% from 1.4% in January, albeit with an unchanged core CPI print of 1.1%. There is no bond supply in the eurozone on Friday but France and Spain will release details of their next bond auctions on March 7. The 10-year Bund yield trades at 0.188%, up 0.5 basis points, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . Bond yields trade inversely to prices. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT - \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  is likely to upgrade Greece in a scheduled review later in the day, and such a move could pave the way for a new 10-year government bond issue, say \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategists. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  currently rates Greece at 'B3' with a positive outlook. "A one-notch upgrade seems likely today, with the last ratings action one year old and Greece having proved its market access via the five-year transaction," according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . The 10-year Greek bond yield trades at 3.66%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . This is the lowest level in more a year, opening the door for another Greek bond syndication as early as next week, with a 10-year bond most likely, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's strategists say. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT - The Chinese yuan this week has seen some of its strongest levels since mid-2018 versus the dollar. DBS doesn't seem the gain getting extended with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  having reportedly agreed to disclose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of a US trade deal. That should erode the case that the yuan will be led higher, the investment bank contends. And being more market-determined means the yuan shouldn't be able "to buck any depreciation pressure when the USD becomes strong again globally." DBS sees the dollar moving back toward CNY6.9 if the ICE Dollar Index rises toward 100. They're respectively at CNY6.7025 and 96.30. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT - The Nikkei notched an 11-week closing high, helped today by drug and cosmetics stocks logging gains of 4-6%. The Japanese benchmark rose 1% to 21602.69, allowing the index to rise 0.8% for the week. It's the 3rd-straight gain on that basis and 7th in the past 8 weeks. Electronics and financial stocks also did well today as concerns eased about US economic strength. Just 7 of the Topix's 33 subindexes fell as the dollar climbed to Y111.76 from Y111.38 late Thursday in New York and Y110.90 this time yesterday in Japan. Meanwhile, 10-year JGB yields rose 1.5 basis points to -0.015%. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT - Malaysian export growth likely slowed sharply in January. The median forecast of 8 economists polled by WSJ is for a 0.9% increase from a year earlier, versus December's 4.8% gain. Bank Islam Malaysia chief economist \uc2 Mohd Afzanizam Abdul Rashid\uc2  tells WSJ that business sentiment is very cautious--not good for demand, especially semiconductors. Meanwhile, import growth is projected to have ticked up to 1.3% from December's 1%. As such, Malaysia's trade surplus is forecasted to have dropped to MYR9.25 billion ($2.27 billion) in January from MYR10.43 billion a month earlier. The data are due Monday. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT - Phillip Lowe is 2 1/2 years into his 7-year tenure leading \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  . In the first third of that term, the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's policy rate has remained at a record-low 1.5%. Some had been betting that would remain so for up to 2 more years before a rate hikes start. But with recent economic data, some instead think the next move will be lower, perhaps by year-ends. \uc2 Citi\uc2  says unemployment expected downtrend could be so slight that the \uc2 RBA\uc2  may keep rates unchanged for any meaningful forecast period. The investment bank doesn't see a strong case for a hike or cut the next few years, but says the labor market will remain the swing factor thanks from whether muted wage growth picks up any and possibly helps fuel broader inflation. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0526 GMT - Amid India's weakest economic growth since 2Q17 at 6.6% last quarter, the extent of weakness in the agriculture sector was more than expected, says \uc2 Citi\uc2 . Rural distress remains a key concern for the government, which announced some supportive measures in the last month's budget. Weakness in private and government consumption also outweighed a pickup in investments and exports. \uc2 Citi\uc2  thinks the GDP slowdown, coupled with lower inflation, could lead to another interest-rate cut. Many had already been predicting one for April's \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 01, 2019 04:15 ET (09:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190301ef31000wm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
