\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Unlikely to Rise Further -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,226 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par 08:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0725 GMT - The Chinese yuan this week has seen some of its strongest levels since mid-2018 versus the dollar. DBS doesn't seem the gain getting extended with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  having reportedly agreed to disclose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of a US trade deal. That should erode the case that the yuan will be led higher, the investment bank contends. And being more market-determined means the yuan shouldn't be able "to buck any depreciation pressure when the USD becomes strong again globally." DBS sees the dollar moving back toward CNY6.9 if the ICE Dollar Index rises toward 100. They're respectively at CNY6.7025 and 96.30. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT - The Nikkei notched an 11-week closing high, helped today by drug and cosmetics stocks logging gains of 4-6%. The Japanese benchmark rose 1% to 21602.69, allowing the index to rise 0.8% for the week. It's the 3rd-straight gain on that basis and 7th in the past 8 weeks. Electronics and financial stocks also did well today as concerns eased about US economic strength. Just 7 of the Topix's 33 subindexes fell as the dollar climbed to Y111.76 from Y111.38 late Thursday in New York and Y110.90 this time yesterday in Japan. Meanwhile, 10-year JGB yields rose 1.5 basis points to -0.015%. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT - Malaysian export growth likely slowed sharply in January. The median forecast of 8 economists polled by WSJ is for a 0.9% increase from a year earlier, versus December's 4.8% gain. Bank Islam Malaysia chief economist \uc2 Mohd Afzanizam Abdul Rashid\uc2  tells WSJ that business sentiment is very cautious--not good for demand, especially semiconductors. Meanwhile, import growth is projected to have ticked up to 1.3% from December's 1%. As such, Malaysia's trade surplus is forecasted to have dropped to MYR9.25 billion ($2.27 billion) in January from MYR10.43 billion a month earlier. The data are due Monday. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT - Phillip Lowe is 2 1/2 years into his 7-year tenure leading \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  . In the first third of that term, the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's policy rate has remained at a record-low 1.5%. Some had been betting that would remain so for up to 2 more years before a rate hikes start. But with recent economic data, some instead think the next move will be lower, perhaps by year-ends. \uc2 Citi\uc2  says unemployment expected downtrend could be so slight that the \uc2 RBA\uc2  may keep rates unchanged for any meaningful forecast period. The investment bank doesn't see a strong case for a hike or cut the next few years, but says the labor market will remain the swing factor thanks from whether muted wage growth picks up any and possibly helps fuel broader inflation. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0526 GMT - Amid India's weakest economic growth since 2Q17 at 6.6% last quarter, the extent of weakness in the agriculture sector was more than expected, says \uc2 Citi\uc2 . Rural distress remains a key concern for the government, which announced some supportive measures in the last month's budget. Weakness in private and government consumption also outweighed a pickup in investments and exports. \uc2 Citi\uc2  thinks the GDP slowdown, coupled with lower inflation, could lead to another interest-rate cut. Many had already been predicting one for April's \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT - Despite a pickup in consumer prices in Thailand and a hawkish tone out of the last \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting, DBS says recent outperformance for the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and trade-related risks will likely keep policymakers' hands tied. The baht has risen 2.2% this year, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next meets on March 20. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0410 GMT - It's enough for a Dan Brown conspiracy novel--only this time involving the \uc2 RBA\uc2  and not the Vatican. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Lowe will speak Wednesday morning on the housing market, a critical issue for policymakers. Wedged between Tuesday's monthly policy meeting and Wednesday's 4Q GDP data, traders will be on edge for any hint the \uc2 RBA\uc2  is further evolving its policy position. \uc2 Lowe\uc2  has demonstrated a preference to signal policy changes in speeches recently, and it's a good chance he will have had a heads-up on the GDP report. This could be the event of a busy week coming Down Under. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0352 GMT - Inflation has slowed to the point that CPI may come in at the lowest level since 2000 in February at 2.6%, says ANZ. Curbing gains have been food and energy prices. It means interest rates are liable to remain steady for some time, coming as the rupiah has stabilized from the tumult seen during 2018. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT - GDP reports are often big. But Australia's 4Q reading due Wednesday may confirm that there's been a noted slowdown in the country's economy--which many expect--and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been overly optimistic. The \uc2 RBA\uc2  had been disbelieving of a sharp slowdown until recently, when it move to a neutral policy bias from a cautiously hawkish one. Another soft quarter for consumers and growth more broadly will stoke talk of a possible 2019 interest-rate cuts. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0320 GMT - Tensions between New Delhi and Islamabad are unlikely to have any major dent on the Indian economy as the state of Jammu & Kashmir accounts for less than 1% of the country's economic output, notes \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . "We also think that the impact of the crisis on financial markets will remain small," though it adds political risk will weigh on equities ahead of the upcoming election. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT - Thailand price growth is projected to have picked up last month amid rebounds in oil and food. The median estimate of 7 economists polled by WSJ is for CPI to have risen 0.6% from a year earlier, versus January's 0.3%. Sequential growth is put at 0.3%. Excluding energy and fresh food, CPI is forecasted to have risen 0.7% and 0.1%, respectively. The data are due later today. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT - Hong Kong-listed shares of property developers haven't fared well in light of yesterday's announcement of planned land sales for the coming year. But the fewer residential tracts set to be offered "is in line with the government's 2018 policy address," notes real-estate-services firm \uc2 JLL\uc2 . At the same time, it says if all 7 commercial sites in the announcement are sold, "the combined 8.8 million square feet of floor area that can be developed would represent the most commercial-land supply provided in a year" during the past 20. Even so, "Hong Kong's Grade A office market will remain supply-constrained" for now. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 01, 2019 02:25 ET (07:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190301ef31000kx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
