\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2019\par 10:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT - Switzerland's manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  rose 1.1 points in February to 55.4, breaking a streak of five-straight monthly declines. Though still solidly above the breakeven threshold of 50, the index remains more than nine points below its August level. The services \uc2 PMI\uc2  meanwhile fell 4.1 points to 55.7. The reports suggest Switzerland's economy is growing, but only modestly, to start 2019. Dollar at 1.0004 francs, up 0.2% from Thursday, while the euro rises 0.1% to 1.1368 francs. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT - After rising to multi-month highs this week on optimism that the U.K. will avoid crashing out of the EU, sterling is now vulnerable to data revealing the U.K. economy is deteriorating due to Brexit uncertainty. The purchasing managers' survey on U.K. manufacturing activity for February at 0930 GMT will therefore be watched closely. The consensus in a WSJ poll is for the \uc2 PMI\uc2  to drop to 51.9 from 52.8 in January. ING expects the data will cause "modest downside" to sterling, lifting EUR/GBP toward 0.8600. EUR/GBP was last flat at 0.8574, having fallen as low as 0.8521 on Wednesday. GBP/USD is down 0.1% at 1.3250, below Wednesday's peak of 1.3351. ING notes manufacturing PMIs have been "artificially high due to stockpiling." (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT - The greenback's post-GDP gains may build near-term versus the Singapore dollar, says \uc2 UOB\uc2 . "While the rapid rise appears to be running ahead of itself, there is scope" to test S$1.3540, the investment bank notes. That as it doubts the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inherent strength, pointing to recent comments by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief on no need for policy stimulus ahead of April biannual policy meeting. The greenback has risen slightly in Asian trading to S$1.3527. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0832 GMT - Though China's economic growth in 2018 was the slowest since 1990 and it may decelerate further this year, CEOs there remain upbeat. PwC's 22nd annual global CEO survey shows 73% of mainland China chieftains are optimistic about global growth in the coming year. That figure is 42% globally, and 41% in Hong Kong. China is also "the only economy among the major territories surveyed who were more optimistic this year than the last," the consultancy notes. But PwC thinks the executives "are likely downplaying fears to maintain stability and confidence as a way to cushion the direct impact of the trade dispute." (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0829 GMT - Swiss consumers started 2019 on weak footing, with retail sales falling 0.3% in January in both monthly and annual terms. Despite very low unemployment that should support confidence and spending, Switzerland is still heavily dependent on exports for growth. EUR/CHF was last flat at 1.1357. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT - Spain's economic challenges shouldn't be underestimated in the run-up to elections, \uc2 Natixis\uc2  analyst Jesus Castillo says. Debt levels are still high in the public and private sectors, as is unemployment, while the labor market is still beset by structural problems. Spain will hold snap elections on April 28, followed by European, regional and municipal elections on May 26. "Spain's main political parties will focus rather more on issues to do with internal politics to stand out from their rivals," Castillo says. Internal political issues include Catalonia's independence efforts and immigration. "As the significant fragmentation characterizing the Congress of Deputies is likely to remain a feature, this raises questions about the next government's capacity to push through the many reforms that are necessary," Castillo says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT - The 10-year German Bund yields are moving away from the 0% level, having risen this week, but the question remains how much further the correction can go, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  . The 10-year Bund yield is trading at 0.19%, having risen from 0.093% last Friday, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . Bond yields move inversely to prices. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  's strategists say that data on Friday are unlikely to provide many impulses. While the eurozone inflation is expected to have edged higher in February, this move will mostly be driven by energy and is unlikely to add much new information after Thursday's releases of national inflation data, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. "That said, a breach of 0.20% level should bring yields pave the way for a test of the 0.30% area," it adds. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0806 GMT The June Gilt future contract, currently trading at 125.91, stands below its previous overlap at 126.35 and remains capped by its declining 50-period moving average at 126.31. As a consequence, as long as 126.35 remains resistance, further weakness is expected toward horizontal supports at 125.50 and at 125.00. A third target is set at 124.60. Alternatively, above 126.35, a rebound toward strong horizontal resistance at 126.63 and toward horizontal resistance at 126.94 would be expected.[This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0804 GMT Currently trading at $1.1372, the Euro is under pressure and stands below its 50-period moving average at $1.1381 on a 30-minute chart. From a technical point of view, the 14-period RSI stands within its selling area between 50 and 30 and confirms the bearish bias. As a consequence, as long as $1.1395 holds as a resistance, further weakness is expected toward strong horizontal support and overlap at $1.1355 and toward $1.1335 in extension. A third target is set at Feb. 22 bottom at $1.1315. Only a rebound above $1.1395 would invalidate this bearish view and favour an up move toward Feb. 28 top at $1.1420 and even toward horizontal resistance at $1.1440 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0755 GMT - Eurozone government bond yields remain subjected to upward pressure as global yields keep rising after stronger-than-expected US gross domestic product numbers, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  analyst Daniel Brodsgaard says. On the data front, flash estimate February eurozone inflation is due at 1000 GMT, with economists in The Wall Street Journal's survey expecting a rise to 1.5% from 1.4% in January, albeit with an unchanged core CPI print of 1.1%. There is no bond supply in the eurozone on Friday but France and Spain will release details of their next bond auctions on March 7. The 10-year Bund yield trades at 0.188%, up 0.5 basis points, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . Bond yields trade inversely to prices. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT - \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  is likely to upgrade Greece in a scheduled review later in the day, and such a move could pave the way for a new 10-year government bond issue, say \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategists. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  currently rates Greece at 'B3' with a positive outlook. "A one-notch upgrade seems likely today, with the last ratings action one year old and Greece having proved its market access via the five-year transaction," according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . The 10-year Greek bond yield trades at 3.66%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . This is the lowest level in more a year, opening the door for another Greek bond syndication as early as next week, with a 10-year bond most likely, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's strategists say. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT - The Chinese yuan this week has seen some of its strongest levels since mid-2018 versus the dollar. DBS doesn't seem the gain getting extended with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  having reportedly agreed to disclose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of a US trade deal. That should erode the case that the yuan will be led higher, the investment bank contends. And being more market-determined means the yuan shouldn't be able "to buck any depreciation pressure when the USD becomes strong again globally." DBS sees the dollar moving back toward CNY6.9 if the ICE Dollar Index rises toward 100. They're respectively at CNY6.7025 and 96.30. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 01, 2019 04:26 ET (09:26 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190301ef31000u9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{