\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 2,119 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 2018\par 23:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MARKETS AT A GLANCE \par (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET) \par  \par                          LAST    CHANGE   % CHG \par DJIA                 23138.82    260.37    1.14% \par Nasdaq                 6579.49     28.65    0.44% \par S&P 500               2488.83     21.13    0.86% \par Japan: Nikkei 225    20077.62    750.56    3.88% \par Hang Seng            25478.88   -172.50   -0.67% \par Shanghai Composite    2483.09    -15.21   -0.61% \par S&P BSE  Sensex       35807.28    157.34    0.44% \par Australia: S&P/ASX    5597.20    103.40    1.88% \par UK: FTSE 100          6584.68   -101.31   -1.52% \par  \par                  PRICE CHG  YIELD \par U.S. 2 Year          4/32   2.568 \par U.S. 5 Year          8/32   2.613 \par U.S. 10 Year        10/32   2.772 \par Australia 10 Year    8/32   2.358 \par China 10 Year        4/32   3.331 \par India 10 Year        0/32   7.242 \par Japan 10 Year        1/32   0.023 \par German 10 Year       6/32   0.231 \par  \par                        LAST(MID)  CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)    0.7033   0.0003 \par Yen (USD/JPY)            110.92    -0.06 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW) 1120.15     0.07 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)   6.8663   0.0000 \par Euro (EUR/USD)           1.1432  -0.0001 \par WSJ Dollar Index          90.17    -0.01 \par  \par                LAST   CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil     45.41   -0.81   -1.75% \par Brent Crude   52.88   -1.59   -2.92% \par Gold         1278.1     5.1    0.40% \par  \par SNAPSHOT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks were hiher, with the Dow industrials swinging about 850 points. \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds rose, as investors sought safe assets. The dollar was dollar fell, also due to a push for haven assets. Crude oil fell amid thin trading. And gold settled solidly higher, inching to a fresh six-month high. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPENING CALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intense competition from global bourses, poor post-IPO performance and reduced valuations would weigh on the Hong Kong's IPO market next year, says accounting firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 EY\f28 \uc2 \f2 . It estimated Hong Kong's IPO market to capture HK$200 billion ($25.5 billion) of funds in 2019, nearly one-third lower than this year. \f28 \uc2 \f2 EY\f28 \uc2 \f2 's cautious estimates come amid uncertainties surrounding global growth and trade. Disappointing pro-debut performance has dulled investors' appetite for IPOs, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 EY\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Asia-Pacific IPO leader Ringo Choi. Although Hong Kong reclaimed the top spot of global IPO market this year, the average first-day return for IPOs in the city fell to just 1.2% from the previous year's 15.2%, with as many as 32% of new listings falling on their debut. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks staged a big comeback heading into the closing bell, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average swinging about 850 points to continue a rocky December in the stock market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The blue-chip index added 260 points, or 1.1%, to 23139 after earlier dropping as much as 611 points. The S&P 500 gained 0.9%, and the Nasdaq Composite rose 0.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The gains come a day after the Dow logged its biggest-ever point gain, surging more than 1,000 points. The volatility and massive moves in December have been dizzying - the Dow industrials has risen in just seven of the 18 trading days in December so far.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow had tumbled more than 1,800 points over the four trading days through Christmas Eve, only to recoup a massive chunk of that in a postholiday rally. All told the Dow has lost more than 1,300 points through that stretch.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The damage, which began in earnest in October, has left investors with few places to hide as they continue to confront many of the same issues that had been weighing on stocks since October: a Fed intent on winding down easy-money policies, a continuing trade spat between the U.S. and China and slowing economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Asia, stocks were mixed, with a 3.9% rise of Japan's Nikkei 225 helping the benchmark to log its best one-day percentage gain since 2016.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The jump took cues from a big rally on Wall Street overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Hong Kong's Hang Seng benchmark gave back earlier gains to fall 0.7% after a two-day Christmas break, tracking weakness in China's stock markets after data showed that China's industrial output fell for the first time in almost three years last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Kospi was largely flat while China's Shanghai Composite was down 0.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yusuke Sakai, senior trader at T&D Asset Management, said concerns persisted over the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  's pace of tightening, Mr. Trump's potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in monetary policy and U.S.-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trade friction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Nothing's changed," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei posted its biggest gain in two years to close above 20000, taking a cue from a big rally on Wall Street. Gains were broad-based.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesian shares rebounded as foreign investors bought back shares following the Wall Street'sovernight gains. The JSX index ended 1.0% higher at 6190.643, with 275 gainers and 133 decliners. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell, as gyrations in stocks sent investors into haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WSJ Dollar Index, which measures the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against a basket of 16 others, was recently down 0.3% at 90.19 in late afternoon trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks roared back late, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average adding 260 points, or 1.1%, after earlier dropping as much as 611 points. The rally comes a day after the Dow rose more than 1000 points for its biggest ever one-day gain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The recent volatility has boosted demand for the Japanese yen, a popular destination for nervous investors. The dollar was down 0.3% against the yen, and has fallen 2.2% over the last month. It also fell 0.8% against the Swiss franc. Gold, an asset that some investors turn to when markets become rocky, was up 0.5%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond prices rose, as investors sought safe assets after an early downturn in stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year Treasury note settled at 2.744%, down from 2.797% Wednesday. The yield has fallen 0.488 percentage point since reaching a seven-year high of 3.232% in early November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields, which fall as prices rise, declined as investors' focus returned to signs of slowing global growth, Federal Reserve policy and trade tensions between the U.S. and China. The Dow Jones Industrial Average recovered from a drop of as much as 611 points, rising 260 points, a day after posting its biggest one-day point gain in history.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors are concerned that the Fed's plans to continue raising interest rates next year after four increases this year could lead to a slower pace of growth, or that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision to shrink the size of its balance sheet at a planned pace of $50 billion a month will increase volatility by pulling cash from financial markets. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMMODITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices fell Thursday amid thin trading volumes due to the holiday season.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said the low liquidity had contributed to the particularly volatile trading this week, with prices having recorded their biggest one-day increase in two years on Wednesday, only to fall again.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Light, sweet crude for February delivery ended 3.5% lower at $44.61 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Brent crude, the global oil benchmark, dropped 4.2%, at $52.16 a barrel, on London's Intercontinental Exchange , having earlier sunk to $50.36 earlier in the week, its lowest level since August 2017.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, gold futures settled solidly higher, inching to a fresh six-month high as the dollar eased and U.S. stocks retreated following a surge the day after Christmas.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold for February delivery on Comex rose $8.10, or 0.6%, to $1,281.10 an ounce. The contract has closed higher for three consecutive sessions, marking the highest finishes for a most-active contract since June, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 FactSet\f28 \uc2 \f2  data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bullion's moves come as stocks fall sharply following a major bounce a day earlier from the heaviest Christmas Eve losses on record. European stock markets also closed sharply lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, a leading U.S. dollar index that measures the buck against a half-dozen rivals, eased back by 0.6% to 96.46. Weakness in U.S. dollars can make gold, which is primarily traded in greenback, more attractive to investors using other monetary units.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Gold prices are trading toward their six-month high as investors factor in all the risk despite the fact that we have seen a stellar rally in the equity markets yesterday," said Naeem Aslam, chief market analyst with Think Markets UK, in a note. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S HEADLINES \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Huawei\f28 \uc2 \f2  Reports Bumper 2018 Sales Despite Setbacks in the West \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chinese tech company said it expects 21% revenue growth for 2018 despite having spent much of the year fighting cybersecurity challenges by the U.S. and its allies. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sen. Johnny Isakson Called \f28 \uc2 \f2 FBI\f28 \uc2 \f2   on Behalf of \f28 \uc2 \f2 MiMedx Group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sen. Johnny Isakson called the \f28 \uc2 \f2 FBI\f28 \uc2 \f2  in 2017 to follow up on a complaint lodged by the founder of biomedical company \f28 \uc2 \f2 MiMedx Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  against an investor who criticized the company and its founder. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Chill From Beijing Buffets China's Tech Sector \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After years of thriving under Beijing's relatively light hand, China's fast-growing technology sector is facing an unfamiliar situation: stepped-up government scrutiny. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ghosn Investigators Focus on Ties to Saudi Businessman \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest investigation by Tokyo prosecutors into \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carlos Ghosn\f28 \uc2 \f2  centers on his relationship with a Saudi Arabian businessman, Khaled Al Juffali, who runs part of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Middle East business. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 McDonald\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Bets on Breakfast-Again \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 McDonald's\f28 \uc2 \f2  is adding meatier sandwiches and $1 sandwiches to its breakfast menu, focusing on mornings to address a softening in U.S. sales and rivals' new breakfast offerings. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Markets Aren't Helping \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Shake Off Bad News \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment banking is getting more difficult, just as the German lender needs support. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Beleaguered \f28 \uc2 \f2 JD.com\f28 \uc2 \f2   Plans Revamp \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese e-commerce giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 JD.com\f28 \uc2 \f2  is revamping \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in what analysts said is a bid to calm investors about the company's plunging stock price and heavy reliance on its founder, who won't face sex-assault charges in the U.S. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par India's Tighter E-Commerce Rules Frustrate \f28 \uc2 \f2 Amazon\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Walmart\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plans \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign companies will no longer be allowed to sell products from their own affiliated companies in India, a new challenge to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Amazon\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Walmart\f28 \uc2 \f2  as they bet billions on the nascent market. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Consumer Confidence Fades in December \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Dec 2018 17:32 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Higher -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Conference Board 's consumer confidence index for American households fell in December, weighed down by weaker expectations for economic growth and heightened market volatility. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par My Lessons From the Market's Wild December \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the shadow of this month's market storm, our investment columnist assesses what he got right and wrong this year, and gives his advice on markets for 2019. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES \par Sen. Johnny Isakson called \f28 \uc2 \f2 FBI\f28 \uc2 \f2   on Behalf of \f28 \uc2 \f2 MiMedx Group\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par U.K. Investigates Leaks in Insider Trading Probe \par Head of Underwriter Defends SoftBank's Troubled Listing \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par (Times in GMT, followed by country and event) \par Friday, Dec. 28, 2018 \par 0100  Japan        November   Steel Imports & Exports Statistics \par 0300  South Korea  November   Net barter terms of trade \par 0400  Japan        October    Auto exports \par 0400  Japan        October    Auto production \par 0430  J