\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Latest on Trump, U.S. Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 1,185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2018\par 11:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and U.S. politics. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0512 ET - The dollar likely will rise versus the Chinese yuan and the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely "will gear up for more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect 7 [level in USD/CNY] until 2019," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is due to meet China President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  at the G20 summit, which starts on Friday. The two leaders are expected to discuss trade relations and hopes are fading that Mr. Trump will abandon the aim to raise tariffs to 25% from 10% on Chinese imports from January. "The bar is low for a positive surprise," SocGen says. "The dollar has weakened after the last two G20 summits," and the "stakes this time are considerably higher." USD/CNY is last up 0.1% at 6.9490. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 ET - The uncertainty over whether the U.K. parliament will vote down the Brexit withrawal deal continues to weigh on the pound, which falls to a 12-day low of $1.2728. The vote is due for Dec. 12 and analysts have warned that sterling is likely to be volatile in the next couple of weeks. U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's comments add to the pressure, with Mr. Trump reportedly suggesting Theresa May 's Brexit agreement could threaten a U.S.-U.K. trade deal. This goes against the insistance of Mrs. May's team that it was "very clear" the U.K. would be able to sign trade deals with countries around the world, the BBC reported. EUR/GBP is up 0.4% at 0.8873. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0313 ET - The dollar is flat on Tuesday, despite U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying in an interview with the WSJ that he expected to go ahead with increasing tariff levels on $200 of Chinese goods to 25% next year, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains largely unimpeded by trade tensions. Trump also said he would put tariffs on remaining Chinese imports if negotiations weren't favorable for the U.S. RBC says this statement is "seen as part and parcel of Trump's negotiating style, piling on the pressure ahead of a crucial meeting," at the G20 summit, which starts on Friday. The DXY dollar index trades flat at 97.11. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0239 ET - The FTSE 100 index is expected to open 6 points lower at 7030, \uc2 London Capital Group\uc2  says, as hopes of breakthrough in U.S.-China trade talks at the upcoming G20 summit fade, leaving investors risk-averse. U.S. President Trump said he expects to go ahead with increasing tariffs on Chinese imports to 25% in January. "His comments reflect how little progress has been made in trade talks so far and highlights the low probability for any deal to be agreed with China's President Xi," says \uc2 Jasper Lawler\uc2 , analyst at LCG. Weaker oil prices may hurt heavyweight oil stocks while travel-related stocks may suffer after holiday company Thomas Cook gave a profit warning for FY 2018 and suspended its dividend. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1653 ET - \uc2 UBS\uc2  says don't read too much into Trump's remarks to WSJ that iPhones could face 10% tariffs starting in January, saying they're likely a negotiating tactic ahead of the G-20 summit later this week. The interview, published following the end of regular stock-market trading, sent shares of \uc2 Apple\uc2  down 2.1% after-hours. The investment bank estimates that 10% tariffs would lower its current FY EPS view for \uc2 Apple\uc2  ($13.06) by 33 cents. (tripp.mickle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2013 ET - The ongoing trade dispute between China and the US--which has led to the imposition of tariffs on grain imports-- combined with excellent growing weather will keep US milk prices anchored below recent peaks, says \uc2 Fitch Solutions\uc2  in a note; "Strong US production owing to good producer margins will prevent another significant rally in milk prices over the coming months." It, however, maintains its CME milk price forecasts and continues to expect them to average marginally higher over the coming years with prices averaging US$14.50/hundredweight in 2018 and US$15.00/hundredweight in 2019, rising modestly to US$15.75/hundredweight in 2021 and 2022. \uc2 CME\uc2  November Class III Milk Futures settled at $14.48/hundredweight. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1713 ET - The Trump administration's latest plan to tackle drug prices doesn't appear to include imminent changes to how Medicare treats rebates, providing relief to insurers and pharmacy benefit managers. The government is seeking, however, to use other discounts--those provided by pharmacies to health-insurers--to lower patients' out-of-pocket costs by as much as $9.2B over a decade. The initiative would also save drugmakers as much as $5.8B over 10 years by reducing the discounts they provide to certain high-cost beneficiaries. \uc2 CVS\uc2  gains 0.2% after-hours while \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2  is flat. (joseph.walker@wsj.com; @joewalkerwsj)\par \par \uc2 1627 ET - New proposed regulations from the Internal Revenue Service and Treasury Department will help companies calculate whether they face limits on deducting their business interest costs. The rules will have their biggest effect on highly leveraged companies. They include provisions that attempt to define interest in ways that companies can't work around by creating economically similar arrangements. (richard.rubin@wsj.com; @RichardRubinDC)\par \par \uc2 1007 ET - The \uc2 Financial Stability Board\uc2  made it official Monday, appointing Federal Reserve Vice Chairman for Supervision \uc2 Randal Quarles\uc2  as its new chair for a three-year term starting Dec. 2. The US push to make him head of the global group of financial regulators had been in danger due to concerns about President Trump's posture toward international institutions. Now the US will be engaged at the highest levels of the FSB. \uc2 Dutch \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Klaas Knot\uc2  will serve as FSB vice chair alongside Quarles and then serve a three-year term as chair starting Dec. 2, 2021, the FSB said. (ryan.tracy@wsj.com; @ryanjtracy)\par \par \uc2 0706 ET - The G20 summit on Friday and Saturday will set the tone for the U.S. dollar against the Chinese yuan, but also the euro, says Kit Juckes, macro strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . "The nature and tone of talks between president Xi ad president Trump in Buenos Aires may play a significant role in the mood in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the rest of 2018," he says. Expectations are that the talks won't result "in a meaningful thawing in trade relations" between the U.S. and China, says Mr. Juckes. If it does, the dollar will fall versus the yuan, he says. "If there's downside potential in USD/\uc2 CNH\uc2 , then there's less downside room in the near term for EUR/USD," he says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 27, 2018 05:12 ET (10:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020181127eebr000w2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
