\par\par \b \uc2 Helping Private Firms Imply Moral Hazard? -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2018\par 03:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2116 ET - While helping the struggling SME/private enterprises may be necessary and incrementally positive, \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  has a more skeptical view on \uc2 Securities Association of China\uc2  's plan to set up funds to ease the strain on stock-pledged loans of select listed non-state-owned companies. There is moral hazard and potential contagious impact on balance sheets of financial institutions involved, \uc2 JPM\uc2  explains, on top of the limited size of the funds, i.e.: CNY100 billion ($14.5 billion) vs. the risk relating to the CNY4.1 trillion stock pledged repo. (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2102 ET - Though it would be wise to be "cautious" over Thailand's exports outlook after last month's surprise contraction, but a further dip is unlikely in coming months, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. The bank says weak demand from its key markets of Japan, Hong Kong and the Philippines could be temporary and likely caused by typhoons hurting economic activity as well as shipments. \uc2 Citi\uc2  points to strong imports of intermediate goods and raw materials, as a gauge for the better export outlook. It also points to a \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's business survey that shows expectations of foreign orders to be above neutral and adds that "moderate growth" is likely to continue in Thai exports. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 1931 ET -- The \uc2 RBA\uc2  will not be fazed by the expected dip in Australian 3Q inflation to below target, particularly as it likely will represent less financial pressure on household budgets, says \uc2 Citi\uc2 . The \uc2 RBA\uc2  has already indicated that the outlook for the economy is little changed from its positive view and recent data, including the fall in the unemployment rate to 5%, support this assessment. However, the desirability of faster wage rises has taken on more importance given the headwind to households from the softening in the housing market. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1849 ET -- With Australia's unemployment rate at 5%, the level touted by the \uc2 RBA\uc2  as representing full employment, the question of where is the wages and inflation pick-up has moved front and center. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says the unemployment rate is consistent with full employment is actually likely to be well below the current 5.0% figure, hence wage growth and inflation are unlikely to pick up anytime soon. This fits with the experience of the U.S. where, with the unemployment rate now a percentage point below historic estimates of full employment, wages are only just starting to accelerate robustly. Inflation is set to remain benign with rates on hold through 2019, 2020, the bank says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1717 ET - The Australian and New Zealand dollars remain comfortably trading within recent ranges. The Kiwi is a tad lower at US$0.6522 and Aussie is a tad stronger at US$0.7080. Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are typically quite sensitive to risk aversion, but the recent spike in equity volatility hasn't translated into sustained downward pressure for either of these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Positioning data shows short positioning for both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are extended. That said, history would suggest that if equity volatility remains elevated then it is still reasonable to expect both to eventually succumb to this pressure, \uc2 NAB\uc2  says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1158 ET - \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  said it was hard to assess the seriousness of the risks facing the eurozone, which include Italy's plans for an expansive budget being met with disapproval by the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  . It is "not easy to distinguish" between whether these risks are "transitory or permanent," whether they are "country-specific" or relate to the eurozone as a whole, and what is impacting consumer spending and what is not. So far, any spillover from Italy to the rest of the eurozone have been "limited," he said. Mr. Draghi was speaking in a press conference following the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's meeting Thursday. Other concerns the \uc2 ECB\uc2  have include uncertainties over Brexit and global trade tensions. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1156 ET - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  downplayed the fact that "the headwinds facing the bloc are rising," says Thomas O'Mahony, portfolio manager at Janus Henderson Investors. However, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  delivered a message: while some momentum had been lost, the drivers of this have been sector-specific and do not amount to a downturn, he says. The portfolio manager adds that a range of challenges face the region, and this, combined with whispers of 'late-cycle' emanating from the U.S., means that a "vigorous reassessment" of the outlook may subsequently be required. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1116 ET - Amid all the volatility in financial markets, don't lose sight of the dollar-Swiss franc exchange rate. The dollar rose above one franc Thursday, something it's only briefly done a handful of times in the past year. This took it to its highest level in more than three months. The dollar may be gaining from higher interest rates in the U.S. versus Switzerland, which make U.S. assets more attractive. Yet the franc benefits from its traditional safe haven status in times of stress. The dollar's rise on Thursday suggests interest-rate differentials are winning out, for now. The dollar last trades up 0.3% at 1.0014 francs, having risen as high as 1.0018, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1109 ET - There are signs that the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  . "We're seeing PBC getting more active in slowing the depreciation," \uc2 RBC\uc2  CM says. September balance sheet data show that PBC sold $17.4 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the largest direct \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early 2017. The Chinese yuan fell to a record low on Thursday, sending USD/CNY up to 6.9526. The PBC bought a record of $66.3 billion on Wednesday, exceeding the previous high of $56.79 billion, when \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively immediately after the August 2015 devaluation, \uc2 RBC\uc2  CM says. USD/CNY is last flat at 6.9490. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1106 ET - A group of state bank regulators reinitiated a lawsuit against \uc2 the Office of the Comptroller of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over its plan to charter fintech companies as limited-purpose banks. The Conference of State Bank Supervisors filed a complaint in federal court in the District of Columbia, arguing the OCC does not have the authority to provide such charters, which would preempt certain state laws including interest rate limits. The group's original lawsuit was dismissed in April because the OCC had not taken any concrete steps to implement the charter yet. New York's banking regulator also reinitiated a lawsuit opposing the plan in September, after an earlier challenge was dismissed over similar reasons. The OCC announced in July that it would begin accepting fintech charter applications under its new Trump-appointed head \uc2 Joseph Otting\uc2  . (lalita.clozel@wsj.com; @laliczl)\par \par \uc2 1059 ET - If EUR/NOK were to fall below 9.40 in coming weeks, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  would recommend buying it, mainly because of the Norwegian krone's "vulnerability to external events towards year-end." But for 2019, "we still pencil in a lower EUR/NOK," says \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  . It remains short EUR/NOK, but it is "increasingly contemplating taking profit" if it falls. EUR/NOK is last down 0.4% at 9.4776 after the \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  earlier left interest rates unchanged and signalled it would raise the interest rate in the first quarter next year. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1029 ET - The bar for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shift away from the current stance is "relatively high," says \uc2 Anna Stupnytska\uc2 , global economist at \uc2 Fidelity International\uc2 , adding that this is in particular true for the asset purchase program. "If economic weakness becomes more broad-based over the next few months, they are more likely to tweak their forward guidance on interest rates, rather than extend QE beyond December 2018," she adds. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 25, 2018 21:16 ET (01:16 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020181026eeaq0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
