\par\par \b \uc2 China Cuts into US Lead on Billionaires -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2018\par 13:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1146 GMT - The U.S. is home to more dollar-based billionaires than any other country, but China has cut into its lead, according to a \uc2 UBS\uc2  and PwC report. Overall, global billionaire wealth increased by a record $1.4 trillion to $8.9 trillion in 2017, a year in which economies around the world grew at robust rates and equity markets soared. The U.S. added 22 billionaires last year, bringing its total to 585. The explosion of Chinese billionaires continued with a net gain of 55 last year to 373. In 2006, there were only 16 billionaires in the world's second-largest economy. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1138 GMT - The recent selloff in risk assets provides an attractive entry point to go tactically long in the high yield credit default swap index (CDX), which contains North American and Emerging Market companies, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  's Anindya Basu tells clients in a note. He says using options can offer superior risk-reward. He sees opportunity in selling a CDX HY31 call option expiring January 19 at a strike price of 100.5 to fund a put option at 105. While general risk sentiment remains bearish and equity indices are well off their highs, this positions for a potential year-end bounce in risk assets. "The structure has a small positive net upfront and will suffer losses only if CDX HY were to fall 4.5pts from current levels," he says.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1127 GMT - Greek economy will enter a period of slowdown from next year to 2022 at the earliest and Greek sovereign bonds will remain below investment grade during that time, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says in a note. The strict fiscal targets set by the country's creditors for the years after the bailout will affect economic growth. The country is seen reaching 2% growth this year, the highest since 2007, but then it will ease to 1.6% in 2019 and 1.3% in 2020 and 2021 before a slight acceleration to 1.4% in 2022, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  estimates. (nektaria.stamouli@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1124 GMT - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is likely to have posted a loss of 10 billion to 13 billion Swiss francs ($10 billion to $13 billion) during the third quarter, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2  . The main reasons are weaker gold prices and a stronger franc, which lowers the franc value of the \uc2 SNB\uc2 's massive foreign stock and bond holdings. Still, given the \uc2 SNB\uc2 's large reserves, the loss shouldn't affect its ability to pay its regular annual dividend to the government. As one of the few central banks with listed shares, the \uc2 SNB\uc2  also pays a tiny dividend to private shareholders. \uc2 SNB\uc2  shares trading at 5,480 francs, down 1.8% on the day. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1118 GMT - The possible cancellation of the ongoing construction of a airport in Mexico is likely to cause a revolt in the country's debt markets. Lenders financing the project could demand their bonds be immediately paid off and investors are likely to turn their back on Mexican corporates. At least $5 billion spent so far would need to written off, and creditors hold $6 billion of corporate bonds issued to finance the work, the WSJ reports. Also, it would "adversely affect sentiment and increase funding costs for Mexican corporates," says Andrey Kuznetsov, portfolio manager at \uc2 Hermes Investment Management\uc2 . Mexicans cast a vote on October 25 over whether the country should continue the construction of a new $13.3 billion airport or scrap it in favor of a cheaper alternative.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal )\par \par \uc2 1036 GMT - Italy's two-year funding cost came in its highest in more than five years at Friday's auction, reflecting investors' concerns over the country's debt sustainability but also reflecting the term premium on the new debt that was sold. The Italian treasury sold EUR3 billion in the new November 2020 CTZ, a new series of zero coupon note, at a yield of 1.626%, the \uc2 Bank of Italy\uc2  said. A month ago, the treasury still tapped the previous, March 2020-dated series at a yield of 0.715%. The higher allotment yield partly includes a term premium which reflects the eight-months' longer maturity versus the previous series. The last time Italy paid higher-than-the-current funding cost at a CTZ auction was in August 2013, at 1.871%. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1030 GMT - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be "comfortable" with markets expectations, say analysts at \uc2 BBVA\uc2  following the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's meeting on Thursday. In line with a broad consensus, \uc2 BBVA\uc2  expects the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to announce details of the reinvestment policy at the December meeting, specifically how the reinvestment framework will be instrumented. Markets are looking to see whether the \uc2 ECB\uc2  will apply the current capital key as guidance for reinvestments or whether it will tweak it. The capital key, which has changed several times since the start of the monetary union, reflects the respective eurozone country's share in the total population and gross domestic product of the EU . (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT - \uc2 J.P. Morgan Asset Management\uc2  says zero and negative real yields of developed market government bonds are a result of central banks' stimulus policies. They are "a reflection of the ongoing distortions created by global central banks," says Robert Michele, chief investment office and head of global fixed income, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodities group at the asset manager. The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  is ahead with policy normalization, being already in a rate increase cycle, while the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's first rate increase isn't expected before the end of summer 2019. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0933 GMT - The fall in the Chinese yuan to a 10-year low against the U.S. dollar overnight "will only exacerbate" the trade spat between the U.S. and China, "as the Trump administration will view the price action as an attempt to offset the costs caused by [U.S.] tariffs," says \uc2 Boris Schlossberg\uc2 , managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at BK Asset Management. USD/CNY rose to a 10-year high of 6.9646 overnight in Asia, shying away from the key 7 level. USD/CNY is last flat at 6.9437. "Given the fact that U..S goods trade deficits widened for the fourth month in a row, the Trump administration's policy on trade is clearly not working and today's action on USD/CNY exchange rates will only make markets more nervous that further retaliation will follow soon," Mr. Schlossberg says. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT - The Singapore dollar fell to a 3-week low against the US dollar late in Asia Friday, amid gains by the greenback against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The USD/SGD was at 1.3825 vs 1.3800 previous close. Intraday, the pair touched a high of 1.3846, the highest since Oct. 8. Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated that the local economy is set to slow a bit, though will stay on an "enduring" growth path in coming quarters. That is likely to cap any gains in the USD, with resistance currently seen near 1.3860. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT - Yuan rate rebounded against the U.S. dollar in late trading after a \b \highlight22\uc2 China central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy chief signalled there could be more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Beijing if the yuan weakens further. "As for those forces that have tried short selling the renminbi, we fought against each other years ago...The memory should remain fresh for all of us," says Pan Gongsheng at a briefing. The \uc2 PBoC\uc2  can't afford a free fall in yuan rate due to rising capital outflow concerns, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. The yuan regained some ground onshore against the U.S. dollar after slipping below 6.95 and is now trading around 6.94.(liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0828 GMT - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  announces its interest rate decision Friday, and is likely to keep its key rate on hold at the 7.5%, analysts say. This is justified by the fact that "inflation is in line with the year-end target, the economy isn't overheated and markets have calmed down in the last six weeks," says ING. As a result, the Russian ruble is likely to remain around same levels on Friday and into year-end, it says. The ruble is last slightly weaker against the dollar, with USD/RUB up 0.2% at 65.6735. But "the risks are that we see a stronger ruble." The \uc2 CBR\uc2 's decision in December is unclear yet. The announcement is due at 1030 GMT. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 26, 2018 07:46 ET (11:46 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020181026eeaq001hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
