\par\par \b \uc2 Global Equities Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2018\par 10:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering Equities. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT - Concerns about ethical sourcing of cobalt will present challenges to manufacturers for years as miners scramble to secure supplies of the key component in lithium-ion batteries ahead of the expected surge in the electric vehicle sales, says \uc2 Fitch Solutions\uc2 . An outcry about cobalt mined by children in conflict mines in Congo, the world's No.1 producer poses a key challenge in terms of ensuring that metals used in batteries have been sourced ethically. Complicating the risks, Congo will continue to dominate global cobalt supplies over the next decade as it accounts for the majority of pipeline projects. "As the consumer-conscious tech industry becomes an increasingly valuable customer... there will be a big push for firms to improve environmental standards and supply-chain transparency" \uc2 Fitch\uc2  says. (Nicholas.Bariyo@wsj.com;@Nicholasbariyo)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT - The reaction to \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  's third-quarter results shows the bank has lost the faith of investors, \uc2 Interactive Investor\uc2  says. The investment platform notes that \uc2 RBS\uc2  topped expectations on operating profit, but its attributable profit was behind consensus. Further disappointment comes from \uc2 RBS\uc2  's falling net-interest margin and rising expenses, \uc2 Interactive Investor\uc2  says. "Returning to the dividend list over the summer was hugely significant for \uc2 RBS\uc2  , but a modest yield cannot support a struggling share price. A lurch lower Friday morning spells further trouble for \uc2 RBS\uc2  ," \uc2 Interactive Investor\uc2  says. Shares are down 4.5% at 223.90 pence. (adam.clark@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT - Yuan rate rebounded against the U.S. dollar in late trading after a \b \highlight22\uc2 China central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy chief signalled there could be more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Beijing if the yuan weakens further. "As for those forces that have tried short selling the renminbi, we fought against each other years ago...The memory should remain fresh for all of us," says Pan Gongsheng at a briefing. The \uc2 PBoC\uc2  can't afford a free fall in yuan rate due to rising capital outflow concerns, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. The yuan regained some ground onshore against the U.S. dollar after slipping below 6.95 and is now trading around 6.94.(liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT - \uc2 Puma\uc2 's third-quarter results "successfully dismissed all bear arguments about the stock" following the company's "clumsy" second-quarter results, analysts at Berenberg say. With another outlook upgrade, Berenberg says there are no cracks in the German sporting-goods company's attractive equity story, underpinned by solid working-capital management. \uc2 Puma\uc2  shares are down 0.5% at EUR442.00. (anthony.shevlin@dowjones.com; @anthony_shevlin)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT - \uc2 Ascendas Real Estate Investment Trust\uc2  remains among the 'must have' Singapore REITs for investors, but both \uc2 DBS Bank\uc2  and \uc2 OCBC\uc2  cut their targets on the stock following softer 2Q earnings reported last evening. Distribution per unit declined 4.2% on year due higher finance costs and a enlarged unit base, and revenue was damped by lower occupancy rates in Singapore. \uc2 OCBC\uc2  cuts its fair value estimate on \uc2 Ascendas REIT\uc2  to S$2.64 from S$2.71. DBS is more bullish, with a buy recommendation and says quarterly earnings missed expectations as contributions from recent acquisitions are yet to reflect in distribution. However, it cuts its target to S$2.95 from S$3.00. \uc2 Ascendas REIT\uc2  is down 1.6% at S$2.54, taking its decline this year to nearly 7%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT - \uc2 DBS Bank\uc2  remains optimistic that orders will start flowing for \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  , the Singapore-based makers of offshore oil drilling rigs. While orders, a key indicator for earnings recovery, have been lagging behind expectations in 2018, DBS says it is partly due to the delay in decisions by oil exploration and production firms. DBS has a buy view on \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  as a "pure proxy" to the recovery in the sector. However, with orders flowing slower than expected, DBS cuts its fair value estimate on \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  's stock to S$2.40 from S$2.50. Shares are down 4.1% at S$1.65 after weak 3Q earnings reported last evening. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT - Hong Kong's Hang Seng logged a fresh 17-month low on Friday, dragged by heavyweight Tencent, finishing the week down 3.3% at 24717.63. Amid the losers, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  slid to a near 2-year low, while \uc2 China Life\uc2  tumbled 6.7% on poor 3Q18 earnings. Smartphone supply chain name \uc2 Sunny Optical\uc2  slumped another 11%, putting its 4-month losses at 63%. China Enterprises Index fared better, riding on the coat tails of its mainland counterparts, with a weekly decline of 1.6% at 10058.63. (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0833 GMT - \uc2 BP\uc2  and \uc2 Royal Dutch Shell\uc2  are in the bottom five of the Euro Stoxx 50 this week following a fall in the oil price. "The oil price has lowered from the $80s to mid $70s, obviously that's affecting sentiment," says Bernstein's \uc2 Oswald Clint\uc2 . Mr. Clint says that while both \uc2 Shell\uc2  and \uc2 BP\uc2  haven't reported their earnings for the quarter yet, results from peers have been strong. He says that the companies' strategies are good and that he "absolutely" expects share price growth for both over time. \uc2 BP\uc2  shares are down 6.3% on the week at 522.40 pence, \uc2 Shell\uc2  's A shares are down 5.9% at EUR26.72. (oliver.griffin@dowjones.com; @OliGGriffin)\par \par \uc2 0832 GMT - \uc2 Electrolux\uc2  shares sink to the bottom of the Stoxx Europe 600 Index after the company said it was being hurt from several outside factors. Raw material costs, U.S. tariffs, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, Brexit and the Sears Chapter 11 filing are all weighing. Third quarter net profit missed expectations and the company warned that raw materials, tariffs, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a bigger drag this year than previously thought. On a call after the earnings, CEO \uc2 Jonas Samuelson\uc2  said the company would further raise the selling prices of its products to mitigate the effects. He was confident that a mix of price rises and efficiency measures would offset the majority of the expected headwinds next year. Shares last trade nearly 9% lower at SEK164.95. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT - Following data that showed Malaysia's inflation was lower than expected in September, ANZ says it sees underlying inflation remaining low and stable through 1H of next year. Combined with more moderate economic activity and external factors such as trade tensions, ANZ says it expects \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the overnight policy rate unchanged at 3.25% for some time. ( yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com ; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0806 GMT - \uc2 Glencore\uc2  's third-quarter production results show continued strong growth in copper, cobalt and zinc, Bernstein analysts say. The bank says while oil-production guidance for the year was cut by 6%, the rest of \uc2 Glencore\uc2  's expected output remains unchanged. Overall, Bernstein says the strong results don't change its recommendation, which is buy, or target price, which is 500 pence. Shares down 2% at 296.95 pence. (oliver.griffin@dowjones.com; @OliGGriffin)\par \par \uc2 0806 GMT - Newly-listed boutique investment China Renaissance staged a more than 10% rebound after the previous-session's crash. That comes after it said before the bell that it plans to spend up to HK$400 million ($51 million) for stock buyback, in "a show of confidence" in the company's "long-term strategy and growth". It says the current stock price does not reflect its "intrinsic value and business prospects". The amount for stock buyback is roughly 15% of the HK$2.7 billion the financial advisor of many top Chinese tech startups raised in an IPO in September. Despite Friday's rebound to HK$15.3, shares were still down by more than 50% of its HK$31.8 IPO price. (joanne.chiu@wsj.com;@joannechiuhk)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 26, 2018 04:51 ET (08:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020181026eeaq000wl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
