\par\par \b \uc2 China's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Creeps Towards Decade-Low Against Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Eisen and Saumya Vaishampayan   Friday's Big Theme \par \uc2 1,474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2018\par 06:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell further this week, reflecting heightened investor caution about the nation's slowing economic growth and an intensifying trade conflict with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 What's Happening\par \par \uc2 The yuan hit 6.9725 per dollar in offshore trading on Friday, its weakest in nearly two years. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen against the greenback each day this week, and on Thursday sank past a prior low from August. The yuan extended declines Friday after China lowered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily trading range against the dollar for onshore trading.\par \par \uc2 After a nearly 7% selloff so far this year, the yuan is at the brink of hitting 7 per dollar--a symbolic threshold that could spark further selling--if Chinese businesses and individuals decide this means they need to expatriate capital ahead of any further decline. The yuan last traded lower than 7 per dollar in May 2008 in the onshore market, while offshore trading was only introduced in 2010.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness comes during a roller-coaster week for global markets. Chinese stocks jumped early in the week but reversed course sharply, factoring into a selloff that hit the U.S. market particularly hard on Wednesday. American benchmarks rebounded on Thursday, recouping some losses. Stocks in mainland China and Hong Kong were mixed Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 What It Means\par \par \uc2 The yuan's decline is a reminder of the diverging directions of the U.S. and Chinese economies.\par \par \uc2 China said last week that gross domestic product rose by 6.5% in the third quarter, the slowest since 2009, prompting a coordinated effort to calm investors. Current and proposed stimulus measures have fallen short for some investors.\par \par \uc2 "They are making all the right noises in terms of the stimulus packages they are putting together, but it does seem rather underwhelming at the moment in terms of the quantum of support that is being brought to bear, " said Aninda Mitra, a senior sovereign analyst in Singapore at BNY Mellon Investment Management.\par \par \uc2 The softening economy stands at odds with the U.S., where GDP growth hit a near four-year high in the second quarter, and short-term interest rates are rising. That has helped lift the dollar: the WSJ Dollar Index, which measures the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a basket of peers, has climbed for five of the past seven trading days, closing Thursday at its highest since May 2017.\par \par \uc2 A weakening yuan is likely to help Chinese exporters that price their goods in dollars but pay most costs in their home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the decline may also add to tensions between the U.S. and China. Both countries have placed billions of dollars worth of tariffs on each other's goods. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has repeatedly asserted that China is artificially holding down its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, there are signs that Beijing is acting to slow the decline in the yuan. On Wednesday, onshore dollar-yuan trading hit a record high of $66.3 billion, topping a previous record from August 2015, according to \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  . Investors often watch volume as a gauge of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though some traders said the higher volumes this time weren't necessarily symptomatic of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  , the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold $17.4 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, its biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early 2017, according to \uc2 RBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In August, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  made it more expensive for traders to place bets against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and tweaked the mechanism for setting the yuan's daily official value to prevent it falling too sharply.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ben Eisen at ben.eisen@wsj.com and Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 26 Oct 2018 08:02 ET  China Puts Yuan Skeptics on Notice as \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Nears Decade Low -- Update\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Saumya Vaishampayan and Ben Eisen \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A top Chinese policy maker warned investors to stop betting against the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday, boosting the yuan after it had fallen to nearly its weakest in a decade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For forces that try to short renminbi, we fought hand to hand a few years ago, and we are very familiar with each other," said Pan Gongsheng, a vice governor of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  , at a briefing on Friday. "I think it's still fresh in both of our memories."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a nearly 7% selloff this year, the yuan is at the brink of hitting 7 per dollar--a closely watched threshold that could trigger further selling if Chinese businesses and individuals decide that means they need to expatriate capital before any further decline. The yuan last traded weaker than 7 per dollar in May 2008 in the onshore market, while offshore trading was only introduced in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The comments from Mr. Pan, who is also the head of the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator, were the latest sign that Beijing is growing concerned about its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again, more than three years after a previous bout of depreciation sparked global market havoc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has made it more expensive this year for traders to place bets against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and tweaked the mechanism for setting its official daily trading range--efforts aimed at reining in the yuan's tumble.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the tightly controlled onshore market, the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell as low as 6.9647 per dollar on Friday--0.03% away from its lowest level in a decade. It later recovered from its 0.2% drop to end roughly flat after Mr. Pan's news conference, in which he also said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wouldn't engage in competitive depreciation and that it would continue to take steps to stabilize expectations for the yuan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Seven is an important level for them," said Eric Liu, a fixed-income portfolio manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Manulife Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 , pointing to the timing of Mr. Pan's remarks, the same day the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  neared its weakest since 2008. Mr. Liu doesn't expect the yuan to breach that level this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar has rallied in 2018 against many \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  including the yuan. The WSJ Dollar Index, which measures the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against a basket of peers, has climbed for five of the past seven trading days, closing Thursday at its highest since May 2017.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, China is dealing with other issues that have further fueled its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's drop: chiefly, trade tensions with Washington and a expected slowdown in growth. As a result, the yuan's declines against the dollar have overshadowed those of many of its peers, helping send its value against a basket of its trading partners' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to a record low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness came amid stock-market volatility this week that started in China and quickly went global. The Shanghai Composite jumped 4.1% on Monday and slid 2.3% on Tuesday, then seesawed through the rest of the week. Major U.S. indexes swung wildly on Wednesday and Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan's decline against the dollar is symptomatic of the diverging directions of each country's economy and monetary policy. China said last week that gross domestic product rose by 6.5% in the third quarter, the slowest since 2009, prompting a coordinated effort to calm investors. In the U.S., GDP growth hit a near four-year high in the second quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. has lifted interest rates three times this year and penciled in a fourth. China has moved to ease policy, helping hold down bond yields, and unveiled various stimulus measures, although those have fallen short for some investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They are making all the right noises in terms of the stimulus packages they are putting together, but it does seem rather underwhelming at the moment in terms of the quantum of support that is being brought to bear, " said Aninda Mitra, a senior sovereign analyst in Singapore at BNY Mellon Investment Management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A weakening yuan is likely to help Chinese exporters that price their goods in dollars but pay most costs in their home \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . However, the decline may add to tensions between the U.S. and China. Both countries have placed billions of dollars worth of tariffs on each other's goods. President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  has repeatedly asserted that China is artificially holding down its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As well as tweaking its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime and sounding verbal warnings, there are signs Beijing has acted to slow the yuan's decline more directly. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold $17.4 billion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last month, which \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  said amounted to its biggest such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since early 2017.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Grace Zhu contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com and Ben Eisen at ben.eisen@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 26, 2018 08:02 ET (12:02 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020181026eeaq00093\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
