\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks End Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 2,188 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2018\par 23:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MARKETS AT A GLANCE \par (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET) \par  \par                         LAST     CHANGE   % CHG \par DJIA                 24984.55    401.13   1.63% \par Nasdaq                 7318.34    209.94   2.95% \par S&P 500               2705.57     49.47   1.86% \par Japan: Nikkei 225    21268.73   -822.45  -3.72% \par Hang Seng            24994.46   -255.32  -1.01% \par Shanghai Composite    2603.80      0.50   0.02% \par S&P BSE  Sensex       33690.09   -343.87  -1.01% \par Australia: S&P/ASX    5664.10   -164.90  -2.83% \par UK: FTSE 100          7004.10     41.12   0.59% \par  \par                  PRICE CHG  YIELD \par U.S. 2 Year          0/32   2.847 \par U.S. 5 Year          2/32   2.958 \par U.S. 10 Year        -4/32   3.118 \par Australia 10 Year   13/32   2.618 \par China 10 Year        8/32   3.561 \par India 10 Year        1/32   7.865 \par Japan 10 Year        6/32   0.114 \par German 10 Year      -1/32   0.401 \par  \par                        LAST(MID)  CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)    0.7079   0.0000 \par Yen (USD/JPY)            112.42     0.01 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW) 1137.45     0.07 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)   6.9489   0.0000 \par Euro (EUR/USD)           1.1376  -0.0001 \par WSJ Dollar Index          90.48    -0.00 \par  \par                LAST   CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil      66.98    0.16  0.24% \par Brent Crude    76.65    0.48  0.63% \par Gold          1234.6     3. 5 0.28% \par  \par SNAPSHOT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rebounded from a bruising Wednesday in part on favorable corporate earnings. \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds ended lower on the rebound in equities. The dollar extended recent gains. Crude oil was higher on a return to a risk-on sentiment. And gold settled modestly higher. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OPENING CALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There are signs that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "We're seeing PBC getting more active in slowing the depreciation," \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  CM says. September balance sheet data show that PBC sold $17.4 billion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the largest direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since early 2017. The Chinese yuan fell to a record low on Thursday, sending USD/CNY up to 6.9526. The PBC bought a record of $66.3 billion on Wednesday, exceeding the previous high of $56.79 billion, when \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively immediately after the August 2015 devaluation, \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  CM says. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rebounded after a bruising start to the week, pushing the S&P 500 and Dow Jones Industrial Average back into positive territory for the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow industrials added 401 points, or 1.6%, to 24984, after tumbling more than 600 points Wednesday. The S&P 500 rose 1.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nasdaq Composite added 2.95%, following the worst day for U.S. tech stocks since 2011. The technology-heavy index, which is on track for its best day since August 2015, is also on pace for its sixth daily move of at least 2% this month, the most since January 2016.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A handful of upbeat corporate reports helped calm investors' nerves, market participants said, with shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tesla\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ford\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Visa\f28 \uc2 \f2  climbing after releasing stronger-than-expected results. The day seemed more like a relief rally following Wednesday's selloff, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's been a wild ride this week," said Paul Brigandi, managing director and head of trading at Direxion. "It's positive that the market bounced back today, but we need to see some follow-through."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bounceback was led by the consumer-discretionary, technology and communications sectors in the S&P 500, which each climbing more than 3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier in the day, a wave of selling slammed stocks in Asia after a steep selloff in U.S. technology companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei 225 dropped 3.7% and South Korea's Kospi retreated 1.6%, sending it into bear-market territory.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors have been grappling with a range of uncertainties this month that have unsettled markets, including rising interest rates, concerns that earnings might have peaked and the possibility that rising trade tensions have weakened Chinese and global economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A number of [corporate] warnings have come out that haven't been great," said Stewart Cook, head of London sales trading at Berenberg. "For the last week, I've felt more nervous and cautious than I have in the past few years."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tech companies in Asia mostly followed their U.S. peers lower, triggering steep moves across the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the electronics and entertainment group, fell more than 5%. In South Korea, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares slumped 3.6% as the country's biggest company by market value has been hit by worries over memory chips and smartphone sales. Shares of Chinese smartphone maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xiaomi\f28 \uc2 \f2  ended the day 3.4% higher, however, after the company unveiled its newest device.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei fell 3.7% at 21268.73 -- the lowest level since March 29. The percentage drop was the fourth biggest in 2018. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar extended a recent stretch of gains, on track to finish at its highest level all year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WSJ dollar Index, which measures the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against a basket of 16 others, rose 0.1% Thursday to 90.51, which would be its highest close since 90.54 in May 2017.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar has climbed this week as investors have sought relatively safe assets during turmoil in global equity markets. The Nasdaq Composite slipped into correction territory and major indexes erased their gains from 2018 on Wednesday, sending investors into assets which traditionally have less risk like Treasurys and gold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks roared back after days of declines.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback's strength this week has come even as President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  has criticized Federal Reserve Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jerome Powell\f28 \uc2 \f2  for raising interest rates too quickly. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond prices edged lower as stocks rebounded from the previous day's sharp declines.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note settled at 3.136%, compared with 3.122% Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields, which rise when bond prices fall, have largely been tracking stocks in recent sessions, falling on days like Wednesday when investors flee riskier assets for the relative safety of Treasurys and climbing when stocks retrace some of their losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Again, we're following stocks," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mary Ann Hurley\f28 \uc2 \f2  , vice president of fixed-income trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 D.A. Davidson & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "As stocks go, bonds will go to the inverse."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite generally strong U.S. economic data, U.S. stocks have had a dismal October, reflecting concerns about everything from higher interest rates tosigns that technology earnings might be peaking.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before October's stock market swoon, Treasury yields had been rising on optimism about the U.S. economy and growing confidence that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will stick to current plan of steady interest-rate increases. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMMODITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures ended higher as a rebound in equity markets signaled a momentary return to a risk-on sentiment, giving U.S. benchmark prices an incentive to move up for a second session in a row.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Energy Information Administration reported Wednesday that U.S. oil stockpiles rose for a fifth straight week, up 6.3 million barrels in the week ended Oct. 19. The data, however, also revealed a drop of 4.8 million barrels in gasoline stockpiles-over three times more than expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the hefty rise in domestic crude stockpiles, a lot of attention Wednesday was on the drop in the equities market as well as concerns surrounding global oil demand, said Robbie Fraser, commodity analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Schneider Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 West Texas Intermediate crude for December delivery on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 51 cents, or 0.8%, to settle at $67.33 a barrel, following a gain of 0.6% a day earlier. Month to date, prices based on the front-month contracts were down more than 8%, which would be the largest monthly loss since July 2016, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 FactSet\f28 \uc2 \f2  data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December Brent crude added 72 cents, or nearly 1%, to $76.89 a barrel after a loss of nearly 0.4% Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, gold futures settled with a modest gain, finding support in the wake of sizable losses a day earlier as data showed that orders for core capital goods in the U.S. fell in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The U.S. core durable number was soft and this took the shine of the durable good order data," said Naeem Aslam, chief market analyst at Think Markets UK. "Hence, there is still a strong demand for gold and it is holding on to gains."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Orders for durable goods increased 0.8% in September, the third gain in the last four months, the government said. Orders for core capital goods, closely watched by economists, fell 0.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors need to understand that the economic data over in the U.S. doesn't have the firepower anymore," said Aslam. "The tax cuts should have produced [a] more favourable number, but the data released today shows that we are not even close to that."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December gold rose $1.30, or 0.1%, to settle at $1,232.40 an ounce, recouping a portion of the 0.5% it lost in the previous session. The yellow metal had settled Tuesday at $1,236.80 - the highest for a most-active contract since mid-July, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 FactSet\f28 \uc2 \f2  data. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S HEADLINES \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Amazon\f28 \uc2 \f2   Reports Another Profit but Sales Underwhelm \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amazon.com\f28 \uc2 \f2  continued its streak of profitability, topping $1 billion for the fourth straight quarter, but reported lackluster sales growth. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  Parent \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alphabet\f28 \uc2 \f2  Delivers Surging Profit but Sales Slow \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  's parent reported surging profit on slightly slower growth in revenue, signaling uncertainty in its core business at a time when it is also dealing with a growing backlash from regulators. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Snap Loses Users but Wrings More Money From Those Who Stayed \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Snap Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  . on Thursday said revenue rose 43% in the third quarter, but its number of users declined as the company recovers from a poorly received redesign. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mattel\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sales Fall Despite Strong Showing From Barbie \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mattel reported another drop in quarterly revenue, as strong gains in Barbie sales failed to offset weakness across the rest of the toy company's portfolio and the loss of business from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toys "R" Us\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Whirlpool\f28 \uc2 \f2  Raises Prices to Offset Costs \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 25 Oct 2018 17:32 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks End Higher -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Whirlpool\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it is raising prices again to stay ahead of material costs, as the appliance maker benefits from one set of tariffs and gets hit by another. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trucking Companies Boost Prices Amid Capacity Squeeze \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trucking companies are flexing their pricing power, pushing retailers and manufacturers to pay more to buy into a strong U.S. economy and adding to inflation pressures in supply chains. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  Boosts Revenue, Swings to Profit \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  's shares soared more than 15% after the social-media company said it was doing a better job of extracting more revenue from people using the service, even as its number of engaged users shrank again in the third quarter as it continued to purge fake accounts. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China Cuts Iran Oil Purchases Ahead of U.S. Sanctions \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China is cutting some of its oil trade with Iran after vowing for months to resist U.S. sanctions on the exports, providing Washington with an unexpected boost to its efforts to isolate the Islamic Republic. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Won't Resume Trade Talks Without Firm Chinese Proposal \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. is refusing to resume trade negotiations with China until Beijing comes up with a concrete proposal to address U.S. complaints about forced technology transfers and other economic issues. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fed Vice Chairman: Strong Growth Supports Continued Rate Rises \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Vice Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Richard Clarida\f28 \uc2 \f2  endorsed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's plans to gradually raise interest rates and highlighted reasons why stronger economic growth might not lead to an inflation upturn that requires more aggressive rate increases. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES \par Trump's DEA Chief Vetted Candidates and Then Took the Job Himself, Riling Police Groups \par U.S. Hesitates Over Scope of Finance Sanctions on Iran \par Activist Investor Wants Liberty Property to Consider Selling Itself \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 BlackRock\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Next U.S. Hub Will Be Far From Wall Street \par U.S. Won't Resume Trade Talks Without Firm Proposal From a Wary China \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par (Times in GMT, followed by country and event) \par Friday, Oct. 26, 2018 \par 0200  Singapore    Q3         Employment \par 0200  Singapore    Q3         Unemployment Rate \par 0300  South Korea  September  Net barter terms of trade \par 0400  Malaysia     September  CPI \par 0500  Singapore    September  Industrial Production Index \par 0600  Germany      November   \f28 \uc2 \f2 GfK\f28 \uc2 \f2  consumer climate survey \par 0645  France       October    Consumer confidence survey \par 0645  France       September  PPI \par 0730  Thailand                Weekly International Reserves \par 0800  Taiwan       September  Business Indicat